WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:13.429 --> 00:00:16.760
Tim Ferriss: Hello, boys and girls, ladies
and germs.

00:00:16.760 --> 00:00:17.840
This is Tim Ferriss.

00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:20.540
Welcome to another episode of The Tim Ferriss
Show.

00:00:20.540 --> 00:00:23.269
My guest today is Todd McFarlane.

00:00:23.269 --> 00:00:26.919
Todd is an Emmy- and Grammy-winning director,
producer, and creator of one of the world's

00:00:26.919 --> 00:00:28.890
best-selling comic books, Spawn.

00:00:28.890 --> 00:00:33.350
He is best known to many comic book fans for
his work as the artist on The Amazing Spider-Man,

00:00:33.350 --> 00:00:36.790
for which he co-created Marvel's top villain,
Venom.

00:00:36.790 --> 00:00:41.720
Todd is the CEO of Todd McFarlane Productions,
McFarlane Toys, one of the US's top action

00:00:41.720 --> 00:00:44.530
figure manufacturers, and McFarlane Films.

00:00:44.530 --> 00:00:50.051
He is also a co-founder of Image Comics, which
debuted Spawn in 1992, selling 1.7 million

00:00:50.051 --> 00:00:52.429
copies of the first issue.

00:00:52.429 --> 00:00:58.130
In 1997, Spawn was made into an Emmy award-winning
animated series on HBO and a live-action feature

00:00:58.130 --> 00:01:01.329
film that grossed more than $100 million.

00:01:01.329 --> 00:01:06.490
In 2019, Todd made history with Spawn #301,
earning the Guinness World Record for the

00:01:06.490 --> 00:01:10.340
longest-running creator-owned superhero comic
book series.

00:01:10.340 --> 00:01:18.390
You can find him online, Instagram @toddmcfarlane,
Twitter @Todd_McFarlane, and Facebook, liketoddmcfarlane.

00:01:18.390 --> 00:01:20.590
Todd, it is nice to see you again.

00:01:20.590 --> 00:01:22.439
Todd McFarlane: Yeah, thanks for having me
back, Tim.

00:01:22.439 --> 00:01:23.439
Appreciate it.

00:01:23.439 --> 00:01:27.950
Tim Ferriss: And I wanted to close the Marvel
chapter, and I'll just explain for folks who

00:01:27.950 --> 00:01:29.479
are hearing this episode first.

00:01:29.479 --> 00:01:34.700
We recorded a first episode, which laid a
lot of the groundwork, your background, established

00:01:34.700 --> 00:01:39.009
your personality, which I'm sure will come
through in this episode as well, and early

00:01:39.009 --> 00:01:46.509
days in comics, many of the key decisions,
negotiations, your magical capacity, with

00:01:46.509 --> 00:01:52.600
your camel bladder, to inflict negotiating
superiority on your adversaries, and many

00:01:52.600 --> 00:01:54.240
other things.

00:01:54.240 --> 00:02:00.509
And I thought we would put a pin in the Marvel
chapter by talking about Stan Lee.

00:02:00.509 --> 00:02:06.340
And perhaps you could just explain who Stan
Lee is and how you first developed a relationship

00:02:06.340 --> 00:02:07.340
with him.

00:02:07.340 --> 00:02:11.690
Todd McFarlane: Probably most people who've
been paying attention to movies already know

00:02:11.690 --> 00:02:20.050
who Stan is, but Stan is sort of the grandfather
of being one of the co-creators of all these

00:02:20.050 --> 00:02:23.230
superhero characters that we all know by name.

00:02:23.230 --> 00:02:24.230
Right?

00:02:24.230 --> 00:02:33.010
So if we go back, when Marvel first began
in the early '60s, he was a writer, and he

00:02:33.010 --> 00:02:41.510
helped create characters like The Fantastic
Four, Spider-Man, Iron Man, The Hulk, Doctor

00:02:41.510 --> 00:02:51.540
Strange, Black Panther, and the X-Men, and
on and on and on and on.

00:02:51.540 --> 00:02:57.900
To put it in perspective really, when he passed
away a few years back, to me — and I think

00:02:57.900 --> 00:03:02.230
a lot of people sort of said the same thing
— you put him in the same category as somebody

00:03:02.230 --> 00:03:08.540
like Walt Disney, in terms of the global impact
that his characters had.

00:03:08.540 --> 00:03:14.959
Not necessarily him as a person, but that
his characters had.

00:03:14.959 --> 00:03:20.180
Stan was a comic book guy for a lot of years
and then eventually moved out to California

00:03:20.180 --> 00:03:29.910
in the '70s and, odd as it is, really sort
of became a big-time celebrity when the movies

00:03:29.910 --> 00:03:30.910
came around.

00:03:30.910 --> 00:03:35.430
And he started making cameos in the Marvel
movies.

00:03:35.430 --> 00:03:37.769
And he started that when he was 85.

00:03:37.769 --> 00:03:41.120
So most people don't begin a new career when
they're 85.

00:03:41.120 --> 00:03:43.519
Stan did, and he did it.

00:03:43.519 --> 00:03:52.689
So my quick sort of meeting with him, first
time I meet him, I'm about 16, 17 years old,

00:03:52.689 --> 00:03:57.090
a Canadian kid in Florida, going to a baseball
camp.

00:03:57.090 --> 00:04:02.840
And I happened to stay overnight, before we're
going to catch a plane, go back home, in a

00:04:02.840 --> 00:04:04.209
Holiday Inn.

00:04:04.209 --> 00:04:09.739
And there was a sign that said down the hall
there was a comic book convention.

00:04:09.739 --> 00:04:15.370
I go down the hall, and Stan Lee — again,
he's not the famous celebrity at this point.

00:04:15.370 --> 00:04:18.120
We're talking now about the mid '70s.

00:04:18.120 --> 00:04:19.789
And Stan Lee was there.

00:04:19.789 --> 00:04:26.229
And to me, he was like The Pope because anybody
who's ever read any Marvel comic books, at

00:04:26.229 --> 00:04:31.290
the top of every Marvel comic books at that
time said, "Stan Lee Presents."

00:04:31.290 --> 00:04:34.690
He was in every book, "Stan Lee Presents,"
every comic book I got.

00:04:34.690 --> 00:04:39.880
So I'm like, "Wow, there's Stan Lee, the guy
who presents every Marvel comic book."

00:04:39.880 --> 00:04:41.450
And I walked up to him.

00:04:41.450 --> 00:04:43.500
I wanted to break into comics.

00:04:43.500 --> 00:04:47.470
And I asked him, "Hey, Mr. Lee, is it okay
if I ask you a couple questions?"

00:04:47.470 --> 00:04:52.250
And without hesitation, he pulled a chair
right next to him, and he said, "Sit down,

00:04:52.250 --> 00:04:53.250
son.

00:04:53.250 --> 00:04:54.250
Ask me what you want."

00:04:54.250 --> 00:04:58.400
And he let me sit next to him for about five
hours, peppering him with questions.

00:04:58.400 --> 00:05:02.680
Now, again, he didn't have a big, giant lineup
because like I said, he hadn't hit his celebrity

00:05:02.680 --> 00:05:06.060
status that would come decades later.

00:05:06.060 --> 00:05:09.790
But just that he just said, "Here, sit next
to me.

00:05:09.790 --> 00:05:10.790
You got questions?"

00:05:10.790 --> 00:05:16.030
And so when I went home to Canada, that was
one of the moments where I went, "Yeah, I'm

00:05:16.030 --> 00:05:19.390
going to really drive and do this."

00:05:19.390 --> 00:05:24.190
My next encounter with him, now, fast forward,
I actually do break into comics.

00:05:24.190 --> 00:05:28.819
I climbed the rank-and-file that you and I
talked about in our first interview.

00:05:28.819 --> 00:05:34.580
I even get to do Spider-Man, which is sort
of the corporate icon of Marvel Comics.

00:05:34.580 --> 00:05:39.460
That puts me on the map, and I set sales records
with those books.

00:05:39.460 --> 00:05:43.889
And then a bunch of us quit, and we start
Image Comics.

00:05:43.889 --> 00:06:01.250
And I end up doing a series of videos with
Stan in 1992, '93 and sort of rekindling 

00:06:01.250 --> 00:06:02.540
a professional relationship.

00:06:02.540 --> 00:06:07.020
Tim Ferriss: Todd, I'm sorry to interrupt.

00:06:07.020 --> 00:06:08.740
What were those videos that you did with Stan?

00:06:08.740 --> 00:06:14.280
What was the content or the intention of those
videos that you did with Stan around '92,

00:06:14.280 --> 00:06:15.280
'93?

00:06:15.280 --> 00:06:16.320
Todd McFarlane: Most of them are up on YouTube.

00:06:16.320 --> 00:06:23.120
They were just Stan and I sitting there, and
he did it with a couple of my other, fellow

00:06:23.120 --> 00:06:24.120
artists.

00:06:24.120 --> 00:06:28.060
And you just sat at a table, and you talked
art, or you drew, and you were talking through

00:06:28.060 --> 00:06:30.229
your drawing like, "How do you create characters?"

00:06:30.229 --> 00:06:31.229
Tim Ferriss: Cool.

00:06:31.229 --> 00:06:32.690
Todd McFarlane: "Why does that guy look like
that?"

00:06:32.690 --> 00:06:33.690
Right?

00:06:33.690 --> 00:06:39.150
And it was just sort of almost a how-do-you-do-it
sort of videos that were there.

00:06:39.150 --> 00:06:46.569
Tim Ferriss: Did he not view that as competitive,
since you guys had split off to be Image at

00:06:46.569 --> 00:06:47.569
that point?

00:06:47.569 --> 00:06:52.040
Todd McFarlane: No, because Stan was very
entrepreneurial himself.

00:06:52.040 --> 00:07:01.060
He was almost an ambassador for Marvel at
this point and doing his own stuff.

00:07:01.060 --> 00:07:07.960
So the interviews and the videotapes were
sort of under an umbrella that was part of

00:07:07.960 --> 00:07:09.520
his own camp.

00:07:09.520 --> 00:07:11.710
Tim Ferriss: Ah, I see.

00:07:11.710 --> 00:07:12.710
Got it.

00:07:12.710 --> 00:07:16.910
Todd McFarlane: I think they were giving him
a salary to be a good steward, going around,

00:07:16.910 --> 00:07:18.750
but he wasn't writing any books at that point.

00:07:18.750 --> 00:07:19.780
Tim Ferriss: Got it.

00:07:19.780 --> 00:07:22.610
Yeah, he was like the professor emeritus of
Marvel.

00:07:22.610 --> 00:07:25.620
Todd McFarlane: That's exactly what he was.

00:07:25.620 --> 00:07:31.180
And let me also say, he was very, very generous
in terms of encouraging us, even though he

00:07:31.180 --> 00:07:35.350
knew that our books were going to be in direct
competition with Marvel.

00:07:35.350 --> 00:07:37.560
Because again, he was a creative guy.

00:07:37.560 --> 00:07:48.650
And having been around Stan as much as I have
been, his superpower to me, besides he created

00:07:48.650 --> 00:07:52.340
all these characters that none of us will
ever come close to — I mean, I've got Spawn.

00:07:52.340 --> 00:07:54.449
He's got like a hundred Spawns.

00:07:54.449 --> 00:08:01.789
And I saw it over and over and over, in all
the times I was with him, within 20 seconds.

00:08:01.789 --> 00:08:05.069
When you have a big lineup, and people are
coming for autographs, you literally have

00:08:05.069 --> 00:08:07.150
20 seconds with each individual.

00:08:07.150 --> 00:08:13.879
And he would make people feel important, special,
and unique in that 20 seconds.

00:08:13.879 --> 00:08:17.169
And then he would reset for the next person.

00:08:17.169 --> 00:08:21.180
And he would do it all over again for thousands
of people.

00:08:21.180 --> 00:08:22.180
Right?

00:08:22.180 --> 00:08:28.699
They felt like that was the best 20 seconds
of Stan's day, even though he was giving it

00:08:28.699 --> 00:08:32.909
to every single person.

00:08:32.909 --> 00:08:35.849
And I saw this when I was 16, when I was sitting
with him for the first time.

00:08:35.849 --> 00:08:41.789
But I just kept becoming more and more aware
of this is how you treat the public because

00:08:41.789 --> 00:08:47.220
the public, without them, without the fans,
we have no careers.

00:08:47.220 --> 00:08:50.940
We are basically self-indulging in our basements.

00:08:50.940 --> 00:08:51.940
Right?

00:08:51.940 --> 00:08:57.670
You can have ideas, but if nobody is consuming
them — I go to shows, and people go, "Todd,

00:08:57.670 --> 00:08:58.920
why do you say thank you to me?

00:08:58.920 --> 00:08:59.960
We should be thanking you."

00:08:59.960 --> 00:09:03.300
It's like, "You give me money.

00:09:03.300 --> 00:09:05.460
Every product you buy, you give me money.

00:09:05.460 --> 00:09:09.410
You put food on my family's table.

00:09:09.410 --> 00:09:11.910
If you get something out of me, cool.

00:09:11.910 --> 00:09:15.760
But I must thank you, too, because I have
a career because of your existence."

00:09:15.760 --> 00:09:21.730
Which is why it's odd, Tim, that I've run
into enough celebrities, whether it's sports,

00:09:21.730 --> 00:09:27.510
music, movies, TV, whatever it is, and some
are a little off-put by their fans.

00:09:27.510 --> 00:09:28.510
Tim Ferriss: Mm-hmm.

00:09:28.510 --> 00:09:36.589
Todd McFarlane: They are the reason we exist.

00:09:36.589 --> 00:09:41.079
And part of it was looking at people like
Stan Lee, I've never been put off by people

00:09:41.079 --> 00:09:43.730
wanting to ask questions or whatever else.

00:09:43.730 --> 00:09:49.630
And I've had reporters ask me, especially
during the whole before comic books were sort

00:09:49.630 --> 00:09:53.310
of a cool, hip thing, "Todd, what's the dumbest
question people ask?

00:09:53.310 --> 00:09:56.600
Is it like Trekkie conventions?"

00:09:56.600 --> 00:09:57.600
Here's the answer.

00:09:57.600 --> 00:09:59.070
"There is no dumb question.

00:09:59.070 --> 00:10:00.070
You know why?

00:10:00.070 --> 00:10:03.250
Because whoever is asking it, it's important
to them.

00:10:03.250 --> 00:10:07.290
And if it's important to them, it's important
for me to answer it.

00:10:07.290 --> 00:10:11.840
So even if the question may seem silly to
you — because they want to know who would

00:10:11.840 --> 00:10:15.120
win, The Hulk or Thor, in a fight — it's
important to that individual.

00:10:15.120 --> 00:10:18.740
So I'm going to give them as good an answer
as I can, and I'm not going to look down my

00:10:18.740 --> 00:10:20.940
nose at them like you are."

00:10:20.940 --> 00:10:31.630
Anyway Stan is involved in a cameo when the
first Iron Man movie comes out, and the studio,

00:10:31.630 --> 00:10:37.470
Paramount, says, "Hey, we're going to give
Stan his own movie premiere."

00:10:37.470 --> 00:10:39.269
And they did it in Vegas.

00:10:39.269 --> 00:10:44.399
Somebody phones me up and says, "Hey, Todd,
can you come and be the MC of that thing?

00:10:44.399 --> 00:10:46.630
There's going to be a dinner" or whatever
else.

00:10:46.630 --> 00:10:48.310
And I'm like, "Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:10:48.310 --> 00:10:49.310
Sure."

00:10:49.310 --> 00:10:50.310
So I come.

00:10:50.310 --> 00:10:51.310
I get up there.

00:10:51.310 --> 00:10:55.459
I say all the gracious and true things about
Stan Lee that I believe.

00:10:55.459 --> 00:10:58.820
And everything goes cool.

00:10:58.820 --> 00:11:02.120
And at the end, Stan comes up to me and says,
"Hey, thanks.

00:11:02.120 --> 00:11:03.389
Thanks, young man.

00:11:03.389 --> 00:11:04.410
That was quite wonderful."

00:11:04.410 --> 00:11:07.400
Now, I'm like, "Oh, thanks, appreciate you
having me, Stan."

00:11:07.400 --> 00:11:12.899
The next day, and here's where it happens,
the next day is I'm walking around the Vegas

00:11:12.899 --> 00:11:16.089
hotel, and I see Stan, and he goes, "Todd.

00:11:16.089 --> 00:11:17.610
Todd, hold us up.

00:11:17.610 --> 00:11:18.610
Hold up.

00:11:18.610 --> 00:11:19.610
Hold up."

00:11:19.610 --> 00:11:22.570
And he comes running over, and he goes, "Oh,
my God.

00:11:22.570 --> 00:11:24.450
You were wonderful last night.

00:11:24.450 --> 00:11:26.160
You were amazing last night."

00:11:26.160 --> 00:11:28.980
And I went, "No, no, no, you said that.

00:11:28.980 --> 00:11:30.089
You said that to me, Stan."

00:11:30.089 --> 00:11:31.870
And he goes, "No, I didn't.

00:11:31.870 --> 00:11:33.279
I can't hear anymore.

00:11:33.279 --> 00:11:35.570
I didn't hear a damn thing you said last night.

00:11:35.570 --> 00:11:41.459
But what they do is that they record it, and
then I go to my room, and then I can actually

00:11:41.459 --> 00:11:43.700
put on the headphones, and then I heard it.

00:11:43.700 --> 00:11:45.420
It was wonderful."

00:11:45.420 --> 00:11:46.420
Right?

00:11:46.420 --> 00:11:52.579
He had just figured out how to be in public
with bad hearing and pull it off.

00:11:52.579 --> 00:12:00.519
So after he hears me, I guess being kind and
a little charismatic, he says, "Todd, I go

00:12:00.519 --> 00:12:03.209
to conventions almost every weekend.

00:12:03.209 --> 00:12:10.250
Travel with me, and come with me around the
country, and you and I, let's team up."

00:12:10.250 --> 00:12:12.440
Now, gracious offer.

00:12:12.440 --> 00:12:13.960
"Stan, I've got a day job."

00:12:13.960 --> 00:12:14.960
Right?

00:12:14.960 --> 00:12:15.960
"So I can't.

00:12:15.960 --> 00:12:17.210
I mean, I understand, Batman.

00:12:17.210 --> 00:12:22.510
You would like me to be your Robin, and it's
very, very complimentary, but I've got to

00:12:22.510 --> 00:12:23.510
do it."

00:12:23.510 --> 00:12:26.380
But I said, "When I can, I will."

00:12:26.380 --> 00:12:27.579
So I did.

00:12:27.579 --> 00:12:29.320
From that point on, I did.

00:12:29.320 --> 00:12:34.660
I've probably been up on stage with Stan more
than any human being on the planet.

00:12:34.660 --> 00:12:38.529
We had this little shtick, and part of it
was at the beginning because, literally, Tim,

00:12:38.529 --> 00:12:39.529
he could not hear.

00:12:39.529 --> 00:12:40.529
He could not hear.

00:12:40.529 --> 00:12:45.560
So what he needed was somebody who could hear
and then turn and slightly give him the question

00:12:45.560 --> 00:12:50.990
or frame it in a way that made sense for a
comic book guy, where a lot of times, moderators

00:12:50.990 --> 00:12:54.710
didn't know the comic book sort of lingo.

00:12:54.710 --> 00:13:00.220
We just sort of had this weird kabuki dance
we were doing so that he could hear.

00:13:00.220 --> 00:13:06.889
Now, it came to a crescendo one time, where
we were in San Diego, and he was up on stage.

00:13:06.889 --> 00:13:10.680
I mean, it was a big room, with probably 5,000
people in.

00:13:10.680 --> 00:13:15.149
And they put up the big giant screen so the
people at the very back can at least look

00:13:15.149 --> 00:13:16.149
at the video.

00:13:16.149 --> 00:13:17.149
Tim Ferriss: Right.

00:13:17.149 --> 00:13:18.149
Todd McFarlane: Right?

00:13:18.149 --> 00:13:19.149
The video screen.

00:13:19.149 --> 00:13:20.149
And Stan was in the middle.

00:13:20.149 --> 00:13:21.149
And I usually sit next to him, but I go, "No.

00:13:21.149 --> 00:13:22.149
Stan, this is your gig.

00:13:22.149 --> 00:13:26.720
I'm going to be over here on the podium, just
talking."

00:13:26.720 --> 00:13:28.060
We start doing our thing.

00:13:28.060 --> 00:13:29.540
Boom, boom, boom, boom, boom, boom, boom,
boom.

00:13:29.540 --> 00:13:31.810
And next thing you know, I'm talking.

00:13:31.810 --> 00:13:34.750
and I'm sort of not paying attention because
I'm looking at the audience, and then I turn,

00:13:34.750 --> 00:13:38.089
and Stan is literally six inches on my flank.

00:13:38.089 --> 00:13:42.490
And then I look up at the screen, because
the screen wasn't mobile.

00:13:42.490 --> 00:13:44.240
The camera was fixed.

00:13:44.240 --> 00:13:50.050
The stage was empty because he had scooted
so far over.

00:13:50.050 --> 00:13:55.250
So I look at him, under my breath, go, "Stan,
you're offscreen.

00:13:55.250 --> 00:13:56.370
You're not on TV.

00:13:56.370 --> 00:13:57.440
All the people in the back..."

00:13:57.440 --> 00:14:00.350
But he's going, "Todd, I can't hear a damn
thing."

00:14:00.350 --> 00:14:05.940
Let me tell you, eventually he got a hearing
aid, and all of a sudden, the heavens opened

00:14:05.940 --> 00:14:09.470
up, and he was like, "Oh, my God, Todd, I
should have done this years ago."

00:14:09.470 --> 00:14:17.399
Even once he got the hearing aid, we still
did our shtick, doing it.

00:14:17.399 --> 00:14:23.579
And there's a photo that, to me, is one of
the dearest photos that I have in my collection,

00:14:23.579 --> 00:14:25.560
outside of my family.

00:14:25.560 --> 00:14:27.590
We were in Phoenix, which is where I live.

00:14:27.590 --> 00:14:29.220
And we were doing our thing.

00:14:29.220 --> 00:14:34.730
It was a little, small convention here, and
we were up on stage.

00:14:34.730 --> 00:14:39.310
This is post his wife passing away.

00:14:39.310 --> 00:14:41.459
That mattered in his life, because, I mean,
I was there.

00:14:41.459 --> 00:14:47.630
I got to bring him up on stage when he got
his Hollywood Walk of Fame star and at the

00:14:47.630 --> 00:14:48.839
Chinese Mann Theater.

00:14:48.839 --> 00:14:49.839
Right?

00:14:49.839 --> 00:14:52.850
I was sort of like his guy to bring him up
on stage.

00:14:52.850 --> 00:14:56.389
So I was there for a couple important moments
of him.

00:14:56.389 --> 00:14:57.949
And I'd gone up on stage with him so many
times.

00:14:57.949 --> 00:14:59.339
He had so much energy.

00:14:59.339 --> 00:15:05.750
And as we walked up the stairs at that one
at Phoenix, he turned to me and said, "Todd,

00:15:05.750 --> 00:15:06.779
you do most of the talking.

00:15:06.779 --> 00:15:08.160
I don't want be here."

00:15:08.160 --> 00:15:14.390
It was the first time ever that I didn't hear
enthusiasm out of that man.

00:15:14.390 --> 00:15:15.390
Right?

00:15:15.390 --> 00:15:17.000
And I mean, he was already old.

00:15:17.000 --> 00:15:22.509
And I saw it, when he arrived, that he looked
as old as I ever saw him.

00:15:22.509 --> 00:15:25.569
Anyways, we did our interview.

00:15:25.569 --> 00:15:26.699
I always used to take these selfies.

00:15:26.699 --> 00:15:29.769
I got all these cool pictures of me and him
taking selfies because he never understood

00:15:29.769 --> 00:15:31.110
what a selfie was.

00:15:31.110 --> 00:15:35.509
So he's always, right, jammed — his face
jammed into the lens.

00:15:35.509 --> 00:15:38.660
So I got these cool, awesome photos.

00:15:38.660 --> 00:15:42.319
In the green room before we went up, he used
to take a nap because he was in his 90s.

00:15:42.319 --> 00:15:43.400
He used to pace himself.

00:15:43.400 --> 00:15:47.149
So I've got all these cool pictures of me
making faces around him while he's napping.

00:15:47.149 --> 00:15:48.149
Right?

00:15:48.149 --> 00:15:50.319
So I'm going to put a whole book out.

00:15:50.319 --> 00:15:51.889
It's going to be Nap Time With Stan.

00:15:51.889 --> 00:15:54.380
I'm going to publish someday.

00:15:54.380 --> 00:15:57.980
But he walked up there, and when he said those
words, it was a dagger in my heart.

00:15:57.980 --> 00:15:59.370
And we did our interview.

00:15:59.370 --> 00:16:01.149
We took the selfie.

00:16:01.149 --> 00:16:06.440
We walked offstage, and that was the last
time he was ever on stage.

00:16:06.440 --> 00:16:08.380
And he shortly passed away.

00:16:08.380 --> 00:16:09.380
Right?

00:16:09.380 --> 00:16:12.279
And I kicked myself because you have a couple
regrets.

00:16:12.279 --> 00:16:17.029
That was one, that I wish we knew that was
going to be the last time that he was going

00:16:17.029 --> 00:16:25.279
to be on a stage in his lifetime because we
would've sent him off with a better applause

00:16:25.279 --> 00:16:27.139
than we did that day.

00:16:27.139 --> 00:16:29.600
Anyways, like I said, I was fortunate.

00:16:29.600 --> 00:16:34.600
Even after he stopped going to shows, I was
one of the few people that was allowed to

00:16:34.600 --> 00:16:40.410
be in his house because there was infighting
amongst some fiefdoms around him, like I said,

00:16:40.410 --> 00:16:42.209
once his wife went away.

00:16:42.209 --> 00:16:49.710
But the last time I went and saw him, I go,
"Hey, Stan, how you doing, bud?"

00:16:49.710 --> 00:16:54.810
And he said, "Todd, I can't see.

00:16:54.810 --> 00:16:56.860
I can't hear.

00:16:56.860 --> 00:16:59.589
I can't go to shows.

00:16:59.589 --> 00:17:02.210
And Joanie," which is his wife, "and Joanie's
not here.

00:17:02.210 --> 00:17:04.970
I just want to be with Joanie."

00:17:04.970 --> 00:17:06.600
Right?

00:17:06.600 --> 00:17:14.400
His two things was he had the love of his
life, his wife, almost married 70 years, those

00:17:14.400 --> 00:17:17.750
two people, 70 years.

00:17:17.750 --> 00:17:21.579
And when she left, you can imagine, he lost
his soulmate.

00:17:21.579 --> 00:17:23.319
And then the only thing he had left was the
conventions.

00:17:23.319 --> 00:17:26.450
And once his hearing and his eyesight went
completely, couldn't go to that.

00:17:26.450 --> 00:17:33.580
Both the things that were sort of important
to him left, and he sort of broke down at

00:17:33.580 --> 00:17:35.289
the end.

00:17:35.289 --> 00:17:37.480
Here's the thing.

00:17:37.480 --> 00:17:43.070
We're talking about a guy who, up to the age
of 93, was doing cartwheels up on stage.

00:17:43.070 --> 00:17:44.900
We never allowed him to be old.

00:17:44.900 --> 00:17:48.000
Tim Ferriss: Do you mean literally doing cartwheels?

00:17:48.000 --> 00:17:49.130
No, you just mean energetically.

00:17:49.130 --> 00:17:50.130
Todd McFarlane: No, no.

00:17:50.130 --> 00:17:51.130
Oh, yeah.

00:17:51.130 --> 00:17:52.820
Yeah, I'm saying 93, he got up there.

00:17:52.820 --> 00:17:55.350
You throw him a question, and he just went.

00:17:55.350 --> 00:17:57.100
At 93 —
Tim Ferriss: 93.

00:17:57.100 --> 00:17:59.059
Todd McFarlane: — he was at the top —
Tim Ferriss: It's incredible.

00:17:59.059 --> 00:18:00.059
Todd McFarlane: — of his game.

00:18:00.059 --> 00:18:01.059
94.

00:18:01.059 --> 00:18:02.059
He passed away at 95.

00:18:02.059 --> 00:18:05.120
he was still at the top of his game onstage.

00:18:05.120 --> 00:18:10.809
And part of it was, he was so immortal to
us that we never acknowledged that he was

00:18:10.809 --> 00:18:11.809
in his 90s.

00:18:11.809 --> 00:18:15.320
We just thought he was like some 50-year-old
guy with a lot of energy.

00:18:15.320 --> 00:18:18.530
Eventually, time came, and he had to leave
us.

00:18:18.530 --> 00:18:27.520
Tim Ferriss: Todd, you spent so much time
with Stan, and you observed so many things,

00:18:27.520 --> 00:18:31.760
picked up on so many things, firsthand and
then as an observer.

00:18:31.760 --> 00:18:36.620
For instance, the energy that he could summon,
the focus that he could summon to be with

00:18:36.620 --> 00:18:45.380
each person for 20 seconds and then reset,
which is really a skill and a talent —

00:18:45.380 --> 00:18:46.380
Todd McFarlane: It is.

00:18:46.380 --> 00:18:48.179
Tim Ferriss: — and very taxing.

00:18:48.179 --> 00:18:50.480
So that is extremely impressive, in and of
itself.

00:18:50.480 --> 00:19:01.830
Are there any other lessons or learnings that
you took from Stan or things that still come

00:19:01.830 --> 00:19:10.350
up that lead you to sort of reminisce about
sort of the University of Stan, as it were,

00:19:10.350 --> 00:19:11.350
spending time with him?

00:19:11.350 --> 00:19:14.230
Todd McFarlane: I'll comment on two things.

00:19:14.230 --> 00:19:21.360
Number one, the Stan that was onstage was
the same man offstage.

00:19:21.360 --> 00:19:22.360
It wasn't a put-on.

00:19:22.360 --> 00:19:23.490
Right?

00:19:23.490 --> 00:19:24.490
It wasn't a put-on.

00:19:24.490 --> 00:19:25.490
I mean, I've got recording.

00:19:25.490 --> 00:19:28.460
He used to let me record him.

00:19:28.460 --> 00:19:35.740
And he'd be in the taxi and pick me up at
the airport, and the whole drive from the

00:19:35.740 --> 00:19:39.820
airport to the hotel, he'd be like, "Why is
Todd here?

00:19:39.820 --> 00:19:42.440
I don't like it when the good-looking guys
are here."

00:19:42.440 --> 00:19:48.039
And he would just do this whole thing, where
he was just jabbing me the whole drive, because

00:19:48.039 --> 00:19:51.030
we did a lot of it up on stage.

00:19:51.030 --> 00:19:53.950
So again, it wasn't put on.

00:19:53.950 --> 00:19:55.610
But I remember.

00:19:55.610 --> 00:19:56.900
And here's the last thing I'll say.

00:19:56.900 --> 00:20:05.679
I remember dozens of times of him being in
the green room as we're getting ready to go

00:20:05.679 --> 00:20:09.690
up on stage or something, and he would turn
to me, And it would just be him and I. And

00:20:09.690 --> 00:20:14.350
he would go, "Todd, isn't this wonderful?

00:20:14.350 --> 00:20:15.770
Isn't this great?

00:20:15.770 --> 00:20:18.270
I'm having a great time this weekend."

00:20:18.270 --> 00:20:22.360
He goes, "Who would've thought, guys like
you and I — we're nobodies, and look at

00:20:22.360 --> 00:20:23.360
this.

00:20:23.360 --> 00:20:24.770
We're having the best..."

00:20:24.770 --> 00:20:30.770
And so what I'm saying is that the reason
he was able to summon that energy is because

00:20:30.770 --> 00:20:35.470
he was getting as much out of the audience
as they were getting out of him.

00:20:35.470 --> 00:20:41.500
It was chicken noodle for his soul when he
went to the shows, which is why when his wife

00:20:41.500 --> 00:20:45.410
was sick, Joanie, she said, "Don't stop going
to the shows.

00:20:45.410 --> 00:20:47.480
Even after I'm gone, don't.

00:20:47.480 --> 00:20:48.610
You need it."

00:20:48.610 --> 00:20:49.610
Right?

00:20:49.610 --> 00:20:51.530
To his credit, he did.

00:20:51.530 --> 00:20:53.110
He kept going.

00:20:53.110 --> 00:20:55.030
Yeah, he liked being there.

00:20:55.030 --> 00:20:56.030
And so it wasn't a put-on.

00:20:56.030 --> 00:21:01.110
Like I said, I've been around a lot of celebrities,
and I think, in all honesty, Tim, of all the

00:21:01.110 --> 00:21:05.650
celebrities I've been around — obviously,
I haven't climbed the mountain of a lot of

00:21:05.650 --> 00:21:06.650
others.

00:21:06.650 --> 00:21:09.360
But of all the celebrities I've been around,
and all the time I spent, if I was to pick

00:21:09.360 --> 00:21:14.110
one person to write the book How to Interact
With Your Fans, by far, Stan Lee's the guy

00:21:14.110 --> 00:21:16.510
who would do the "how to do it."

00:21:16.510 --> 00:21:19.270
Tim Ferriss: Amazing.

00:21:19.270 --> 00:21:20.270
Stan.

00:21:20.270 --> 00:21:25.350
I mean, I remember, as a collector, seeing
the masthead as a little kid, right, and then

00:21:25.350 --> 00:21:26.420
even into my early teens.

00:21:26.420 --> 00:21:27.420
Todd McFarlane: The "Stan Lee Presents" masthead.

00:21:27.420 --> 00:21:28.420
Tim Ferriss: Oh, of course.

00:21:28.420 --> 00:21:29.420
Yes.

00:21:29.420 --> 00:21:30.420
Todd McFarlane: Yes.

00:21:30.420 --> 00:21:31.420
Tim Ferriss: Stan Lee.

00:21:31.420 --> 00:21:35.450
Correct me if I'm getting this wrong, but
the voice that you actually impersonated really

00:21:35.450 --> 00:21:39.900
well, for people who don't know, would come
on some of the early cartoon adaptations.

00:21:39.900 --> 00:21:40.900
Todd McFarlane: Yes.

00:21:40.900 --> 00:21:44.150
Tim Ferriss: And his voice would come on,
and I would see that every morning, eating

00:21:44.150 --> 00:21:46.070
my cereal, getting ready to go to school.

00:21:46.070 --> 00:21:55.501
So Stan Lee, for me, also had this iconic,
Pope-like importance as a little kid in a

00:21:55.501 --> 00:21:58.110
childhood experience where that doesn't really
exist.

00:21:58.110 --> 00:22:00.029
It's like, "Who is this mythic Stan Lee?"

00:22:00.029 --> 00:22:02.049
Todd McFarlane: I'll tell you another little
silly story.

00:22:02.049 --> 00:22:05.860
We're in the trailer — again, the equivalent
of green room — waiting to go up on stage.

00:22:05.860 --> 00:22:09.010
And I was sitting with Stan, and they were
talking about him doing his cameos.

00:22:09.010 --> 00:22:10.750
He'd done three or four cameos in a row.

00:22:10.750 --> 00:22:12.480
And I went, "Oh, yeah."

00:22:12.480 --> 00:22:15.570
And I don't really go to the Marvel movie,
so I don't know.

00:22:15.570 --> 00:22:17.640
The next one coming up was Thor.

00:22:17.640 --> 00:22:19.049
The commercials were running.

00:22:19.049 --> 00:22:20.169
And I kept seeing the commercial.

00:22:20.169 --> 00:22:21.730
And he had short hair.

00:22:21.730 --> 00:22:22.730
Thor had short hair.

00:22:22.730 --> 00:22:24.030
And to me, I'm like, "What?

00:22:24.030 --> 00:22:25.230
Thor's got long hair.

00:22:25.230 --> 00:22:26.230
That's weird."

00:22:26.230 --> 00:22:29.120
And so anyways, I'm talking to Stan, we're
in the trailer, and I go, "Oh, you've got

00:22:29.120 --> 00:22:30.230
to do some more cameos.

00:22:30.230 --> 00:22:31.230
Oh, yeah.

00:22:31.230 --> 00:22:32.559
And the Thor, what did you do on the Thor
one?"

00:22:32.559 --> 00:22:34.520
And he goes, "Let me think.

00:22:34.520 --> 00:22:35.799
I'm trying to remember.

00:22:35.799 --> 00:22:37.539
Oh, yeah, I think I was the barber."

00:22:37.539 --> 00:22:38.660
And I went, "What?"

00:22:38.660 --> 00:22:41.110
He goes, "Yeah, I do this scene where I'm
the barber."

00:22:41.110 --> 00:22:42.110
And I go, "So what do you do?"

00:22:42.110 --> 00:22:43.530
He goes, "I cut Thor's hair."

00:22:43.530 --> 00:22:45.120
"Hold a sec.

00:22:45.120 --> 00:22:46.250
I've been seeing this commercial.

00:22:46.250 --> 00:22:47.480
He's got short hair.

00:22:47.480 --> 00:22:48.480
You cut his hair?"

00:22:48.480 --> 00:22:49.600
And he's like, "Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:22:49.600 --> 00:22:50.970
I cut his hair.

00:22:50.970 --> 00:22:55.300
It's this weird thing where I've got this
big blade, and I've got bad eyesight, and

00:22:55.300 --> 00:22:56.929
they're pinning him down."

00:22:56.929 --> 00:23:00.560
And I'm going, "Oh, my gosh, that's the coolest
thing."

00:23:00.560 --> 00:23:06.690
And then I just went, "Oh, man, if we only
had a pair of scissors, we could reenact that."

00:23:06.690 --> 00:23:08.940
He goes, "I'll get a pair of scissors, Todd."

00:23:08.940 --> 00:23:14.070
And he actually went and found a pair of scissors,
and then he literally reenacted — I videotaped

00:23:14.070 --> 00:23:19.500
it — where I'm Thor, and they're holding
me down, and he's saying his lines, and he's

00:23:19.500 --> 00:23:22.400
like going — he's doing all of it.

00:23:22.400 --> 00:23:27.669
Like I said, we're we're about to go on stage,
but he's still just goofing around.

00:23:27.669 --> 00:23:28.679
Right?

00:23:28.679 --> 00:23:30.230
Someday I'll release it.

00:23:30.230 --> 00:23:34.980
That is arguably the best piece of video I
have from Stan, because he was a very kind,

00:23:34.980 --> 00:23:39.770
polite, gentle man in terms of his language,
if you will, when he was up on stage.

00:23:39.770 --> 00:23:44.990
And I have the one where I recorded, where
he goes, "Todd, can I tell you something?"

00:23:44.990 --> 00:23:46.190
Again, out of the blue.

00:23:46.190 --> 00:23:47.409
"Can I tell you something?"

00:23:47.409 --> 00:23:48.409
"Yeah, Stan.

00:23:48.409 --> 00:23:50.169
You can say whatever you want."

00:23:50.169 --> 00:23:57.760
"Do you know what the most flexible word is
in the English dictionary, that has the most

00:23:57.760 --> 00:23:58.830
ways you can use it?"

00:23:58.830 --> 00:24:00.830
And I went, "No, Stan."

00:24:00.830 --> 00:24:01.830
"Fuck."

00:24:01.830 --> 00:24:03.899
And I went, "What?"

00:24:03.899 --> 00:24:06.750
I don't even think I ever heard him even say
"damn" up to this point.

00:24:06.750 --> 00:24:08.110
And I'd been around him a lot.

00:24:08.110 --> 00:24:10.059
And it sort of shocked me.

00:24:10.059 --> 00:24:11.059
Right?

00:24:11.059 --> 00:24:12.100
And I went, "Pardon?"

00:24:12.100 --> 00:24:13.430
"Yeah, Todd.

00:24:13.430 --> 00:24:14.430
Fuck."

00:24:14.430 --> 00:24:20.570
And then he went on a dissertation of how
you can use that word as a noun, a pronoun,

00:24:20.570 --> 00:24:24.610
an adjective, and gave examples of all them.

00:24:24.610 --> 00:24:32.640
It was like a school teacher sort of doing
a classic masterclass in how to use a word

00:24:32.640 --> 00:24:33.640
more than once.

00:24:33.640 --> 00:24:34.890
It just happened to be that word.

00:24:34.890 --> 00:24:35.890
So maybe —
Tim Ferriss: Wow.

00:24:35.890 --> 00:24:37.390
Todd McFarlane: — someday that audio will
come out.

00:24:37.390 --> 00:24:43.960
Tim Ferriss: Well, it seems like, in maybe
the appendices of the How to Interact With

00:24:43.960 --> 00:24:51.399
Your Fans book that he would author, you would
have How to Enjoy Life and Not Complain as

00:24:51.399 --> 00:24:52.680
the supplemental materials.

00:24:52.680 --> 00:25:02.320
He seems like he was very good at enjoying
life, pausing, not getting swept up in all

00:25:02.320 --> 00:25:07.630
the superficial nonsense, but stopping you
and saying, "Isn't this great?

00:25:07.630 --> 00:25:09.179
Let's look at this for a second.

00:25:09.179 --> 00:25:10.179
Isn't this amazing?"

00:25:10.179 --> 00:25:16.740
It seems like he was able to really harness
his appreciation in a way that maybe fed him

00:25:16.740 --> 00:25:24.539
and allowed him to get to his mid-90s still
doing the metaphoric cartwheels on stage.

00:25:24.539 --> 00:25:26.169
That's something that comes across in the
stories.

00:25:26.169 --> 00:25:31.510
Todd McFarlane: I think Stan, he comes from
the school of those of us who believe our

00:25:31.510 --> 00:25:36.640
15 minutes of fame is just about over every
day we wake up.

00:25:36.640 --> 00:25:37.640
We always —
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

00:25:37.640 --> 00:25:40.429
Todd McFarlane: — think tomorrow is the
day where they're going to understand that

00:25:40.429 --> 00:25:45.730
we're imposters and that there's way more
important people that are more skilled, that

00:25:45.730 --> 00:25:47.170
have more to say.

00:25:47.170 --> 00:25:53.080
And all of a sudden you go to your next show,
and there's another group of people, and you

00:25:53.080 --> 00:25:55.220
go, "Wow, I guess it's going to last one more
day."

00:25:55.220 --> 00:25:56.220
Right?

00:25:56.220 --> 00:25:57.220
And you keep going.

00:25:57.220 --> 00:25:58.220
And you're thankful for it.

00:25:58.220 --> 00:25:59.220
Right?

00:25:59.220 --> 00:26:00.220
Tim Ferriss: Mm-hmm.

00:26:00.220 --> 00:26:05.990
Todd McFarlane: Again, at some point, once
you know that you're fortunate, that's okay.

00:26:05.990 --> 00:26:09.540
I mean at my end, I think there's a little
bit of Canadiana in it.

00:26:09.540 --> 00:26:11.120
I was born in Canada.

00:26:11.120 --> 00:26:18.710
I saw people like Steve Nash and Wayne Gretzky,
my big personal hero, sort of act like gentlemen,

00:26:18.710 --> 00:26:20.260
even though they were superstars.

00:26:20.260 --> 00:26:28.000
And I think part of it is, and I'm maybe overstating
it, but when you're from Canada, you know

00:26:28.000 --> 00:26:32.080
that the big brother is America, and that's
where all of the big action is.

00:26:32.080 --> 00:26:38.799
And if you can get any attention, and you
can even in infiltrate down there, you always

00:26:38.799 --> 00:26:42.970
are sort of looking over your flank, going,
"Eventually you're going to see that they

00:26:42.970 --> 00:26:44.230
can just pick one of their own.

00:26:44.230 --> 00:26:45.270
They don't need us."

00:26:45.270 --> 00:26:46.270
Right?

00:26:46.270 --> 00:26:49.480
And so you see that with a lot of comedians
that come from Canada and actors come from

00:26:49.480 --> 00:26:51.140
Canada.

00:26:51.140 --> 00:26:55.419
Stan sort of had a little bit of Canada, Canadian,
in him, where he was just like, "Enjoy it

00:26:55.419 --> 00:26:56.419
while you can.

00:26:56.419 --> 00:26:57.760
You don't know if it's going to be here tomorrow."

00:26:57.760 --> 00:26:59.410
Like your health.

00:26:59.410 --> 00:27:04.630
Tim Ferriss: It seems to me, or at least it
seemed to me when I was younger, and even

00:27:04.630 --> 00:27:08.900
now in some respects, once a comic guy, always
a comic guy.

00:27:08.900 --> 00:27:17.409
However, you have managed to branch out and
infiltrate all of these different areas.

00:27:17.409 --> 00:27:19.270
And I don't even know how to put them in order.

00:27:19.270 --> 00:27:20.730
So you've got McFarland Toys.

00:27:20.730 --> 00:27:21.730
Right?

00:27:21.730 --> 00:27:23.620
So let's just generically lay it out.

00:27:23.620 --> 00:27:28.180
You've got toys, music, directing, TV, film.

00:27:28.180 --> 00:27:33.730
What is the order of how you entered those
various things?

00:27:33.730 --> 00:27:39.280
So you're forging new paths, setting records
at Image.

00:27:39.280 --> 00:27:42.040
When did these things start to pop up, and
in what order?

00:27:42.040 --> 00:27:46.200
Todd McFarlane: In all honesty, Tim, they're
a bit scattered.

00:27:46.200 --> 00:27:48.700
So let's see if we can go through them real
fast.

00:27:48.700 --> 00:27:49.700
Right?

00:27:49.700 --> 00:27:56.559
Let's just talk first about how the toys come
to be, real quickly.

00:27:56.559 --> 00:27:57.559
Okay?

00:27:57.559 --> 00:27:58.580
We start Image Comics.

00:27:58.580 --> 00:28:00.110
We talked about that in the last show.

00:28:00.110 --> 00:28:04.039
Image Comics, the third largest comic book
company in North America.

00:28:04.039 --> 00:28:05.840
Marvel, DC and then Image Comics.

00:28:05.840 --> 00:28:10.090
If you ask who's three, that's us, for 30
years in a row.

00:28:10.090 --> 00:28:16.190
And because of that, when we came out in 1992,
that was when we first came out, and Spawn

00:28:16.190 --> 00:28:17.970
came out in 1992.

00:28:17.970 --> 00:28:22.550
We're celebrating the 30th anniversary of
both those this year.

00:28:22.550 --> 00:28:25.720
Spawn went to the top of the charts, right,
set sales records at top of the charts.

00:28:25.720 --> 00:28:27.520
And I'm not saying for independent comic books.

00:28:27.520 --> 00:28:29.260
I'm saying top of the charts for comic books.

00:28:29.260 --> 00:28:32.350
It was ahead of every Marvel and every DC
comic book.

00:28:32.350 --> 00:28:36.240
And so you can imagine, when you have something
at the top of a chart, then people in other

00:28:36.240 --> 00:28:39.960
industries come around, and they start knocking
on your door.

00:28:39.960 --> 00:28:43.680
And so people were coming and knocking on
my door going, "Oh, my God, Spawn is at the

00:28:43.680 --> 00:28:44.680
top of the charts.

00:28:44.680 --> 00:28:49.840
You want to do pajamas and pillowcases and
toothbrushes and all these..."

00:28:49.840 --> 00:28:52.220
what they call ancillary merchandising.

00:28:52.220 --> 00:28:54.000
And so all those phone calls were coming.

00:28:54.000 --> 00:28:57.840
Now, I gently said, "No, no, no, no, no.

00:28:57.840 --> 00:29:03.789
I understand you want to do pillowcases, but
have you read a Spawn comic book?"

00:29:03.789 --> 00:29:05.980
It was like, "Well, no, but we see it's number
one."

00:29:05.980 --> 00:29:07.340
"So, do you know he…"

00:29:07.340 --> 00:29:08.480
Tim Ferriss: Wrong answer for Todd.

00:29:08.480 --> 00:29:09.480
Todd McFarlane: Yeah.

00:29:09.480 --> 00:29:11.559
"Do you know he's from the pit of Hell?"

00:29:11.559 --> 00:29:12.559
Right?

00:29:12.559 --> 00:29:13.950
And you can just see there's a hesitation.

00:29:13.950 --> 00:29:14.950
Right?

00:29:14.950 --> 00:29:17.570
They're going — so I go, "Why don't you
take a couple of the issues.

00:29:17.570 --> 00:29:18.860
This character's from the pit of Hell.

00:29:18.860 --> 00:29:22.920
Go read a couple, and then decide whether
you want to do pillowcases for kids that are

00:29:22.920 --> 00:29:24.010
five years old."

00:29:24.010 --> 00:29:25.010
Right?

00:29:25.010 --> 00:29:29.010
Now, a bunch of them didn't follow up with
their phone calls after I sort of put their

00:29:29.010 --> 00:29:30.090
nose in the product.

00:29:30.090 --> 00:29:37.840
All they saw was that Spawn was ahead of Spider-Man
and Batman and Superman, so it must be super

00:29:37.840 --> 00:29:38.840
awesome.

00:29:38.840 --> 00:29:39.840
Right?

00:29:39.840 --> 00:29:40.840
That was it.

00:29:40.840 --> 00:29:42.039
That was all their homework was at that point.

00:29:42.039 --> 00:29:46.750
Here's what I had in the back of my mind when
we left Marvel Comic Books and we helped start

00:29:46.750 --> 00:29:47.750
Image Comics.

00:29:47.750 --> 00:29:54.970
I wanted to build a foundation because I feel
that, metaphorically, if you can build a strong

00:29:54.970 --> 00:30:00.630
foundation, just like in the real world, you
can put a skyscraper on a strong foundation.

00:30:00.630 --> 00:30:01.630
How do we know?

00:30:01.630 --> 00:30:03.380
Because they exist.

00:30:03.380 --> 00:30:04.380
Right?

00:30:04.380 --> 00:30:12.159
So the foundation to me was, could I do movies,
TV, video games, and toys?

00:30:12.159 --> 00:30:13.190
Those were the four.

00:30:13.190 --> 00:30:14.320
Those were the four corner stuff.

00:30:14.320 --> 00:30:15.320
Tim Ferriss: Could you just repeat those four?

00:30:15.320 --> 00:30:16.320
Todd McFarlane: I mean, just for me.

00:30:16.320 --> 00:30:17.320
Just for me.

00:30:17.320 --> 00:30:18.320
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, for you.

00:30:18.320 --> 00:30:19.320
Todd McFarlane: Right.

00:30:19.320 --> 00:30:20.320
My foundations were going to be video games,
TV, movies, and toys because I felt that if

00:30:20.320 --> 00:30:21.320
you could get your brand into those four corners,
then — and again, because I was playing

00:30:21.320 --> 00:30:28.279
in the comic book world — then I could branch
out into these areas and plant some corners

00:30:28.279 --> 00:30:30.680
and get people to go, "Oh."

00:30:30.680 --> 00:30:35.419
And it's worked, because I know people who've
since say, "I know Spawn from HBO," not because

00:30:35.419 --> 00:30:36.760
they've read the comic or bought the toys.

00:30:36.760 --> 00:30:38.850
It was they saw it on HBO or wherever.

00:30:38.850 --> 00:30:39.850
I don't really care.

00:30:39.850 --> 00:30:41.120
Let me just tell you, Tim.

00:30:41.120 --> 00:30:42.120
I don't really care.

00:30:42.120 --> 00:30:44.179
My attitude is building a business.

00:30:44.179 --> 00:30:45.179
You build a house.

00:30:45.179 --> 00:30:50.350
That's your business, and you put as many
doors on your house as possible, and you make

00:30:50.350 --> 00:30:53.200
sure they're all unlocked all the time.

00:30:53.200 --> 00:30:55.289
I don't care how they get into the house.

00:30:55.289 --> 00:30:57.390
I don't care how they come to the party.

00:30:57.390 --> 00:30:59.669
It's party central inside the house.

00:30:59.669 --> 00:31:00.669
Right?

00:31:00.669 --> 00:31:03.820
I don't care if they go, "Todd, man, I saw
you did that music video."

00:31:03.820 --> 00:31:04.820
I don't care.

00:31:04.820 --> 00:31:05.820
"Door's open.

00:31:05.820 --> 00:31:07.690
Come on in, come on in, come on in."

00:31:07.690 --> 00:31:09.110
I don't care how you got here.

00:31:09.110 --> 00:31:15.899
I am not discriminating on a fan, because
the bigger your army is, the collective whole,

00:31:15.899 --> 00:31:21.110
the better chance you have of then sending
out your message for the next thing that you're

00:31:21.110 --> 00:31:23.110
about to do, and it might work.

00:31:23.110 --> 00:31:26.220
So those were the four pillars.

00:31:26.220 --> 00:31:29.010
And to go through them real quick, I made
a deal with the video games.

00:31:29.010 --> 00:31:33.529
I made the deal with HBO, and we'll get back
to that, on the TV.

00:31:33.529 --> 00:31:38.730
New Line Cinema, we made the deal with the
movie.

00:31:38.730 --> 00:31:39.880
And then we got to the toys.

00:31:39.880 --> 00:31:47.789
And all the Fortune 500, the public companies,
came to my door, and they all gave their presentation.

00:31:47.789 --> 00:31:48.830
Some of them were pretty elaborate.

00:31:48.830 --> 00:31:53.620
They had prototypes and everything.

00:31:53.620 --> 00:31:57.929
At the end of it, I just go, "I don't think
they get it."

00:31:57.929 --> 00:32:04.000
What I heard was they're going to do traditional
toys and put them in traditional places and

00:32:04.000 --> 00:32:08.700
market them in traditional ways, which basically
meant they were going to put Spawn next to

00:32:08.700 --> 00:32:10.899
Teletubbies and all their Disney product.

00:32:10.899 --> 00:32:13.380
And I thought it was a recipe for failure.

00:32:13.380 --> 00:32:17.570
And oh, by the way, if I gave it to them,
and it failed, then when it didn't work, which

00:32:17.570 --> 00:32:21.919
I thought it wouldn't, they would hand me
my brand back, and it would be tainted.

00:32:21.919 --> 00:32:26.409
How do I then pick up those pieces, when people
go, "Ah, it failed last time, Todd.

00:32:26.409 --> 00:32:29.139
Why would we want now to dust it off and try
it again?"

00:32:29.139 --> 00:32:32.960
So I never felt like I could get what I wanted.

00:32:32.960 --> 00:32:36.889
And so you have to then ask the next question.

00:32:36.889 --> 00:32:38.730
How hard is it to make my own choice?

00:32:38.730 --> 00:32:39.830
That's it.

00:32:39.830 --> 00:32:40.830
Right?

00:32:40.830 --> 00:32:41.830
And sometimes it's out of anger.

00:32:41.830 --> 00:32:44.210
I've started companies just because I got
pissed off.

00:32:44.210 --> 00:32:49.919
It's because people think that what they're
doing is way, way more complicated than it

00:32:49.919 --> 00:32:50.919
is.

00:32:50.919 --> 00:32:52.919
Let me tell you something right now, Tim.

00:32:52.919 --> 00:32:55.850
We've got the Image Comics and we do our own
comic books.

00:32:55.850 --> 00:32:58.669
And you know what making comic books is at
its core?

00:32:58.669 --> 00:33:04.070
Ink on paper, period, period, period, ink
on paper.

00:33:04.070 --> 00:33:06.269
Toys are not that much different.

00:33:06.269 --> 00:33:08.910
It is plastic in a shape.

00:33:08.910 --> 00:33:12.130
And if you have the right mold, you can make
a shape.

00:33:12.130 --> 00:33:13.460
How do I know?

00:33:13.460 --> 00:33:16.779
Anybody who's kind of old that remembers this,
when we were kids, remember you used to get

00:33:16.779 --> 00:33:22.029
these little trays and they're in the shape
of dinosaurs, and then you poured the jello

00:33:22.029 --> 00:33:25.460
into it, and you put it in the fridge, and
then you flip it out, and all of a sudden

00:33:25.460 --> 00:33:27.730
your jello looked like dinosaurs.

00:33:27.730 --> 00:33:28.730
You know why?

00:33:28.730 --> 00:33:30.390
Because that was what the mold looks like.

00:33:30.390 --> 00:33:35.490
So toys are plastic in jello molds, essentially.

00:33:35.490 --> 00:33:37.870
Little bit more details I'm leaving out.

00:33:37.870 --> 00:33:39.330
But essentially, that's it.

00:33:39.330 --> 00:33:42.809
So when I asked the question, "How do you
make toys?

00:33:42.809 --> 00:33:46.750
How do you make comics?"

00:33:46.750 --> 00:33:54.789
Let me tell you, the people in that industry,
the people that are the leaders in that industry

00:33:54.789 --> 00:33:58.529
always want you to think that it's way more
complicated, always want you to think that

00:33:58.529 --> 00:34:05.190
it's basically undoable, and will never, ever
sidestep, no matter how good your idea is,

00:34:05.190 --> 00:34:06.700
to let you pass them.

00:34:06.700 --> 00:34:10.980
If anything, they will intentionally try and
trip you as you're passing them, and tell

00:34:10.980 --> 00:34:15.619
you it can't be done, can't do it, will hire
people away from it.

00:34:15.619 --> 00:34:18.630
They'll tell you everything, "Stop rocking
the boat.

00:34:18.630 --> 00:34:20.669
Why don't you just get along?"

00:34:20.669 --> 00:34:22.280
Everything that you can possibly imagine.

00:34:22.280 --> 00:34:23.280
Why?

00:34:23.280 --> 00:34:26.220
They don't want competition.

00:34:26.220 --> 00:34:30.960
So at some point you have to shut all that
stuff down and go, "I don't care if I sell

00:34:30.960 --> 00:34:32.780
five, I'm good enough."

00:34:32.780 --> 00:34:34.010
And it was my money.

00:34:34.010 --> 00:34:38.310
Again, the whole thing, spend other people's
money, I disagree one hundred percent.

00:34:38.310 --> 00:34:39.310
Spend your own money.

00:34:39.310 --> 00:34:40.310
You know why?

00:34:40.310 --> 00:34:42.460
Because nobody gets to tell you what to do.

00:34:42.460 --> 00:34:47.490
If you want to go buy a car with polka dots
on it, and it's your money, do it, and drive

00:34:47.490 --> 00:34:49.070
it with a smile on your face.

00:34:49.070 --> 00:34:52.590
And if somebody doesn't like it, tell them
to buy their own damn car.

00:34:52.590 --> 00:34:55.859
But the moment you take somebody's money,
and they get to tell you whether they like

00:34:55.859 --> 00:34:57.290
the polka dots on the car or not.

00:34:57.290 --> 00:34:59.109
And so I just go, "No."

00:34:59.109 --> 00:35:04.150
I spent my own cash when I started the companies,
and I was fortunate because the comic books

00:35:04.150 --> 00:35:05.570
were paying royalties.

00:35:05.570 --> 00:35:10.400
Let me just tell you, I wasn't getting paid
a lot per page.

00:35:10.400 --> 00:35:14.760
That's not what made me, just so everybody
knows, at the height of my career at Marvel,

00:35:14.760 --> 00:35:17.900
I was making $125 per page.

00:35:17.900 --> 00:35:19.680
That was the max they were paying me.

00:35:19.680 --> 00:35:25.200
It was the royalties, I was getting a percentage
of the sales, and that was the magic of it.

00:35:25.200 --> 00:35:31.290
It's what literally made us economically at
Image Comics because we were getting four

00:35:31.290 --> 00:35:33.270
percent of the profits.

00:35:33.270 --> 00:35:35.340
And when we went and started our own company,
guess what?

00:35:35.340 --> 00:35:37.369
We were getting 100 percent.

00:35:37.369 --> 00:35:45.440
Do the math, if you take a hundred, divide
it by four, if we were selling 1/25th the

00:35:45.440 --> 00:35:48.700
amount of books that we were doing at Marvel,
we would make the same money.

00:35:48.700 --> 00:35:49.980
And guess what, boys and girls?

00:35:49.980 --> 00:35:57.260
We sold the same, in some cases, more than
we were selling at Marvel.

00:35:57.260 --> 00:36:03.190
So we were then all of a sudden instantly
making 25 times more than we were overnight.

00:36:03.190 --> 00:36:04.610
And that's a whole 'nother conversation.

00:36:04.610 --> 00:36:09.030
Tim Ferriss: And you could use that to bankroll
the toy development, your piece of that?

00:36:09.030 --> 00:36:10.030
Todd McFarlane: Correct.

00:36:10.030 --> 00:36:12.780
I wasn't spending it, I wasn't buying fancy
cars, I wasn't doing anything.

00:36:12.780 --> 00:36:14.860
I'm kind of a tight when you get right down
to it.

00:36:14.860 --> 00:36:19.220
I was just putting it in a corner for rainy
days, or when I was going to get angry, because

00:36:19.220 --> 00:36:20.890
I knew my personality.

00:36:20.890 --> 00:36:29.070
So we started the toy company, and we came
out with the first toys.

00:36:29.070 --> 00:36:33.000
Action figures at that point were 5.99 and
I priced mine at 6.99.

00:36:33.000 --> 00:36:38.290
And here is the thing, if you're listening
to my voice, all the big companies came to

00:36:38.290 --> 00:36:45.550
me and patted me on the back and went, "Little
boy, you can't sell a 6.99 toy.

00:36:45.550 --> 00:36:47.240
They're 5.99."

00:36:47.240 --> 00:36:50.510
Here's the answer: of course you can.

00:36:50.510 --> 00:36:55.890
Here's how you sell a 6.99 toy: you give them
$6.99 of value, period.

00:36:55.890 --> 00:36:58.849
Oh, I'll even give you an upgrade.

00:36:58.849 --> 00:37:03.859
Give them 7.99 of value, and price it at 6.99.

00:37:03.859 --> 00:37:08.079
What they were thinking, because they were
the big corporate guys, how do you sell a

00:37:08.079 --> 00:37:09.920
5.99 toy for 6.99?

00:37:09.920 --> 00:37:14.520
I get that, you can't sell that because people
will understand you're overpricing it.

00:37:14.520 --> 00:37:19.980
I'm saying build a Cadillac and sell it at
Ford Motor prices, and you can sell that all

00:37:19.980 --> 00:37:23.371
day long, because people understand value
and they can do it.

00:37:23.371 --> 00:37:24.970
Because I didn't have any big brands.

00:37:24.970 --> 00:37:27.380
I didn't have Superman and Batman or Star
Wars.

00:37:27.380 --> 00:37:31.940
I had Spawn, and there was only one way I
was going to be able to compete with those

00:37:31.940 --> 00:37:38.280
big names, just give them better quality,
and give them more plastic.

00:37:38.280 --> 00:37:41.260
Again, never underestimate plastic.

00:37:41.260 --> 00:37:46.640
It's like saying, "Do you want the 15-ounce
bar of chocolate for a dollar or the 10-ounce

00:37:46.640 --> 00:37:47.990
bar of chocolate?"

00:37:47.990 --> 00:37:49.280
"I'll take the 15."

00:37:49.280 --> 00:37:50.280
What are you talking about?

00:37:50.280 --> 00:37:51.960
Always take the bigger bar.

00:37:51.960 --> 00:37:53.770
For a buck, take more chocolate.

00:37:53.770 --> 00:37:54.770
Come on, man.

00:37:54.770 --> 00:37:58.650
So I was the guy who was just giving them
more, and more, and more.

00:37:58.650 --> 00:38:05.359
Tim Ferriss: How did you learn about making
toys, and figure out the process for making

00:38:05.359 --> 00:38:07.100
a quality product?

00:38:07.100 --> 00:38:16.220
How did you educate yourself on the bits and
pieces, and steps, and right way to approach

00:38:16.220 --> 00:38:17.220
it?

00:38:17.220 --> 00:38:20.020
Todd McFarlane: I did it slowly.

00:38:20.020 --> 00:38:25.830
No, when I finally hung up my last meeting
with somebody wanting to take the rights of

00:38:25.830 --> 00:38:28.869
Spawn to make them toys, and I said, "No."

00:38:28.869 --> 00:38:34.050
I knew a couple people, and I phoned somebody
who was sort of entrepreneurial, and I said,

00:38:34.050 --> 00:38:35.750
"You know anybody that makes toys?"

00:38:35.750 --> 00:38:37.900
And they're going, "Oh, yeah, I got a couple
buddies."

00:38:37.900 --> 00:38:41.000
And we got on the phone with them, and that
was it.

00:38:41.000 --> 00:38:45.660
And they just went, "Yeah, I've been involved
in making toys," and that was it.

00:38:45.660 --> 00:38:49.579
You sculpt the toy out of clay, back then,
you can do it digitally now, but back then

00:38:49.579 --> 00:38:53.220
it was out of clay.

00:38:53.220 --> 00:38:58.680
You make a mold of it, and you send it over
to your factory, and most toys are manufactured

00:38:58.680 --> 00:39:00.970
in China, we found factories in China.

00:39:00.970 --> 00:39:06.070
And they cut a steel mold, and they go, "How
many do you want?"

00:39:06.070 --> 00:39:07.530
And you're off to the races.

00:39:07.530 --> 00:39:16.910
So again, it's more complicated than that,
but at its core, it's not really.

00:39:16.910 --> 00:39:20.369
If you've got art, you can make art in any
shape you want.

00:39:20.369 --> 00:39:26.550
And so what helped me, Tim, more than anything
else, and I've said this, I've never invented

00:39:26.550 --> 00:39:27.550
anything original.

00:39:27.550 --> 00:39:29.690
I've never done anything new.

00:39:29.690 --> 00:39:34.210
What I've done is taken everything that I've
seen with my eyes and just made it sexier

00:39:34.210 --> 00:39:35.940
and cooler, period.

00:39:35.940 --> 00:39:36.940
That's it.

00:39:36.940 --> 00:39:38.359
That's the recipe.

00:39:38.359 --> 00:39:41.980
Everybody who's out there is listening and
wants to be an entrepreneur, stop putting

00:39:41.980 --> 00:39:46.500
the pressure on yourself to think you have
to come up with something new and original.

00:39:46.500 --> 00:39:47.500
You do not.

00:39:47.500 --> 00:39:57.060
You just have to add three percent sexy to
it, and you will now be the genius.

00:39:57.060 --> 00:39:58.480
I'll give you an example.

00:39:58.480 --> 00:40:02.410
10, 12 years ago, there was a guy named Steve
Jobs, you may have heard of him.

00:40:02.410 --> 00:40:06.839
And he went up on stage one day and he said,
"Ladies and gentlemen," I remember the day,

00:40:06.839 --> 00:40:13.960
there was a bunch of people, and he goes,
"We are Apple," because they were doing iPods,

00:40:13.960 --> 00:40:15.412
and he go, "We are going to come out with
a phone."

00:40:15.412 --> 00:40:24.040
And the people in the audience went, "Steve,
little late to the game.

00:40:24.040 --> 00:40:26.300
Cell phones have been out for a while."

00:40:26.300 --> 00:40:30.360
"No, but get this, ours can dial people."

00:40:30.360 --> 00:40:31.730
"Already been done, Steve."

00:40:31.730 --> 00:40:38.750
"And we text," done, "And photos," done, "Download
stuff," done, "Email," done, done, done.

00:40:38.750 --> 00:40:40.840
There's nothing you're doing, Steve, that
hasn't been done.

00:40:40.840 --> 00:40:50.340
And then the moment he said, "Yes, but when
you text, 'Hi, Mom,' on their phone, you are

00:40:50.340 --> 00:40:53.589
touching a QWERTY with plastic buttons.

00:40:53.589 --> 00:40:59.940
And when you say, 'Hi, Mom,' on mine, you're
not touching plastic, you're touching glass."

00:40:59.940 --> 00:41:03.430
Boom, boom.

00:41:03.430 --> 00:41:11.109
It did the exact same thing as every other
phone on the marketplace, except you touched

00:41:11.109 --> 00:41:12.390
glass.

00:41:12.390 --> 00:41:13.760
That was the genius.

00:41:13.760 --> 00:41:17.720
He gave them the sexy glass instead of plastic.

00:41:17.720 --> 00:41:21.680
Still have to put H-I-M-O-M, Hi, Mom.

00:41:21.680 --> 00:41:24.810
It's still the exact same thing, he just figured
out the three percent.

00:41:24.810 --> 00:41:28.880
If you can figure out the three percent on
any idea, they'll make you a genius.

00:41:28.880 --> 00:41:31.470
So what's the genius they've given me, Tim?

00:41:31.470 --> 00:41:37.370
"Oh, my gosh, Todd, we've done sports figures
for decades.

00:41:37.370 --> 00:41:39.540
Oh, my gosh, I've won awards.

00:41:39.540 --> 00:41:46.130
Oh, my gosh, Todd, your toys are so detailed
and so realistic.

00:41:46.130 --> 00:41:47.270
How do you do it?"

00:41:47.270 --> 00:41:49.050
You're asking the wrong question.

00:41:49.050 --> 00:41:52.450
The question should be, "How did they not
do it?"

00:41:52.450 --> 00:41:57.770
I'll tell you how I do it, easy, we use this
high tech, if you don't understand the high

00:41:57.770 --> 00:42:00.730
tech, go to Google, it's called a camera.

00:42:00.730 --> 00:42:05.880
And if you take a camera and you push the
button, it gives you a photo.

00:42:05.880 --> 00:42:10.990
And if you take the photo, and you take your
clay, and you do not stop manipulating the

00:42:10.990 --> 00:42:16.520
clay until it looks like the photo, then it
looks exactly like it.

00:42:16.520 --> 00:42:20.099
The question isn't how did I make my toys
look realistic?

00:42:20.099 --> 00:42:23.160
The question is, how did they not?

00:42:23.160 --> 00:42:29.690
Because the camera has been around for a hundred
years, if not more, how did they not use the

00:42:29.690 --> 00:42:30.690
tool?

00:42:30.690 --> 00:42:34.640
And so I use photo reference, and I'm a genius.

00:42:34.640 --> 00:42:38.150
Let me tell you, Tim, another rule of life.

00:42:38.150 --> 00:42:44.250
If you want to be smart, act and hang around
dumb people, because why does using photo

00:42:44.250 --> 00:42:46.329
reference get me awards?

00:42:46.329 --> 00:42:48.680
It shouldn't.

00:42:48.680 --> 00:42:50.760
But God bless dumb —
Tim Ferriss: And those are for the athletes,

00:42:50.760 --> 00:42:51.790
is that what you're talking about?

00:42:51.790 --> 00:42:52.790
The photographs?

00:42:52.790 --> 00:42:53.790
Todd McFarlane: Yeah, for the athletes.

00:42:53.790 --> 00:42:54.790
That was it.

00:42:54.790 --> 00:42:58.569
Look, we were making toys back then, it's
just manipulating clay.

00:42:58.569 --> 00:43:05.230
Clay will go into any position and pose and
shape you want it to.

00:43:05.230 --> 00:43:09.000
I wasn't doing anything original, I was just
putting the clay in a different shape.

00:43:09.000 --> 00:43:12.609
They had clay too, and they've been making
toys for 50 years.

00:43:12.609 --> 00:43:17.099
Why they chose not to put it in those shapes,
I don't know, go ask them.

00:43:17.099 --> 00:43:20.950
But some of it, Tim, became crystal clear
to me as I broke into the business why they

00:43:20.950 --> 00:43:22.559
didn't do it.

00:43:22.559 --> 00:43:27.140
Because if they did the detail that I did,
they would then have to paint it.

00:43:27.140 --> 00:43:33.349
Ah, and here's the magic, that every time
you put paint on a toy, that's a penny.

00:43:33.349 --> 00:43:35.590
"Oh, now we've got to paint that.

00:43:35.590 --> 00:43:36.590
Oh, a penny.

00:43:36.590 --> 00:43:37.590
Oh, a penny.

00:43:37.590 --> 00:43:38.590
Oh, a penny."

00:43:38.590 --> 00:43:43.040
And eventually they go, "That's 7, 8, 10,
12 extra cents.

00:43:43.040 --> 00:43:44.069
We can't do that.

00:43:44.069 --> 00:43:45.069
Why?

00:43:45.069 --> 00:43:48.140
Because we're making tens of millions of toys
times 12 cents."

00:43:48.140 --> 00:43:50.110
It just becomes a number.

00:43:50.110 --> 00:43:54.770
They're in the business to maximize shareholder
profits every 90 days.

00:43:54.770 --> 00:44:00.920
Their job isn't to be great artists, it's
to maximize margins and profits.

00:44:00.920 --> 00:44:04.000
So they go, "We need to make 10 million.

00:44:04.000 --> 00:44:05.110
How do we make the toy?

00:44:05.110 --> 00:44:07.750
We've got to manufacture it for 98 cents."

00:44:07.750 --> 00:44:09.250
I do the opposite, Tim.

00:44:09.250 --> 00:44:14.300
I go make the product as good as you can,
price it afterwards.

00:44:14.300 --> 00:44:17.270
And if it's of value, people will pay you
the money.

00:44:17.270 --> 00:44:22.150
It's why people will pay 10,000 for one car,
and other people will give you 70,000 for

00:44:22.150 --> 00:44:23.150
another car.

00:44:23.150 --> 00:44:25.280
They believe there's a value in that price.

00:44:25.280 --> 00:44:31.080
And at Toys"R"Us, they're going, "You can't
have a toy at eight or nine bucks.

00:44:31.080 --> 00:44:34.540
People won't give you eight or nine bucks
for a toy," back then.

00:44:34.540 --> 00:44:40.680
Of course they will, because they're walking
in another aisle giving you $200 for a PlayStation.

00:44:40.680 --> 00:44:44.821
If people think that there is value, they
will give the money to you.

00:44:44.821 --> 00:44:48.400
Just make value, price it afterwards.

00:44:48.400 --> 00:44:52.630
But they don't, and so they were making cheap,
ugly toys.

00:44:52.630 --> 00:44:53.630
God bless them.

00:44:53.630 --> 00:44:57.490
I was able to come in and make mediocre toys
and I look like a genius.

00:44:57.490 --> 00:44:59.731
Tim Ferriss: So two things real quick on that.

00:44:59.731 --> 00:45:06.581
The first is, for people who don't have a
lot of exposure to toys, and there's maybe

00:45:06.581 --> 00:45:11.700
a blur between toys and sculpture on some
level, but I just came back from Japan where

00:45:11.700 --> 00:45:17.140
I went to a showroom where they have, in effect,
toys, they don't have many movable joints

00:45:17.140 --> 00:45:22.610
or anything like that, that sell for thousands
of dollars.

00:45:22.610 --> 00:45:28.490
They cost as much as a car, and they're video
game characters that have been morphed into

00:45:28.490 --> 00:45:35.010
these beautiful three-dimensional, in effect,
plastic sculptures.

00:45:35.010 --> 00:45:38.300
There's always a market for high quality.

00:45:38.300 --> 00:45:39.780
Todd McFarlane: At a price!

00:45:39.780 --> 00:45:40.780
At a price.

00:45:40.780 --> 00:45:41.780
High quality at a price.

00:45:41.780 --> 00:45:42.780
Tim Ferriss: At a price.

00:45:42.780 --> 00:45:43.780
How did you think about distribution?

00:45:43.780 --> 00:45:44.810
You didn't want to be right next to the Teletubbies,
so what did you do?

00:45:44.810 --> 00:45:45.810
Todd McFarlane: No.

00:45:45.810 --> 00:45:47.690
Well, okay, here's where beggars and choosers
come.

00:45:47.690 --> 00:45:51.890
Look, I've said to people before, "If you're
going to have any amount of success in your

00:45:51.890 --> 00:45:59.630
life, one of the people you have to hang around
with is that guy named dumb luck."

00:45:59.630 --> 00:46:03.079
He has to come be a visitor every now and
then.

00:46:03.079 --> 00:46:06.790
So I'm going to give you my dumb luck moment.

00:46:06.790 --> 00:46:12.359
In comic books, I hate to say it, my dumb
luck moment was that an artist on a book literally

00:46:12.359 --> 00:46:13.680
died, and there was an opening.

00:46:13.680 --> 00:46:14.990
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, I remember that.

00:46:14.990 --> 00:46:17.550
Todd McFarlane: Okay, so he's dead.

00:46:17.550 --> 00:46:21.320
Todd, not good for him, good for you.

00:46:21.320 --> 00:46:28.369
My big break in toys was — and do not do
what I'm just about to say, because I don't

00:46:28.369 --> 00:46:31.849
think this lightning is going to strike in
the same place a couple times.

00:46:31.849 --> 00:46:37.000
I went to Toy Fair in New York City, comes
once a year, and this is where everybody brings

00:46:37.000 --> 00:46:39.210
all their worldly goods.

00:46:39.210 --> 00:46:44.690
At that time, this was back in 1994, Hasbro,
let's just take a big toy company, or Mattel,

00:46:44.690 --> 00:46:48.190
they had their own building, 10 stories high,
filled with everything.

00:46:48.190 --> 00:46:52.800
And the buyers would go into that building,
circle the floor one, go up the floor two,

00:46:52.800 --> 00:46:55.200
and go all the way up and down 10 floors.

00:46:55.200 --> 00:47:00.010
I, on my first Toy Fair, was in a building
that only had five floors.

00:47:00.010 --> 00:47:03.670
I was on one floor, but on that floor there
was 20 rooms.

00:47:03.670 --> 00:47:07.730
I was in one room, and that room was cut into
40 pieces.

00:47:07.730 --> 00:47:11.420
My space was five feet by five feet.

00:47:11.420 --> 00:47:16.980
Okay, now I'm at Toy Fair, and I'm in this
little corner here, and even dumber than that,

00:47:16.980 --> 00:47:18.869
Tim, I don't even have a prototype.

00:47:18.869 --> 00:47:23.040
This is how stupid I was, I didn't even bring
a prototype, we didn't have time to get it

00:47:23.040 --> 00:47:24.040
done.

00:47:24.040 --> 00:47:26.740
Tim Ferriss: Wait, so you bring — sorry,
keep going.

00:47:26.740 --> 00:47:28.290
I don't want to interrupt, but I'm just like,
"What the Hell are you doing?"

00:47:28.290 --> 00:47:33.359
Todd McFarlane: I'll tell you what I brought,
a drawing of Spawn on foamcore, and I cut

00:47:33.359 --> 00:47:36.770
it out and I put it in — so I go, "It's
going to look sort of like that drawing."

00:47:36.770 --> 00:47:39.950
That was it, that was my sell, that was my
whole speech.

00:47:39.950 --> 00:47:46.170
And, oh, by the way, I had made some Hot Wheel
toys based on a funny car that I had bought,

00:47:46.170 --> 00:47:49.060
and I'd hand them the Hot Wheel.

00:47:49.060 --> 00:47:52.280
Okay, and now the moment.

00:47:52.280 --> 00:47:56.770
At this point, Toys"R"Us is number one toy
buyer, not Walmart, and pretty soon Walmart's

00:47:56.770 --> 00:47:58.880
going to crush them, probably within six months.

00:47:58.880 --> 00:48:03.260
But at that point, Toys"R"Us is the toy buying
gods.

00:48:03.260 --> 00:48:09.400
And then, we're in this room with 40 of us,
like a bad swap meet when I was a kid.

00:48:09.400 --> 00:48:15.280
And the door opens up, and everybody stands
up and snaps like they're in an army barracks

00:48:15.280 --> 00:48:16.340
and the sergeant has come in.

00:48:16.340 --> 00:48:19.000
And I'm like, "Huh, what's that about?"

00:48:19.000 --> 00:48:24.270
Now, again, the room was so small that you
had to come down a little bit of a walkway,

00:48:24.270 --> 00:48:26.930
and then bend, there was a jog, and I was
around the jog.

00:48:26.930 --> 00:48:30.780
So I didn't know, I just saw people stand
up and present themselves in front of their

00:48:30.780 --> 00:48:31.780
beds.

00:48:31.780 --> 00:48:32.780
And it's like, "Oh, okay."

00:48:32.780 --> 00:48:37.300
And so I go around the corner to see, and
I go, "Who is that?"

00:48:37.300 --> 00:48:40.910
And they're going, "Oh, my God, that's the
buyer from Toys"R"Us.

00:48:40.910 --> 00:48:43.599
And it was like The Pope had come into the
room.

00:48:43.599 --> 00:48:45.440
And it was like, "Oh, my God."

00:48:45.440 --> 00:48:51.220
So I was watching him come, and as he's walking
closer and closer, down towards where I was

00:48:51.220 --> 00:48:54.330
standing, if he walked by you, he wasn't buying
anything.

00:48:54.330 --> 00:48:58.480
So it was a really cool visual, I still have
this moment, where it's like as you walk by

00:48:58.480 --> 00:49:02.859
people, you could just see them collapse physically
because it's like, "Oh, shit, he's not buying

00:49:02.859 --> 00:49:03.859
mine."

00:49:03.859 --> 00:49:08.310
And he'd keep walking by, and they'd go, "Oh,
crap, oh, crap, oh, crap, oh, crap, oh, crap."

00:49:08.310 --> 00:49:13.220
And then I went, "Oh, I guess I better go
stand by my barrack, and my bed."

00:49:13.220 --> 00:49:17.730
And so I stood in front of my little thing,
and they come around the corner, and guess

00:49:17.730 --> 00:49:18.730
what?

00:49:18.730 --> 00:49:22.140
The Toys"R"Us buyer stopped at my place.

00:49:22.140 --> 00:49:26.670
Now, Tim, did this buyer care about Spawn?

00:49:26.670 --> 00:49:27.670
No.

00:49:27.670 --> 00:49:28.670
Did he even know who Spawn was?

00:49:28.670 --> 00:49:29.670
No.

00:49:29.670 --> 00:49:30.670
Did he know who I was?

00:49:30.670 --> 00:49:31.670
Of course he didn't.

00:49:31.670 --> 00:49:33.270
So why did he stand there?

00:49:33.270 --> 00:49:39.110
This is the moment, he had an assistant, and
the assistant looked like he was about 21

00:49:39.110 --> 00:49:40.110
or 22.

00:49:40.110 --> 00:49:44.690
And then the magic words, "Hey, boss, this
is the guy I was talking to you about."

00:49:44.690 --> 00:49:49.190
Now remember, at this point, Spawn the comic
book is number one in the nation.

00:49:49.190 --> 00:49:56.010
So basically, this kid, I don't even remember
his name, I owe my career to this dude.

00:49:56.010 --> 00:50:01.589
And when he said, "This is a guy I'm talking
to you about," and all that Toys"R"Us buyer

00:50:01.589 --> 00:50:07.581
said, he just looked at it, and just went,
"Let me ask you a question.

00:50:07.581 --> 00:50:08.920
Can you get it to me at this price?"

00:50:08.920 --> 00:50:09.920
"Yes, sir."

00:50:09.920 --> 00:50:11.140
Did I know if I could, Tim?

00:50:11.140 --> 00:50:12.650
Of course I didn't.

00:50:12.650 --> 00:50:19.420
Always say yes to any opportunity; figure
out whether you can actually do it later.

00:50:19.420 --> 00:50:21.140
"Can you give me at that price?"

00:50:21.140 --> 00:50:22.140
"Of course I can."

00:50:22.140 --> 00:50:24.059
"Can you deliver it on this date?"

00:50:24.059 --> 00:50:26.770
"Of course I can."

00:50:26.770 --> 00:50:28.080
"Fine."

00:50:28.080 --> 00:50:29.190
I didn't know if I could, either.

00:50:29.190 --> 00:50:30.970
I didn't even have factories at that point.

00:50:30.970 --> 00:50:37.559
And so he goes, "If you can do that, I'll
put you in all my stores, I'll go storewide."

00:50:37.559 --> 00:50:42.369
And just so you guys know, if they take a
sample, they don't usually put you storewide.

00:50:42.369 --> 00:50:46.589
But he said, I don't know who his assistant
was, he must have been bending his ear, he

00:50:46.589 --> 00:50:47.820
goes, "I'll put you storewide."

00:50:47.820 --> 00:50:50.480
And I went, "Yes, sir."

00:50:50.480 --> 00:50:54.819
So he left all of a sudden all the other people
in the room came around me, because it was

00:50:54.819 --> 00:50:59.809
like, "Oh, my God," like I just won the lotto.

00:50:59.809 --> 00:51:06.250
And then, this is why those moments matter,
I couldn't even get a meeting with Walmart,

00:51:06.250 --> 00:51:09.510
and Kmart, and Target and those people back
then.

00:51:09.510 --> 00:51:15.700
And now, all of a sudden, I'm in Walmart's
room, and I get to now say, "Well, just to

00:51:15.700 --> 00:51:21.910
let you know, I've got this Spawn product,
and it will be going storewide in Toys"R"Us."

00:51:21.910 --> 00:51:24.650
Now does Walmart know anything about me or
Spawn?

00:51:24.650 --> 00:51:26.380
Of course they don't.

00:51:26.380 --> 00:51:29.190
But what they don't want to do is get beat
by their competition.

00:51:29.190 --> 00:51:31.710
So they go, "Toys"R"Us going storewide?"

00:51:31.710 --> 00:51:32.710
"Yeah."

00:51:32.710 --> 00:51:34.660
"Okay, we'll take some."

00:51:34.660 --> 00:51:36.410
And then, the dominoes.

00:51:36.410 --> 00:51:41.430
I go to Kmart, and Target, and all these other
places, and then you just keep adding.

00:51:41.430 --> 00:51:46.060
"Well, Toys"R"Us and Walmart are on board,"
and they're like, "What are we missing?"

00:51:46.060 --> 00:51:47.320
And it just becomes this thing.

00:51:47.320 --> 00:51:52.720
The next thing you know, I'm in all these
big stores, and I go, "Wow, without even having

00:51:52.720 --> 00:51:54.590
so much as a prototype."

00:51:54.590 --> 00:51:56.890
It doesn't usually work that way.

00:51:56.890 --> 00:52:01.180
Now, once you get into the stores, you must
now sell.

00:52:01.180 --> 00:52:05.799
Because people go, "Wow, Todd, you got your
product selling at Walmart," or whatever.

00:52:05.799 --> 00:52:08.359
Is that —
Tim Ferriss: Hold on, we're skipping a few

00:52:08.359 --> 00:52:09.359
steps.

00:52:09.359 --> 00:52:14.990
What was the hardest part of figuring out
how the Hell to make that volume of toys?

00:52:14.990 --> 00:52:18.700
Because I'm just thinking about the upfront
cost.

00:52:18.700 --> 00:52:21.230
Thinking about it from the standpoint of Toys"R"Us,
they're like, "Okay, great, we'll give you

00:52:21.230 --> 00:52:23.119
net 90 terms.

00:52:23.119 --> 00:52:27.309
We have the ability to return all of them
if they don't sell," it could just kill you

00:52:27.309 --> 00:52:28.309
financially, I would think.

00:52:28.309 --> 00:52:29.970
So how do you think —
Todd McFarlane: No, they don't return.

00:52:29.970 --> 00:52:30.970
Tim Ferriss: Okay.

00:52:30.970 --> 00:52:32.630
All right, tell me more.

00:52:32.630 --> 00:52:36.220
Because if it was a Home Shopping Network
or something, I don't think this is absolutely

00:52:36.220 --> 00:52:40.880
accurate, but with some retailers, they have
all these terms that can just crush you if

00:52:40.880 --> 00:52:41.880
it's a lot of inventory.

00:52:41.880 --> 00:52:44.820
Todd McFarlane: Yeah, I've been fortunate
enough that both toys and comic books are,

00:52:44.820 --> 00:52:47.549
if you order, it's yours.

00:52:47.549 --> 00:52:55.210
Okay, what you want to do on in the toy side
is to make sure that they're not overbuying.

00:52:55.210 --> 00:52:56.619
Both parties need to be realistic.

00:52:56.619 --> 00:53:01.180
And it's one of the reasons I think, Tim,
that I've survived as long as I have, because

00:53:01.180 --> 00:53:03.550
I've talked them down from orders.

00:53:03.550 --> 00:53:05.960
Which is actually the opposite of what they're
used to.

00:53:05.960 --> 00:53:09.180
Which I go, "I know you want 75,000 units
of that.

00:53:09.180 --> 00:53:12.890
But you know what, why don't we start you
at 40?

00:53:12.890 --> 00:53:16.530
I'll build some inventory, it'll be on my
nickel.

00:53:16.530 --> 00:53:20.440
And if you need more, then we'll just supply
you as you need.

00:53:20.440 --> 00:53:23.180
I think if you buy 75..."

00:53:23.180 --> 00:53:25.530
Because here's the math, just so everybody
knows.

00:53:25.530 --> 00:53:31.950
You buy 75 ,and you sell 60, they're going
to go, "Ah, I got 15,000 in inventory, it's

00:53:31.950 --> 00:53:33.240
not a success."

00:53:33.240 --> 00:53:39.549
If you give them 40, and then they have to
buy another 20, and they sell 60, they go,

00:53:39.549 --> 00:53:42.650
"Wow, we ordered 50 more than our original
order."

00:53:42.650 --> 00:53:49.109
You're still at 60, but one, they're disappointed,
and one, they're not.

00:53:49.109 --> 00:53:53.230
You're still playing some weird mind games
a little bit so that they feel good about

00:53:53.230 --> 00:53:55.369
whatever number was there.

00:53:55.369 --> 00:54:01.040
And again, I'm not a public company, and I
don't have to maximize profits.

00:54:01.040 --> 00:54:06.540
My goal is, I've told all my employees, "Get
me to zero every year, and we get to do it

00:54:06.540 --> 00:54:08.599
again for another year."

00:54:08.599 --> 00:54:14.700
So as long as we're money in, money out, sort
of about the same, let's keep doing it.

00:54:14.700 --> 00:54:16.800
I'm okay, I don't need to impress anybody.

00:54:16.800 --> 00:54:20.280
My kids, I've already paid for their college,
they're off, the house is paid for, we're

00:54:20.280 --> 00:54:21.280
good.

00:54:21.280 --> 00:54:22.280
My wife and I are good.

00:54:22.280 --> 00:54:25.770
We don't need —
Tim Ferriss: Is the paired directive just

00:54:25.770 --> 00:54:30.250
like, "Let's make the best stuff we possibly
can?"

00:54:30.250 --> 00:54:34.500
Because there's probably a couple of paired
principles, I would imagine, that go with

00:54:34.500 --> 00:54:35.500
that.

00:54:35.500 --> 00:54:36.500
Todd McFarlane: That was it.

00:54:36.500 --> 00:54:40.549
The company came from one simple question,
"Why can't toys be cooler?"

00:54:40.549 --> 00:54:42.900
Again, I was looking at it from an artistic
point of view.

00:54:42.900 --> 00:54:45.210
I wasn't asking it from a business point of
view.

00:54:45.210 --> 00:54:50.190
Again, you find out what that means when you
ask that question.

00:54:50.190 --> 00:54:52.160
And so I go, "Why can't they make cooler toys?"

00:54:52.160 --> 00:54:57.850
I found out, Tim, that the reason they weren't
making cooler toys is because, again, like

00:54:57.850 --> 00:55:03.290
I said, every time they did an extra joint,
or an extra movement, or an extra paint job

00:55:03.290 --> 00:55:06.609
or something, it was pennies, and it just
added up.

00:55:06.609 --> 00:55:13.819
And so I ran into this thing where I had my
prototypes at Toy Fair, because I started

00:55:13.819 --> 00:55:17.170
actually making prototypes, and each year
I would bring them.

00:55:17.170 --> 00:55:21.150
And we would sculpt at twice the size of the
final product.

00:55:21.150 --> 00:55:22.150
Why?

00:55:22.150 --> 00:55:24.549
Because the bigger you sculpt it, the more
detail you can put in it.

00:55:24.549 --> 00:55:27.089
And when you shrink it down, it holds the
detail.

00:55:27.089 --> 00:55:31.300
So I would bring these sculpts, and I'd bring
them to the showrooms.

00:55:31.300 --> 00:55:35.020
And the buyers would go, "Yeah, Todd, they
look really, really, really nice.

00:55:35.020 --> 00:55:37.520
I get it, but what's the product going to
look like?

00:55:37.520 --> 00:55:38.640
It's never going to look like this."

00:55:38.640 --> 00:55:39.640
And I went, "What are you talking about?

00:55:39.640 --> 00:55:40.740
It's going to look exactly like this."

00:55:40.740 --> 00:55:42.920
And they're like, "Silly boy, that's not how
it works."

00:55:42.920 --> 00:55:48.260
I found out, because I spied on Hasbro and
Mattel and all the big guys, I was able to

00:55:48.260 --> 00:55:52.820
get into their showrooms, that they were doing
this thing where they would bring these prototypes

00:55:52.820 --> 00:55:54.349
out that were awesome.

00:55:54.349 --> 00:55:58.740
Think of them as like the concept cars at
an auto show, but you never will see those

00:55:58.740 --> 00:55:59.740
cool cars.

00:55:59.740 --> 00:56:01.570
They're just there, they're eye candy.

00:56:01.570 --> 00:56:04.930
And I remember seeing this one toy that was
super awesome.

00:56:04.930 --> 00:56:07.120
And I went, "Wow, that's good."

00:56:07.120 --> 00:56:13.579
And then I saw it months later, literally
at the store, and it was blue plastic.

00:56:13.579 --> 00:56:15.670
Didn't put even a drop of paint on it.

00:56:15.670 --> 00:56:20.589
So what I ended up having to do, because I
was paying for the sins of these public companies,

00:56:20.589 --> 00:56:27.549
is that I then took my prototypes, and I showed
from step 1 to step 10.

00:56:27.549 --> 00:56:30.170
And when the buyer got to step 10, guess what
it was?

00:56:30.170 --> 00:56:34.070
It was the product, in package, manufactured.

00:56:34.070 --> 00:56:39.640
I go, "That's the manufactured toy, that's
what it's going to look like in your store."

00:56:39.640 --> 00:56:44.780
And they couldn't believe it, that it was
like, "Whoa, that looks just like your prototype."

00:56:44.780 --> 00:56:47.000
"Yes, that's what I've been saying."

00:56:47.000 --> 00:56:49.440
Tim Ferriss: What's the point of a prototype?

00:56:49.440 --> 00:56:52.271
Todd McFarlane: Right, but everybody else
had been using a prototype just to —

00:56:52.271 --> 00:56:53.271
Tim Ferriss: Was doing it the other way.

00:56:53.271 --> 00:56:54.271
Todd McFarlane: — yeah, to sucker them in.

00:56:54.271 --> 00:56:58.190
So eventually, they got to the point that
like, "Hey, if McFarlane shows you something,

00:56:58.190 --> 00:57:00.559
that's actually what it's going to look like."

00:57:00.559 --> 00:57:07.730
And they started buying into that maybe there
was a quality component to these products

00:57:07.730 --> 00:57:08.730
that were coming in.

00:57:08.730 --> 00:57:14.440
I didn't have any big brands, so I had to
beat them with pricing and quality, because

00:57:14.440 --> 00:57:18.960
how else am I going to survive next to Star
Wars and Transformers and G.I.

00:57:18.960 --> 00:57:20.380
Joe and Marvel?

00:57:20.380 --> 00:57:21.380
I can't.

00:57:21.380 --> 00:57:22.650
I have to just —
Tim Ferriss: And what year is this, roughly,

00:57:22.650 --> 00:57:23.650
would you say?

00:57:23.650 --> 00:57:29.960
Todd McFarlane: Well, we started the toy company
in '94, and so literally from the first time,

00:57:29.960 --> 00:57:31.540
we hit the ground running.

00:57:31.540 --> 00:57:37.079
And here's the thing that's interesting, Tim,
is that if you look at the first two, three,

00:57:37.079 --> 00:57:41.079
four waves of toys I put out, they were winning
awards.

00:57:41.079 --> 00:57:44.020
And people were going, "Oh, my God, Todd,
look at you.

00:57:44.020 --> 00:57:47.140
You've upgraded the whole industry."

00:57:47.140 --> 00:57:52.790
And if you look at those toys, compared to
my standards today, they are laughable.

00:57:52.790 --> 00:57:57.880
And even yet, back then, just that upgrade
put me on the map.

00:57:57.880 --> 00:58:02.960
And here's the other thing too, nobody else
was doing what I was doing in terms of quality,

00:58:02.960 --> 00:58:07.480
in terms of the pricing that went with it,
and then in terms of brand selection.

00:58:07.480 --> 00:58:11.799
So here's the thing about big corporations.

00:58:11.799 --> 00:58:15.000
Their blessing and their curse is their size.

00:58:15.000 --> 00:58:20.609
They have more people, more money, more everything,
they can squish you at every moment.

00:58:20.609 --> 00:58:26.589
But because of that giant, enormous size,
they don't move fast.

00:58:26.589 --> 00:58:31.830
And if you can just be quick and nimble with
your business — I never once said, "I'm

00:58:31.830 --> 00:58:33.829
going to slay the giant."

00:58:33.829 --> 00:58:36.839
That's never the question, "Todd, how do you
take down Hasbro?"

00:58:36.839 --> 00:58:39.090
No, you're asking the question in reverse.

00:58:39.090 --> 00:58:42.250
Not how do I take down Hasbro and Mattel,
they're billion-dollar empires.

00:58:42.250 --> 00:58:46.910
The question is, how do billion-dollar empires
not squish me?

00:58:46.910 --> 00:58:48.480
I am a gnat.

00:58:48.480 --> 00:58:52.060
How can they not kill me?

00:58:52.060 --> 00:58:57.450
Why would Hasbro and Mattel, or any other
public company, want me to have one foot of

00:58:57.450 --> 00:58:58.640
space in Walmart?

00:58:58.640 --> 00:59:02.020
That's their territory, that's their money.

00:59:02.020 --> 00:59:04.990
And so the question is, why can't they kill
me?

00:59:04.990 --> 00:59:10.420
And the answer is, because when you're big,
and you're a giant, and you're a sloth, and

00:59:10.420 --> 00:59:14.200
I'm a mosquito, and I'm biting you on your
right arm.

00:59:14.200 --> 00:59:20.480
By the time you contemplate slapping that
mosquito, you're so slow with your movement,

00:59:20.480 --> 00:59:25.770
that by the time you slap your arm, I'm on
the other side.

00:59:25.770 --> 00:59:28.490
They can't move quick enough, Tim.

00:59:28.490 --> 00:59:30.319
And that's their weakness.

00:59:30.319 --> 00:59:37.400
And the weakness in toys was they will never
do anything outside their comfort zone.

00:59:37.400 --> 00:59:38.400
What does that mean?

00:59:38.400 --> 00:59:42.730
They'll never make Freddy Krueger, they'll
never make The Terminator toy, they'll never

00:59:42.730 --> 00:59:46.970
make The Matrix, they'll never make, essentially,
R-rated toys.

00:59:46.970 --> 00:59:52.730
And I felt that there was a need, that you
could sell toys to adults, you just have to

00:59:52.730 --> 00:59:54.800
put it in the right content.

00:59:54.800 --> 00:59:59.890
I can sell a toy to my mom, I just have to
make it look like Tom Jones, and she'll buy

00:59:59.890 --> 01:00:02.119
it, because she loves Tom Jones.

01:00:02.119 --> 01:00:06.819
So if you put it in the right brand, then
there's an audience that's out there.

01:00:06.819 --> 01:00:08.890
And now we call it geekdom.

01:00:08.890 --> 01:00:14.290
And I was the only guy doing it, so everything
I was doing was like, "Oh, my gosh."

01:00:14.290 --> 01:00:18.980
There's now dozens of people and companies
that do what I do, so I'm not nearly as unique

01:00:18.980 --> 01:00:19.980
anymore.

01:00:19.980 --> 01:00:21.299
But back then, I was the trailblazer.

01:00:21.299 --> 01:00:23.789
Tim Ferriss: Let's talk about 1994.

01:00:23.789 --> 01:00:30.520
The reason I asked about that timing ties
into where you were going a handful of minutes

01:00:30.520 --> 01:00:33.770
ago with respect to how do you sell these
toys.

01:00:33.770 --> 01:00:42.010
How do you get the sell through once you get
toys placed storewide in, say, a Toys"R"Us?

01:00:42.010 --> 01:00:48.470
And 1994 is relevant, because I was thinking,
how would you have any direct fan outreach

01:00:48.470 --> 01:00:50.070
to live sales?

01:00:50.070 --> 01:00:52.310
Todd McFarlane: Right, the internet, it doesn't
exist.

01:00:52.310 --> 01:00:56.770
Tim Ferriss: Because now you have social,
you have email, even email was really nascent

01:00:56.770 --> 01:01:00.350
in terms of mainstream adoption in 1994.

01:01:00.350 --> 01:01:04.770
So a couple of questions, and you can tackle
them in any order.

01:01:04.770 --> 01:01:10.329
One is, how on Earth do you actually ensure
these toys sell once you get them at retail?

01:01:10.329 --> 01:01:17.579
The second is related to the comment you made
about so-and-so huge toy company owning real

01:01:17.579 --> 01:01:22.299
estate in a given retail store or chain.

01:01:22.299 --> 01:01:27.030
And I know that it's fairly common at retail
for people to pay for placement.

01:01:27.030 --> 01:01:33.750
They pay extra, they pay the retailer for
end caps, they own the first, say, 20 feet

01:01:33.750 --> 01:01:34.750
of a store.

01:01:34.750 --> 01:01:41.010
I'm making up this example, but Coca-Cola,
boom, they own the first 20-feet deep at A,

01:01:41.010 --> 01:01:42.740
B, and C retailers.

01:01:42.740 --> 01:01:48.750
And you literally cannot invade that territory,
it's just not possible, it's been paid for.

01:01:48.750 --> 01:01:51.270
This is true in publishing as well, with books
and so on.

01:01:51.270 --> 01:01:54.599
There are all these, they might call them
co-marketing fees, they might call them placement

01:01:54.599 --> 01:01:55.599
fees.

01:01:55.599 --> 01:02:01.450
There are all sorts of crazy incentives that
make it hard for a smaller company to break

01:02:01.450 --> 01:02:02.450
in.

01:02:02.450 --> 01:02:06.340
So one is, how on Earth do you sell these
toys when you have them placed at retail?

01:02:06.340 --> 01:02:13.150
But then, maybe the precursor to that is,
how do you even get the placement?

01:02:13.150 --> 01:02:16.260
Maybe this doesn't exist in toys, but do you
have to pay those placement fees?

01:02:16.260 --> 01:02:18.190
In which case, how do you pay for it?

01:02:18.190 --> 01:02:23.230
Todd McFarlane: There are iterations of what
you're talking about, but not quite what you're

01:02:23.230 --> 01:02:24.420
saying.

01:02:24.420 --> 01:02:28.280
I think this is closer to what the reality
is.

01:02:28.280 --> 01:02:33.510
The stores, let's say they have a run of a
hundred feet.

01:02:33.510 --> 01:02:40.030
Again, I'm in the boys toy action figure aisle,
let's call it aisle five.

01:02:40.030 --> 01:02:43.390
Aisle five is a hundred feet long.

01:02:43.390 --> 01:02:50.319
I think the buyers, for the most part, go,
"I'm going to dedicate 85 percent of that

01:02:50.319 --> 01:02:52.830
to the big brands."

01:02:52.830 --> 01:02:59.240
And we all know what they are, because we
see foot upon foot upon foot of these brands.

01:02:59.240 --> 01:03:03.650
And they rotate them out depending on what
the movies are, and what's hot out in the

01:03:03.650 --> 01:03:04.650
zeitgeist.

01:03:04.650 --> 01:03:07.619
But they go, "Okay, 85 percent of that's going
to be given to the thing that's going to be

01:03:07.619 --> 01:03:09.600
in every company's..."

01:03:09.600 --> 01:03:13.849
I don't care whether you're Kmart, Walmart,
Amazon, everybody's going to have it.

01:03:13.849 --> 01:03:15.930
Then there's the 15 percent left.

01:03:15.930 --> 01:03:23.680
And the 15 percent is "Ah, this is just in
case we're missing something.

01:03:23.680 --> 01:03:30.000
Just to give us a little bit of diversity,
just so that we can hedge our bet at the roulette

01:03:30.000 --> 01:03:31.520
table a little bit."

01:03:31.520 --> 01:03:34.829
And that was the space that I needed to get
into.

01:03:34.829 --> 01:03:42.010
I needed to figure out, could I rise and get
into the sales that were worthy of that 15

01:03:42.010 --> 01:03:43.260
percent?

01:03:43.260 --> 01:03:49.240
And so, to your first question, how did I
get people to know and buy the Spawn toys

01:03:49.240 --> 01:03:50.690
at the beginning, I didn't.

01:03:50.690 --> 01:03:55.370
In all honesty, the internet, and I know this
seems foreign to people, was literally just

01:03:55.370 --> 01:03:56.800
in its infancy.

01:03:56.800 --> 01:03:59.730
And so that wasn't the gameplay.

01:03:59.730 --> 01:04:08.730
The only play was value, quality, and put
it together, and hopefully people would notice

01:04:08.730 --> 01:04:09.730
it.

01:04:09.730 --> 01:04:16.710
Because the upside of making toys and action
figures is the packaging is see through, so

01:04:16.710 --> 01:04:18.789
you actually get to see what you're buying.

01:04:18.789 --> 01:04:24.160
There's no fake photo, and then you take it
home and you go, "Man, that didn't look that

01:04:24.160 --> 01:04:25.329
good."

01:04:25.329 --> 01:04:27.200
What you see is what you get.

01:04:27.200 --> 01:04:35.440
And I think that my toys just quickly passed
the eye test to a lot of people.

01:04:35.440 --> 01:04:40.190
And again, remember, I was coming off setting
records with Spider-Man, and then we quickly

01:04:40.190 --> 01:04:44.869
went to do the comic books with Spawn, so
there was a big fan base that was there.

01:04:44.869 --> 01:04:47.829
And they went, "Oh, my gosh, Todd's going
to do some toys."

01:04:47.829 --> 01:04:54.460
So they jumped on very quickly, and as you
know, in any business, you're only as good

01:04:54.460 --> 01:04:56.760
as your last set of sales.

01:04:56.760 --> 01:05:01.869
It doesn't matter what you have today, they
just look at the data of your last ones.

01:05:01.869 --> 01:05:08.650
And so once the first series came out and
did really well, and then the second one continued,

01:05:08.650 --> 01:05:13.220
because I got to put some characters in it,
I don't know, it just — and then the internet

01:05:13.220 --> 01:05:16.480
started to become a little bit relevant.

01:05:16.480 --> 01:05:20.750
And we were getting traffic even on our own
website that was phenomenal.

01:05:20.750 --> 01:05:24.000
I was getting pulled into Hollywood, people
going, "Todd, how are you getting that much

01:05:24.000 --> 01:05:25.000
traffic?"

01:05:25.000 --> 01:05:30.309
I mean big studio asking me how I was getting
the traffic that they weren't getting as a

01:05:30.309 --> 01:05:31.309
studio.

01:05:31.309 --> 01:05:36.299
So at some point, the geeks just find you,
Tim.

01:05:36.299 --> 01:05:42.140
And I would like to say that I knew what I
was doing all this time, and that somehow

01:05:42.140 --> 01:05:43.250
I'm a savant.

01:05:43.250 --> 01:05:44.450
It's not true.

01:05:44.450 --> 01:05:52.430
You just try your darnedest, and sometimes
that 30-foot shot banks into the bucket, and

01:05:52.430 --> 01:05:54.359
you score the three points and you go, "Wow."

01:05:54.359 --> 01:05:57.089
I can't say I can replicate it.

01:05:57.089 --> 01:06:01.500
But what you do is, once you have momentum,
can you keep momentum?

01:06:01.500 --> 01:06:05.380
It's like a good disc jockey, the good disc
jockey keeps people on the floor dancing.

01:06:05.380 --> 01:06:09.270
The bad ones figure out how to put on a song
that everybody walks off.

01:06:09.270 --> 01:06:14.109
I need to basically keep the momentum going
and finding places.

01:06:14.109 --> 01:06:15.760
Here's where I kept the momentum.

01:06:15.760 --> 01:06:24.010
We started selling places like Virgin Records
and Tower records and KB Toys and Hot Topic

01:06:24.010 --> 01:06:29.130
and we were going to what I was said earlier,
non-traditional places, and they were buying

01:06:29.130 --> 01:06:30.760
lots and lots of toys.

01:06:30.760 --> 01:06:31.760
You know why?

01:06:31.760 --> 01:06:38.140
Because I was making like crazy R-rated, at
times gory, toys, and then all they cared

01:06:38.140 --> 01:06:42.760
about was the one magic sentence, "Walmart's
not buying this."

01:06:42.760 --> 01:06:44.240
Boom, "We'll take it."

01:06:44.240 --> 01:06:48.839
Because now they don't have to compete, because
if you go into competition with the big boys,

01:06:48.839 --> 01:06:52.180
everybody has the same product, you're just
going to get into a pricing war.

01:06:52.180 --> 01:06:56.900
And they want a product that the big boys
didn't have.

01:06:56.900 --> 01:07:02.080
And back then I could make anything up in
my head, put it in the plastic, and I had

01:07:02.080 --> 01:07:06.619
hundreds and hundreds of thousands of sales
waiting for these guys who didn't want to

01:07:06.619 --> 01:07:08.819
compete with the big boys.

01:07:08.819 --> 01:07:16.359
Unfortunately, the world changes and Babbage's
and Virgin Records and KB, all these companies

01:07:16.359 --> 01:07:18.329
don't even exist anymore.

01:07:18.329 --> 01:07:22.760
So there was a consolidation and some of the
fun that we were having at the beginning is

01:07:22.760 --> 01:07:25.180
no longer sort of available to us.

01:07:25.180 --> 01:07:30.569
Tim Ferriss: So momentum, you strike me as
someone who's a very good sort of creative

01:07:30.569 --> 01:07:38.980
and business DJ, to push the metaphor, which
I like, you mentioned the four pillars, games,

01:07:38.980 --> 01:07:41.109
TV, movies, toys.

01:07:41.109 --> 01:07:48.069
It seems like toys came in one of the earlier
chapters in terms of setting that pillar of

01:07:48.069 --> 01:07:49.500
the foundation.

01:07:49.500 --> 01:07:58.799
Where did, whether it's music, TV, directing,
any of those other elements, come into the

01:07:58.799 --> 01:07:59.799
picture?

01:07:59.799 --> 01:08:00.799
Todd McFarlane: Okay, so I'm going to get
to that.

01:08:00.799 --> 01:08:03.010
Let me just go back to toys and tell one more
quick story —

01:08:03.010 --> 01:08:04.010
Tim Ferriss: Sure.

01:08:04.010 --> 01:08:05.010
Todd McFarlane: — on the toy end.

01:08:05.010 --> 01:08:10.500
So there is another story that people to this
day scratched their head on, and it was in

01:08:10.500 --> 01:08:15.400
1998, Mark McGwire — if you're a baseball
fan — Mark McGwire and Sammy Sosa were going

01:08:15.400 --> 01:08:17.460
for the home run title.

01:08:17.460 --> 01:08:22.230
And at the end of the season Mark McGwire
had hit 70 home runs, the prior record was

01:08:22.230 --> 01:08:24.009
61 home runs by Roger Maris.

01:08:24.009 --> 01:08:26.730
Sammy Sosa hit 66 that year.

01:08:26.730 --> 01:08:29.509
Okay, why is that relevant?

01:08:29.509 --> 01:08:35.460
Because for a couple years I had been — I'd
started my toy company in 1994 and I'm an

01:08:35.460 --> 01:08:37.550
athlete, and again, stick to what you know.

01:08:37.550 --> 01:08:39.510
I know sports.

01:08:39.510 --> 01:08:43.610
And I was trying to make sports toys and I
couldn't make sports toys.

01:08:43.610 --> 01:08:44.610
Why?

01:08:44.610 --> 01:08:46.780
I couldn't even get a meeting because I'm
a nobody.

01:08:46.780 --> 01:08:51.900
The NHL, major league baseball, the NBA, NHL,
why would anybody let me in the front door?

01:08:51.900 --> 01:08:53.029
They wouldn't.

01:08:53.029 --> 01:08:56.679
And so you have to figure out back doors and
alternatives.

01:08:56.679 --> 01:08:57.969
So here is the play.

01:08:57.969 --> 01:09:03.960
And again, from one perspective it seems like
the dumbest thing that a human being can do.

01:09:03.960 --> 01:09:06.109
I'll give you what I was doing during that
play.

01:09:06.109 --> 01:09:11.400
Anyways, the 70 McGwire ball, the last ball
he hit that was a record, goes up for an auction

01:09:11.400 --> 01:09:12.880
in Madison Square Garden.

01:09:12.880 --> 01:09:14.319
I'm the anonymous bidder.

01:09:14.319 --> 01:09:20.520
Anyways, nobody's ever spent more than $300,000,
$400,000 on any baseball paraphernalia.

01:09:20.520 --> 01:09:26.040
At the end of the auction, I end up winning
the 70 ball and it costs me three million

01:09:26.040 --> 01:09:27.040
dollars.

01:09:27.040 --> 01:09:29.850
10 times what the previous record was, three
million dollars.

01:09:29.850 --> 01:09:40.279
Now you can say, and I understand this perspective,
why would any human being with a brain spend

01:09:40.279 --> 01:09:43.819
three million for a $3 baseball?

01:09:43.819 --> 01:09:45.380
Why would they do that?

01:09:45.380 --> 01:09:49.290
Especially, given a couple years later, Barry
Bonds broke the record.

01:09:49.290 --> 01:09:52.699
Oh, by the way, I had to go buy that ball
too.

01:09:52.699 --> 01:09:54.570
But I bought the 70 ball.

01:09:54.570 --> 01:09:57.360
Here's what the 70 ball did and it seems like
the stupidest thing.

01:09:57.360 --> 01:09:58.400
Three million dollars for a baseball.

01:09:58.400 --> 01:09:59.400
It's stupid.

01:09:59.400 --> 01:10:00.490
You're never going to get your money back
out of it.

01:10:00.490 --> 01:10:03.340
It depends on how you're looking at it.

01:10:03.340 --> 01:10:05.220
Here's how I was looking at it.

01:10:05.220 --> 01:10:09.300
I knew that spending and getting that ball
would get me headlines.

01:10:09.300 --> 01:10:16.550
So instantly it was a national story and I
was on lots of talk radio shows and TV shows

01:10:16.550 --> 01:10:17.550
and everything.

01:10:17.550 --> 01:10:19.880
I mean I canvased the planet.

01:10:19.880 --> 01:10:24.679
And do you know how much money it would cost
to get that attention by buying ads in any

01:10:24.679 --> 01:10:25.679
of those areas?

01:10:25.679 --> 01:10:30.400
It would cost you way more than three million,
but let's put that to the side.

01:10:30.400 --> 01:10:31.640
Here's what it did.

01:10:31.640 --> 01:10:37.820
Those headlines then got seen by all the sports
leagues and the unions.

01:10:37.820 --> 01:10:42.201
And when I phoned the next time and said,
"Hey, I'm Todd McFarlane and maybe you've

01:10:42.201 --> 01:10:44.870
heard of me, I bought the McGwire ball, but
I'd just like to talk to you.

01:10:44.870 --> 01:10:46.160
I'm also in the toy business."

01:10:46.160 --> 01:10:49.950
They let me in the door and I got to have
a conversation with them.

01:10:49.950 --> 01:10:56.050
In short order I ended up having the license
for baseball, football, basketball, and hockey.

01:10:56.050 --> 01:10:58.040
I did it for 13 years.

01:10:58.040 --> 01:11:02.250
I can tell you, ladies and gentlemen, I made
more than three million dollars in profits

01:11:02.250 --> 01:11:07.060
on those toys multiple times over those 13
years.

01:11:07.060 --> 01:11:13.850
So at some point you have to put up an ante
on the table to play the big game with the

01:11:13.850 --> 01:11:16.130
big boys in poker.

01:11:16.130 --> 01:11:23.320
And my ante, unfortunately, was a three million
dollar baseball so that they took that as

01:11:23.320 --> 01:11:27.610
saying, which, just so everybody knows, it
was every red cent I had and then some.

01:11:27.610 --> 01:11:30.640
I had to beg, borrow and steal to pay for
it.

01:11:30.640 --> 01:11:35.469
Then what ended up happening, people see those
headlines and they go, "Oh, if he's got three

01:11:35.469 --> 01:11:40.210
million dollars to wipe his ass on a baseball,
he must be successful, he must have a lot

01:11:40.210 --> 01:11:41.560
of money.

01:11:41.560 --> 01:11:43.760
Bring the successful guy into the room."

01:11:43.760 --> 01:11:47.710
And I got the four licenses and it was there.

01:11:47.710 --> 01:11:52.750
One last comment on it, why do you care that
I spent three million on a baseball?

01:11:52.750 --> 01:11:58.560
Because for me, I've made it over multiple
times, and any time I do any interview, because

01:11:58.560 --> 01:12:02.870
I'm now the expert — just so everybody knows
— guess how many interviews I did when Aaron

01:12:02.870 --> 01:12:06.179
Judge, this year, was going to do a ball,
people were asking me how much the ball is

01:12:06.179 --> 01:12:07.179
worth.

01:12:07.179 --> 01:12:10.770
I'm now an expert because I spent an ungodly
amount on one ball, I've become an expert.

01:12:10.770 --> 01:12:12.310
This is how silly all this is.

01:12:12.310 --> 01:12:16.590
But I get to amortize that three million dollars
once again that's sitting there.

01:12:16.590 --> 01:12:23.151
But if I don't spend that money and I don't
get there, I don't get to the table, then

01:12:23.151 --> 01:12:26.530
my company never does any of it.

01:12:26.530 --> 01:12:32.929
And here's why, this is one of the reasons,
Tim, I can't be public, because you can't

01:12:32.929 --> 01:12:34.350
play that game.

01:12:34.350 --> 01:12:39.370
I can't go to the shareholders and to the
board and the chairman of the board and say,

01:12:39.370 --> 01:12:45.500
"Hey, I've got this gut feeling that if I
do this one thing I think it will pay off."

01:12:45.500 --> 01:12:50.990
If you can't categorize it on a spreadsheet
in rows and columns and tell them what it's

01:12:50.990 --> 01:12:56.571
going to equate to in terms of capital money
and profit margins in 90 days, you're going

01:12:56.571 --> 01:12:57.940
to get shot down.

01:12:57.940 --> 01:13:00.489
And this is why I can't go public.

01:13:00.489 --> 01:13:06.230
And let me tell you, when my toy companies
were flying, did Wall Street come and knock

01:13:06.230 --> 01:13:07.230
on my door?

01:13:07.230 --> 01:13:08.230
Of course they did, Tim.

01:13:08.230 --> 01:13:09.570
Of course they did.

01:13:09.570 --> 01:13:16.989
But I horrified them to the point that I'd
become a bit of a talking moment because I've

01:13:16.989 --> 01:13:18.300
run into them years later.

01:13:18.300 --> 01:13:21.360
They would come, and here's what I found with
Wall Street.

01:13:21.360 --> 01:13:25.290
"Todd, we're going to give you X amount of
dollars and we're going to buy your company."

01:13:25.290 --> 01:13:28.320
"Okay, that's cool, but I'm still in control."

01:13:28.320 --> 01:13:31.090
"Well, no, no, no, no, at that amount of money
you're not in control."

01:13:31.090 --> 01:13:32.090
"What?

01:13:32.090 --> 01:13:33.520
I've got to give up control of my company?"

01:13:33.520 --> 01:13:34.520
"Uh-huh."

01:13:34.520 --> 01:13:35.520
"For what, your money?"

01:13:35.520 --> 01:13:36.520
"Uh-huh."

01:13:36.520 --> 01:13:37.520
"I already got got money.

01:13:37.520 --> 01:13:39.120
I don't need any of your money."

01:13:39.120 --> 01:13:40.370
So they're like, "Okay."

01:13:40.370 --> 01:13:44.810
And then here's where Wall Street is literally
a one-trick pony.

01:13:44.810 --> 01:13:49.540
"Okay Todd, we won't give you X amount of
money.

01:13:49.540 --> 01:13:53.250
We see that you're a little stubborn, we'll
give you 2X."

01:13:53.250 --> 01:13:58.699
"Okay, but if you give me twice the money,
do I have control?"

01:13:58.699 --> 01:13:59.699
"No."

01:13:59.699 --> 01:14:02.690
"Whoa, that's a problem."

01:14:02.690 --> 01:14:06.780
"Todd, last time, we'll give you 3X of money."

01:14:06.780 --> 01:14:13.070
"Guys, let me now define what money is to
me.

01:14:13.070 --> 01:14:16.820
Here's the word — I'm Canadian so maybe
you guys in America do it a little bit differently.

01:14:16.820 --> 01:14:20.489
So my dad taught me a word, it's called useless.

01:14:20.489 --> 01:14:21.780
And here's what useless is.

01:14:21.780 --> 01:14:25.679
It's when you have something and you don't
use it.

01:14:25.679 --> 01:14:29.739
So do you know that I have money in the bank
right now, which basically means I'm not using

01:14:29.739 --> 01:14:30.739
it.

01:14:30.739 --> 01:14:33.090
So it's kind of useless to me because I'm
not using it because it's just sitting in

01:14:33.090 --> 01:14:35.760
a bank doing nothing."

01:14:35.760 --> 01:14:42.320
Do you know the reaction to people from Wall
Street when you call their one product useless?

01:14:42.320 --> 01:14:45.050
Do you know how horrifying it is?

01:14:45.050 --> 01:14:48.679
That they're just going, "What did you just
call money?"

01:14:48.679 --> 01:14:50.210
And eventually they stopped coming.

01:14:50.210 --> 01:14:54.250
And then I remember I was at a party years
later and I introduced myself and then they

01:14:54.250 --> 01:14:56.710
went, "Todd McFarlane, why do I know that
name?"

01:14:56.710 --> 01:14:58.480
And it was somebody I'd never met before.

01:14:58.480 --> 01:15:03.050
"Oh, you're the guy that doesn't like money."

01:15:03.050 --> 01:15:07.460
And let me tell you, and I've told everybody,
look guys, I'm not a saint.

01:15:07.460 --> 01:15:08.460
I'm not a saint.

01:15:08.460 --> 01:15:09.460
I can be bought.

01:15:09.460 --> 01:15:10.520
We all have our price.

01:15:10.520 --> 01:15:15.159
The price though that I needed that day was
that they would give me enough money that

01:15:15.159 --> 01:15:19.670
I would then give up control of the company
but have so much money I could then buy it

01:15:19.670 --> 01:15:25.090
back at a discount, fire all of them, and
I'd still have a profit and I'd have my company

01:15:25.090 --> 01:15:26.090
back.

01:15:26.090 --> 01:15:31.060
But nobody's ever given me that deal so I've
just never sold the company and they've stopped

01:15:31.060 --> 01:15:32.440
saying, "Todd."

01:15:32.440 --> 01:15:33.679
I'm just a madman.

01:15:33.679 --> 01:15:35.760
So anyways, over to Hollywood now.

01:15:35.760 --> 01:15:40.100
Tim Ferriss: Well, before we get to Hollywood,
I want to do a deep dive on the madman part.

01:15:40.100 --> 01:15:45.949
So you mentioned that this three million was
basically all the money you had and then some.

01:15:45.949 --> 01:15:50.060
You strike me as a risk taker, but you strike
me as a calculated risk taker.

01:15:50.060 --> 01:15:54.010
You don't give me the impression of being
reckless.

01:15:54.010 --> 01:15:57.739
Some people could say, well, if you say yes
to Toys"R"Us and you haven't even figured

01:15:57.739 --> 01:15:58.750
out manufacturing, that's reckless.

01:15:58.750 --> 01:16:00.239
But it's not reckless because what's the downside?

01:16:00.239 --> 01:16:02.960
You can't figure it out and you're like, "Sorry
pal, I can't make it work."

01:16:02.960 --> 01:16:05.330
The downside's actually really, really limited
for you.

01:16:05.330 --> 01:16:07.730
Three million bucks, different story.

01:16:07.730 --> 01:16:14.040
So given that the NHL and NBA and so on hadn't
returned your phone calls up to that point,

01:16:14.040 --> 01:16:17.840
what gave you the confidence or how did you
do the calculus?

01:16:17.840 --> 01:16:23.410
Did you have enough safety net that you were
like, "Ah, this could suck for a year or two

01:16:23.410 --> 01:16:26.180
if they don't actually open up to me?"

01:16:26.180 --> 01:16:27.980
How did you think about this?

01:16:27.980 --> 01:16:29.659
What was the reasoning going into it?

01:16:29.659 --> 01:16:33.960
Todd McFarlane: Okay, so there's two things
that I thought were, again, keeping the momentum

01:16:33.960 --> 01:16:35.199
going in my favor.

01:16:35.199 --> 01:16:40.090
One, I had the data of all the toys that I
was doing with Spawn.

01:16:40.090 --> 01:16:45.290
And Spawn at that point, it hit a point where
consecutive series that were coming out, I

01:16:45.290 --> 01:16:50.800
think we got all the way up to 36, was the
second longest running streak of series of

01:16:50.800 --> 01:16:53.780
toys other than I think Transformers.

01:16:53.780 --> 01:16:56.300
It was even longer than Star Wars.

01:16:56.300 --> 01:16:59.650
Star Wars had gone quiet and gone dark for
a while.

01:16:59.650 --> 01:17:01.370
So again, there was a little bit of that.

01:17:01.370 --> 01:17:08.340
But I planted one small seed before I made
the crazy play, and the small seed was I was

01:17:08.340 --> 01:17:13.730
able to get to the players' union of the NHL.

01:17:13.730 --> 01:17:17.719
Because of the four major sports most people
will say, especially in terms of revenue,

01:17:17.719 --> 01:17:23.650
that the NHL is the fourth one, the stepchild,
if you will.

01:17:23.650 --> 01:17:26.150
And so I didn't go to the league because I
knew I couldn't get there.

01:17:26.150 --> 01:17:27.870
I went to the union.

01:17:27.870 --> 01:17:32.850
And just so you know, if you ever make any
sports toys of any current players, you must

01:17:32.850 --> 01:17:33.920
have two licenses.

01:17:33.920 --> 01:17:38.850
You have to license the union so you can get
the players and their likeness, and then you

01:17:38.850 --> 01:17:43.270
have to get a contract from the league so
that you can get basically their uniforms

01:17:43.270 --> 01:17:44.560
and their logos.

01:17:44.560 --> 01:17:48.210
And then you have to glue the two together
and you have a double royalty that you have

01:17:48.210 --> 01:17:49.210
to pay.

01:17:49.210 --> 01:17:52.710
So it's not the best model, but if you want
do it that's how it works.

01:17:52.710 --> 01:18:00.520
So I went to the NHL players' union, which
was like nobody ever gives us any money because

01:18:00.520 --> 01:18:05.489
they just sell jerseys and stuff with logos,
everything's logo, logo, logos on the hat.

01:18:05.489 --> 01:18:10.889
And I said, "I'm going to make toys of your
players but unfortunately I can't put their

01:18:10.889 --> 01:18:13.170
uniforms on them because I don't have an NHL.

01:18:13.170 --> 01:18:17.429
I'm going to have to do generic uniforms."

01:18:17.429 --> 01:18:19.550
And they went, "Yeah, sure, we'll take money."

01:18:19.550 --> 01:18:22.340
So I had in my back pocket one small seed.

01:18:22.340 --> 01:18:24.929
I had the union of the NHL.

01:18:24.929 --> 01:18:31.400
So that later, once I spent the money on the
McGwire ball and I was hoping I would get

01:18:31.400 --> 01:18:36.630
a meeting, then I could go in there and say
— one of the first meetings I took was with

01:18:36.630 --> 01:18:43.719
the NHL per se, and I just pulled out my toys
and I go, "Guys, look at these toys.

01:18:43.719 --> 01:18:44.719
Aren't they cool?

01:18:44.719 --> 01:18:49.400
They look just like Pavel Bure and Mario Lemieux
and Wayne Gretsky.

01:18:49.400 --> 01:18:51.780
Look at them, these are super awesome, right?

01:18:51.780 --> 01:18:55.340
Here's the thing, notice that I don't get
to use your uniform."

01:18:55.340 --> 01:19:01.300
And at that point I sort of had a conversation
with them and they were threatening to sue

01:19:01.300 --> 01:19:05.610
me because they said that my generic uniforms
were looking like them.

01:19:05.610 --> 01:19:11.400
I think I had even done some generic McGwires
too.

01:19:11.400 --> 01:19:18.320
And I'd even at that point then went to the
NFL union and I go, "Hey, I've got the baseball

01:19:18.320 --> 01:19:19.330
and I've now got the hockey."

01:19:19.330 --> 01:19:21.460
Again, it all tumbles.

01:19:21.460 --> 01:19:26.580
And so I was then able to go into rooms and
I remember these odd conversations you have,

01:19:26.580 --> 01:19:30.980
Tim, that I just want to hit my head against
walls, but whatever.

01:19:30.980 --> 01:19:37.730
I'm in a room with the NHL and there's like
six lawyers, there's always lawyers, and they're

01:19:37.730 --> 01:19:44.980
threatening to sue me because I'm doing like
a sort of a generic uniform on one of the

01:19:44.980 --> 01:19:46.500
players that I had there.

01:19:46.500 --> 01:19:49.380
And they're going, "We're going to sue you.

01:19:49.380 --> 01:19:50.880
You cannot do these toys."

01:19:50.880 --> 01:19:52.990
And I'm like, "Oh, yeah?

01:19:52.990 --> 01:19:55.489
You sue my ass.

01:19:55.489 --> 01:19:59.620
You sue my ass, because here's how it works
and you guys aren't even smart enough to know

01:19:59.620 --> 01:20:00.780
how this works.

01:20:00.780 --> 01:20:04.670
Number one, you're sitting here right now
across this table telling me you own everything.

01:20:04.670 --> 01:20:09.929
Good, sue me, and if you win, you don't get
a fucking ounce of upgrade.

01:20:09.929 --> 01:20:14.500
You say you got a hundred percent at the end,
the best you can hope for is a hundred percent.

01:20:14.500 --> 01:20:15.909
"Go ahead and do it.

01:20:15.909 --> 01:20:19.370
But if you pay attention and you do a little
bit of research on me, you're pulling the

01:20:19.370 --> 01:20:22.000
wrong tiger's tail, because I'll do this.

01:20:22.000 --> 01:20:26.980
Because here's the thing, if we go to court
and you find out that you don't have a hundred

01:20:26.980 --> 01:20:33.710
percent, and I'm betting you don't, that not
only do I get a piece of that a hundred percent,

01:20:33.710 --> 01:20:38.250
it is going to basically be a story for every
human being on this planet that they can now

01:20:38.250 --> 01:20:39.640
go against you.

01:20:39.640 --> 01:20:41.380
So you've got two choices.

01:20:41.380 --> 01:20:48.900
You can either go to court and say that somehow
you own reddish colors and bluish colors,

01:20:48.900 --> 01:20:55.340
if you want to make that argument be my guest,
or what I'm saying, I've got a check for half

01:20:55.340 --> 01:20:59.530
a million dollars here I'd like to shove down
your throat to give me your fucking logos.

01:20:59.530 --> 01:21:04.630
I don't know, sue me and potentially lose
a hundred percent or take a half a million

01:21:04.630 --> 01:21:05.630
dollars.

01:21:05.630 --> 01:21:09.820
This seems like my six-year-old could figure
out what this thing is."

01:21:09.820 --> 01:21:12.179
Eventually they took the half a million bucks.

01:21:12.179 --> 01:21:15.110
So what are you talking about, of course they
did.

01:21:15.110 --> 01:21:17.560
Of course they did.

01:21:17.560 --> 01:21:18.560
Stop it.

01:21:18.560 --> 01:21:21.360
And sadly I had to sort of play the same conversation
with everybody.

01:21:21.360 --> 01:21:23.989
When I had the NFL and I'm like, "I'm doing
your players.

01:21:23.989 --> 01:21:26.820
The question is, do you want any of the cash?"

01:21:26.820 --> 01:21:32.020
I mean at some point forget the art and forget
anything else, just get down to the core of

01:21:32.020 --> 01:21:33.020
it.

01:21:33.020 --> 01:21:34.570
"Do you want any of this money I'm about to
make?

01:21:34.570 --> 01:21:36.429
The answer is yes or no.

01:21:36.429 --> 01:21:37.429
And if you don't, fine.

01:21:37.429 --> 01:21:39.880
Oh, by the way, I thought you were in the
commerce business because you're a public

01:21:39.880 --> 01:21:42.790
company, but you guys go ahead and do what
you want to do."

01:21:42.790 --> 01:21:47.790
It is amazing how many times I've had to try
and shove money down people's throats.

01:21:47.790 --> 01:21:50.590
But anyways, that's corporate America.

01:21:50.590 --> 01:21:52.610
They do their thing, I do mine.

01:21:52.610 --> 01:22:00.900
Tim Ferriss: So doing your thing, let's hop
across some lily pads and touch on, whichever

01:22:00.900 --> 01:22:08.580
you want touch on next, the music, TV, movies,
how do any of these other elements, I know

01:22:08.580 --> 01:22:13.170
you've mentioned games, but which of these
elements enter the picture?

01:22:13.170 --> 01:22:15.920
Todd McFarlane: So let's talk about TV first.

01:22:15.920 --> 01:22:22.520
So again, go back to the release of the Spawn
comic book after we leave Marvel, the group

01:22:22.520 --> 01:22:28.239
of us leave Marvel, my character Spawn comes
out, bam, shoots to the top of the charts.

01:22:28.239 --> 01:22:31.640
And for months and months, even maybe a couple
of years it was the number one selling book.

01:22:31.640 --> 01:22:33.010
So it was at the top of the chart.

01:22:33.010 --> 01:22:36.060
So like I said, phone calls were constantly
coming.

01:22:36.060 --> 01:22:41.930
I get the phone call and on the other line
is HBO, legit at that point.

01:22:41.930 --> 01:22:43.800
I mean, what are you talking about, they're
still legit, right?

01:22:43.800 --> 01:22:46.449
But they were one of the big sort of companies
out there.

01:22:46.449 --> 01:22:48.110
And I go, "Oh, cool."

01:22:48.110 --> 01:22:50.640
And they said, "Hey, we want to do some animation."

01:22:50.640 --> 01:22:56.590
And I knew animation is like Saturday morning
cartoons and a couple of my partners at Image

01:22:56.590 --> 01:22:59.810
Comics had sold and were going to get Saturday
morning cartoons, which they did.

01:22:59.810 --> 01:23:06.219
But I did Spawn, again, the guy from the pit
of Hell, it just not wasn't what I wanted.

01:23:06.219 --> 01:23:10.910
And I don't say this as a joke, and I'll tell
you why I asked this one question.

01:23:10.910 --> 01:23:13.050
I go, "Hey, guys, it's cool.

01:23:13.050 --> 01:23:14.530
I appreciate your enthusiasm.

01:23:14.530 --> 01:23:15.730
Let me ask you just one question.

01:23:15.730 --> 01:23:17.870
I just have one question for you.

01:23:17.870 --> 01:23:21.520
Can I say 'Fuck' in my show?"

01:23:21.520 --> 01:23:23.830
And it wasn't meant to be a joke.

01:23:23.830 --> 01:23:30.870
And they paused and then they said, "Yeah,
probably, why not?"

01:23:30.870 --> 01:23:32.630
And that was all I needed to hear.

01:23:32.630 --> 01:23:38.179
Not that I wanted to use that word, Tim, not
that I wanted to do and show naked women or

01:23:38.179 --> 01:23:39.179
anything.

01:23:39.179 --> 01:23:46.409
I just thought that if they were willing to
basically say yes to that, then I had a wide

01:23:46.409 --> 01:23:48.489
lane of creativity.

01:23:48.489 --> 01:23:56.810
And the reason I needed a wide lane, because
my character lives in a dark, serious world

01:23:56.810 --> 01:24:00.670
and I'm going to have corrupt policemen and
I'm going to have mafia people and I'm going

01:24:00.670 --> 01:24:02.590
to have criminals, I'm going to have drug
addicts.

01:24:02.590 --> 01:24:08.020
And these people don't talk politely when
they get mad.

01:24:08.020 --> 01:24:11.370
You have to be able to go into watching an
R-rated movie.

01:24:11.370 --> 01:24:15.490
You have to be able to have a certain dialect
for your characters that matches who they

01:24:15.490 --> 01:24:16.719
are.

01:24:16.719 --> 01:24:21.889
And so I just needed to, if the moment came,
I needed to be able to do that and have the

01:24:21.889 --> 01:24:23.969
creative freedom to do it.

01:24:23.969 --> 01:24:30.510
So when they said, "Yeah, sure," then I was
like, "Cool, then let's do it.

01:24:30.510 --> 01:24:32.500
Oh, one more thing.

01:24:32.500 --> 01:24:39.050
I know nothing about animation, never spent
a second on it, but I've got to be in charge,

01:24:39.050 --> 01:24:44.880
so you guys go and have that talk and do what
you've got to do."

01:24:44.880 --> 01:24:48.520
And they came back and said, "Yeah, yeah,
yeah, you can be in charge but we're going

01:24:48.520 --> 01:24:55.100
to put some good people on your flanks," which
they did, and we went in there.

01:24:55.100 --> 01:25:00.070
And so I was working on HBO animation.

01:25:00.070 --> 01:25:05.160
At this time, Tim, HBO had started an animation
division.

01:25:05.160 --> 01:25:11.380
I think there was five, maybe six shows going
on, of which Spawn was one.

01:25:11.380 --> 01:25:18.750
Very quickly, the other five failed and I
was sort of the lone wolf by the end of it.

01:25:18.750 --> 01:25:26.100
But when we first came out, I was in the room
now with this group of people they had amassed

01:25:26.100 --> 01:25:32.180
to make an animated show, who collectively
probably had 600 years of experience, which

01:25:32.180 --> 01:25:33.180
is cool.

01:25:33.180 --> 01:25:34.180
But there's also moments where I realized
I'm in front of people at times who have been

01:25:34.180 --> 01:25:35.180
institutionalized.

01:25:35.180 --> 01:25:41.050
And here's the frustrating thing that I've
run into over and over and over in my life.

01:25:41.050 --> 01:25:43.900
The bark being way better than the bite.

01:25:43.900 --> 01:25:48.760
And so artists would go, all these creative
people, all these people who worked on animation,

01:25:48.760 --> 01:25:52.369
"Man, if we could only do it our way, man,
these people, these executives, they don't

01:25:52.369 --> 01:25:53.369
know anything."

01:25:53.369 --> 01:25:54.970
I've heard that over and over and over.

01:25:54.970 --> 01:26:00.239
And we finally get to a point where literally
we can almost do anything, and guess what

01:26:00.239 --> 01:26:01.570
a lot of them did?

01:26:01.570 --> 01:26:03.810
They just went back to status quo.

01:26:03.810 --> 01:26:06.469
They just went back to status quo.

01:26:06.469 --> 01:26:12.540
And so being the dumbest guy in the room,
I used to ask these questions.

01:26:12.540 --> 01:26:15.510
Any question, there's a legitimate answer
why you can't do it.

01:26:15.510 --> 01:26:17.700
It's usually time or money.

01:26:17.700 --> 01:26:20.130
"Oh, you can do it but it's going to cost
more money."

01:26:20.130 --> 01:26:21.540
"Can't do that, I have a budget."

01:26:21.540 --> 01:26:25.489
"No, it doesn't cost any more money, but it's
going to take twice as long."

01:26:25.489 --> 01:26:27.520
"We don't have the luxury of the deadline."

01:26:27.520 --> 01:26:29.580
So that's it.

01:26:29.580 --> 01:26:30.580
And I understand.

01:26:30.580 --> 01:26:33.790
So I would constantly be asking these questions,
"Can we do this?"

01:26:33.790 --> 01:26:34.790
"No."

01:26:34.790 --> 01:26:35.790
"Why?"

01:26:35.790 --> 01:26:36.790
"Because of this."

01:26:36.790 --> 01:26:41.360
But every now and then, being the dumb guy
in the room I didn't know where the guardrails

01:26:41.360 --> 01:26:47.020
were so I'd ask a question and then you get
the moment, Tim, which is you ask a question

01:26:47.020 --> 01:26:52.140
and you've got 50 people in a room, and I
go, "If we do this will it cost us any more

01:26:52.140 --> 01:26:53.140
time or money?

01:26:53.140 --> 01:26:56.870
I mean a little bit of effort maybe, but about
the same."

01:26:56.870 --> 01:27:03.020
And then you get, to me, the golden moment,
a pregnant pause, silence.

01:27:03.020 --> 01:27:09.739
So there was nobody in the room with 600 years
experience saying, "No, we can't do that.

01:27:09.739 --> 01:27:10.739
No, we can't do that."

01:27:10.739 --> 01:27:16.370
And I went, "So you're saying that it won't
take more time?"

01:27:16.370 --> 01:27:17.370
"No."

01:27:17.370 --> 01:27:18.370
"And it won't cost more money?"

01:27:18.370 --> 01:27:19.370
"No."

01:27:19.370 --> 01:27:20.370
"So why don't we do it?"

01:27:20.370 --> 01:27:21.370
"Well, that's not how we do it here."

01:27:21.370 --> 01:27:22.830
"No, no, I understand that's not how we do
it.

01:27:22.830 --> 01:27:24.300
I'm not asking that question.

01:27:24.300 --> 01:27:27.780
I'm saying will it take more time or money
and you're saying no."

01:27:27.780 --> 01:27:35.300
Why are we figuring out how to self edit ourself,
self censor ourself, when nobody else is?

01:27:35.300 --> 01:27:37.050
This is a crime.

01:27:37.050 --> 01:27:39.510
Why are we putting guardrails on ourselves?

01:27:39.510 --> 01:27:44.820
We should be smashing up against the walls
until the executives upstairs go, "Whoa guys,

01:27:44.820 --> 01:27:47.460
whoa, whoa, whoa, pull it back."

01:27:47.460 --> 01:27:48.460
Right?

01:27:48.460 --> 01:27:49.550
That's what we should be doing.

01:27:49.550 --> 01:27:56.280
But when you've had a dog that has been in
a small backyard for so long that it gets

01:27:56.280 --> 01:28:02.989
comfortable, that the moment you throw the
doors open to the wild farmlands and you go,

01:28:02.989 --> 01:28:04.650
"Run, Rover, go."

01:28:04.650 --> 01:28:10.679
It was amazing how many creative people sat
down instead of running because that's all

01:28:10.679 --> 01:28:11.679
they knew.

01:28:11.679 --> 01:28:16.300
They knew one way to do animation and they
knew one way to draw it and they knew one

01:28:16.300 --> 01:28:17.760
way to storytell.

01:28:17.760 --> 01:28:21.199
And they only knew one way and one way and
one way and one way.

01:28:21.199 --> 01:28:27.630
And here's the frustration, by the end of
the three years we were butting heads because

01:28:27.630 --> 01:28:30.480
they all thought I was the crazy guy and whatever
else.

01:28:30.480 --> 01:28:39.639
We won Emmys on that show, and I remember
being up on the stage and getting my Emmy

01:28:39.639 --> 01:28:46.179
and behind me was 15 of these people, most
of them I was constantly having to basically

01:28:46.179 --> 01:28:50.040
drag them into some of these decisions.

01:28:50.040 --> 01:28:54.400
And they were all holding an Emmy in their
hands too and with a big smile.

01:28:54.400 --> 01:29:00.619
And I was like going, "Oh, my God, here you
are, so happy you've got an Emmy in your hand,

01:29:00.619 --> 01:29:05.510
and you were resistant to almost everything;
that is the reason why we're up on this stage

01:29:05.510 --> 01:29:06.510
today."

01:29:06.510 --> 01:29:11.889
It bugged me that I was putting that award
in their hands because they were not willing

01:29:11.889 --> 01:29:15.190
participants for the ride.

01:29:15.190 --> 01:29:20.409
But the reason we ended up getting canceled
wasn't because people weren't watching it.

01:29:20.409 --> 01:29:27.040
It was because, and this is again just the
way of the world, name's Todd, it only rhymes

01:29:27.040 --> 01:29:29.560
with God, I don't get to control any of this.

01:29:29.560 --> 01:29:35.449
And it was that they had five shows, remember
I said they started an animation show, and

01:29:35.449 --> 01:29:40.780
every time a show got canceled, one of the
animations got canceled, they just put all

01:29:40.780 --> 01:29:43.470
the costs on the existing show.

01:29:43.470 --> 01:29:48.300
And eventually all of them failed except for
me.

01:29:48.300 --> 01:29:53.200
And I remember them calling me in saying,
"Todd, we've got to stop the animation."

01:29:53.200 --> 01:29:54.200
I go, "Why?"

01:29:54.200 --> 01:29:55.200
And they go, "It's getting too expensive."

01:29:55.200 --> 01:29:56.730
And I went, "Expensive?

01:29:56.730 --> 01:30:00.480
You haven't given me one red cent more than
you did the first year.

01:30:00.480 --> 01:30:02.780
I'm working on the exact same budget."

01:30:02.780 --> 01:30:09.000
And then I found out I was covering all the
other overhead that had basically failed.

01:30:09.000 --> 01:30:10.280
Tim Ferriss: Accrued, yeah.

01:30:10.280 --> 01:30:16.219
Todd McFarlane: And so they put that on my
budget and then they have to get so many eyeballs

01:30:16.219 --> 01:30:22.150
per dollar and somehow they had put all that
money that had accrued onto mine and it fell

01:30:22.150 --> 01:30:26.960
under their threshold and whatever matrix
they used, and they'd never go public with

01:30:26.960 --> 01:30:28.880
any of it but it was like that was it.

01:30:28.880 --> 01:30:33.750
We were doing the exact same job, winning
Emmys, doing exactly what we were doing, but

01:30:33.750 --> 01:30:37.790
now all of a sudden they had to let us go
because somehow the math didn't work for them.

01:30:37.790 --> 01:30:40.320
Tim Ferriss: So a question for you about that.

01:30:40.320 --> 01:30:49.409
It seems like, and I know this is simplifying,
but you've had this incredible bull run with

01:30:49.409 --> 01:30:55.699
Spider-Man, Image Toys and then you have this
television experience, which is super challenging,

01:30:55.699 --> 01:30:59.760
it doesn't end the way you probably would've
liked it to end.

01:30:59.760 --> 01:31:02.080
Todd McFarlane: Well, we won Emmys, so I'll
take that —

01:31:02.080 --> 01:31:03.080
Tim Ferriss: Okay, no, no.

01:31:03.080 --> 01:31:07.630
Well, that leads into my question, which is
are you glad you had that experience or would

01:31:07.630 --> 01:31:11.360
you have removed it and replaced it with something
else?

01:31:11.360 --> 01:31:12.360
Todd McFarlane: No.

01:31:12.360 --> 01:31:14.050
Tim Ferriss: How did you contend with that
yourself?

01:31:14.050 --> 01:31:15.340
Did you feel good about it at the end?

01:31:15.340 --> 01:31:16.340
Todd McFarlane: Yeah, sure.

01:31:16.340 --> 01:31:17.340
Tim Ferriss: Proud of it?

01:31:17.340 --> 01:31:18.340
Todd McFarlane: Yeah.

01:31:18.340 --> 01:31:19.340
Tim Ferriss: Okay.

01:31:19.340 --> 01:31:20.340
Todd McFarlane: Tim, this is easy.

01:31:20.340 --> 01:31:23.880
There's only one human being I have dominion
over: me.

01:31:23.880 --> 01:31:25.030
Period.

01:31:25.030 --> 01:31:28.139
My wife, I love her, I've been with her for
over 40 years.

01:31:28.139 --> 01:31:33.710
I don't even control her and she's the closest
person that I have in the world.

01:31:33.710 --> 01:31:38.280
Okay, I don't worry — I mean, it can be
exhausting at times trying to drag people

01:31:38.280 --> 01:31:41.300
to the water but you can't make the horse
drink at times.

01:31:41.300 --> 01:31:46.300
So at some point I just go, "Guys, you do
your thing.

01:31:46.300 --> 01:31:48.730
I do my thing and we'll just figure it out."

01:31:48.730 --> 01:31:53.449
I do not stand by water coolers and complain
about bosses.

01:31:53.449 --> 01:31:56.219
I quit jobs and start my own companies.

01:31:56.219 --> 01:32:03.071
As a creative person, if I'm going to complain
about how the business works, then I will

01:32:03.071 --> 01:32:05.949
then go and become my own businessman.

01:32:05.949 --> 01:32:09.010
I consider myself, Tim, to be bilingual.

01:32:09.010 --> 01:32:12.450
I have learned the language of business.

01:32:12.450 --> 01:32:18.300
I am first and foremost a fluent speaker in
art, that is my native tongue.

01:32:18.300 --> 01:32:25.820
But I have learned business to the point where
I can now go into a meeting with executives

01:32:25.820 --> 01:32:34.010
or bankers or government people or whatever
and sound like a CEO who happens to be a pretty

01:32:34.010 --> 01:32:35.070
good artist.

01:32:35.070 --> 01:32:40.650
I can convince them that I'm that guy, that
they go, "Oh, okay, the art is sort of like

01:32:40.650 --> 01:32:42.929
he just does it on the side."

01:32:42.929 --> 01:32:46.889
Because I've got two choices.

01:32:46.889 --> 01:32:53.110
I either let people with experience do the
business side of it and have full trust in

01:32:53.110 --> 01:32:58.139
them and knowledge of what they're doing so
I know that I can trust them or I just figure

01:32:58.139 --> 01:32:59.139
it out myself.

01:32:59.139 --> 01:33:02.110
My personality didn't allow me to put it in
the hands of others.

01:33:02.110 --> 01:33:04.489
If I'm going to fail, I want to fail on my
own merits.

01:33:04.489 --> 01:33:07.210
And so I just learned business.

01:33:07.210 --> 01:33:10.060
Did it take away from the art?

01:33:10.060 --> 01:33:11.100
Of course it did.

01:33:11.100 --> 01:33:16.100
Every time I'm in one of these dumb meetings
for an hour, I am acutely aware that I am

01:33:16.100 --> 01:33:24.030
not creating and everything in my world business-wise
is a byproduct of me doing some kind of creating.

01:33:24.030 --> 01:33:25.790
It's a fallout of my me creating.

01:33:25.790 --> 01:33:30.850
And when I'm not creating, I don't think I'm
bringing the most value to my company.

01:33:30.850 --> 01:33:33.860
I need to be an artist as much as possible.

01:33:33.860 --> 01:33:41.940
But I also need to be able to sing and tell
the story of what we do for the company so

01:33:41.940 --> 01:33:49.800
that we can get past the nos, because I can
be very stubborn of going, "Well, before you

01:33:49.800 --> 01:33:55.400
say no, let me just download you with the
reality of what you're walking away from."

01:33:55.400 --> 01:33:59.290
And at the beginning it was all BS, let me
just tell you, it was BS.

01:33:59.290 --> 01:34:02.290
But as the years have gone by, there's hardcore
data now.

01:34:02.290 --> 01:34:05.449
Tim, let me just again go back a little bit.

01:34:05.449 --> 01:34:10.080
Last year, I don't even pay attention to it,
but last year — there's the equivalent of

01:34:10.080 --> 01:34:16.800
the Nielsen ratings in toys and they literally
count when something gets scanned at the big

01:34:16.800 --> 01:34:19.550
superstores and it goes into a database.

01:34:19.550 --> 01:34:23.409
So we're not talking about "You're the most
popular, you've got the coolest toy, Todd,"

01:34:23.409 --> 01:34:25.100
those are different awards.

01:34:25.100 --> 01:34:29.679
This is data based on sales.

01:34:29.679 --> 01:34:36.800
Last year, our line of DC Multiverse toys,
I don't even have the master license of DC

01:34:36.800 --> 01:34:38.310
Multiverse, I have a fraction of it.

01:34:38.310 --> 01:34:40.850
I have a collective little sliver of it.

01:34:40.850 --> 01:34:47.830
And our sliver of that outsold not only the
master toy license of that, but it outsold

01:34:47.830 --> 01:34:51.429
everything Marvel and everything Star Wars.

01:34:51.429 --> 01:34:56.010
It was the number one selling line in America
and Canada.

01:34:56.010 --> 01:35:04.140
My company literally that is a handful of
people sold more toys in action figure lines

01:35:04.140 --> 01:35:10.860
than Hasbro, Mattel, Playmates, Spin Master,
all these public companies.

01:35:10.860 --> 01:35:18.770
And the question is either I and my people
are geniuses or they're not getting full value

01:35:18.770 --> 01:35:20.710
for whatever infrastructure they did.

01:35:20.710 --> 01:35:24.040
I think the answer lies somewhere in between.

01:35:24.040 --> 01:35:29.250
How is it that I can take on Fortune 500 companies?

01:35:29.250 --> 01:35:33.650
Last year I had the number one selling comic
book, the number one selling comic book.

01:35:33.650 --> 01:35:34.900
Marvel is owned by Disney.

01:35:34.900 --> 01:35:42.250
DC Comics is owned by Warner Bros., AT&amp;T Warner
Bros., Discovery, whatever it is this week.

01:35:42.250 --> 01:35:44.490
Those are billion dollar empires.

01:35:44.490 --> 01:35:47.980
And my comic book division is four people.

01:35:47.980 --> 01:35:55.190
How do four people take on billion dollar
empires and beat them at times at their own

01:35:55.190 --> 01:35:56.190
game?

01:35:56.190 --> 01:35:57.190
I don't know.

01:35:57.190 --> 01:35:58.190
I don't know.

01:35:58.190 --> 01:35:59.190
It's a curiosity.

01:35:59.190 --> 01:36:00.190
Why aren't they selling more?

01:36:00.190 --> 01:36:01.190
I don't know.

01:36:01.190 --> 01:36:02.190
Go ask them.

01:36:02.190 --> 01:36:03.190
I'm just doing my gig.

01:36:03.190 --> 01:36:04.190
But it's doable.

01:36:04.190 --> 01:36:11.010
And I'm hoping that the story of a guy like
me should be, hopefully to some people, somewhat

01:36:11.010 --> 01:36:12.469
inspirational.

01:36:12.469 --> 01:36:17.730
Because every time I read a story where David
took on Goliath and didn't get killed, didn't

01:36:17.730 --> 01:36:26.670
slay the giant, just didn't get killed himself,
those should be encouraging of the yes, you

01:36:26.670 --> 01:36:29.909
too can go against these guys and succeed.

01:36:29.909 --> 01:36:35.640
And here's the thing, as long as your company
is the right size, with the right overhead,

01:36:35.640 --> 01:36:37.260
you can make a profit.

01:36:37.260 --> 01:36:43.760
So you can make $10,000 a month and make a
profit as long as your overhead is less than

01:36:43.760 --> 01:36:44.810
10,000.

01:36:44.810 --> 01:36:52.080
Or you can make a hundred million a year and
lose money because your overhead is over a

01:36:52.080 --> 01:36:53.760
hundred million.

01:36:53.760 --> 01:36:57.320
You just have to right size your company and
you can make money.

01:36:57.320 --> 01:36:58.920
Don't worry about being rich.

01:36:58.920 --> 01:36:59.989
Just go.

01:36:59.989 --> 01:37:04.230
I make 30,000; as long as we spend 25, we're
making five grand a month.

01:37:04.230 --> 01:37:05.230
It's a living.

01:37:05.230 --> 01:37:06.230
It's a living.

01:37:06.230 --> 01:37:07.230
Cool.

01:37:07.230 --> 01:37:08.230
And you're having fun doing it.

01:37:08.230 --> 01:37:12.909
Tim Ferriss: Two questions related to all
of this, the first is, and we're going to

01:37:12.909 --> 01:37:21.900
bounce around chronologically, but how do
you protect or create the time for creativity

01:37:21.900 --> 01:37:22.900
and making?

01:37:22.900 --> 01:37:27.469
So if we think about it as making versus managing,
so the making is the creative stuff, the managing

01:37:27.469 --> 01:37:31.780
is all the meetings, all of the CEO stuff.

01:37:31.780 --> 01:37:35.200
I would have to imagine there have been times
where you've looked at your calendar after

01:37:35.200 --> 01:37:40.590
a hard week and you're like, "For fuck's sake,
I've spent 90 plus maybe a hundred percent

01:37:40.590 --> 01:37:43.860
of my time this week managing stuff."

01:37:43.860 --> 01:37:49.360
How have you been able to block out or protect
the time to create?

01:37:49.360 --> 01:37:52.980
Have you thought about that as someone who
has all these different business ventures

01:37:52.980 --> 01:37:53.980
now?

01:37:53.980 --> 01:37:57.090
Todd McFarlane: I think it's the eternal question
we all struggle with.

01:37:57.090 --> 01:37:58.830
How do we balance our life?

01:37:58.830 --> 01:38:01.840
We all ask that, both on the personal and
the work.

01:38:01.840 --> 01:38:05.840
We're always going how does this work?

01:38:05.840 --> 01:38:15.790
I, through failures, Tim, found that I was
a five-ball juggler.

01:38:15.790 --> 01:38:20.590
And here's what that means, that I don't care
who you are.

01:38:20.590 --> 01:38:25.599
I don't care if you're the best juggler in
Vegas and you're with Cirque du Soleil and

01:38:25.599 --> 01:38:28.060
you can juggle 25 balls.

01:38:28.060 --> 01:38:34.550
Even that person, if you go one ball more
than they're capable of, guess what happens?

01:38:34.550 --> 01:38:36.580
All the balls fall.

01:38:36.580 --> 01:38:40.640
Every ball you're juggling hits the floor
and it's a mess.

01:38:40.640 --> 01:38:51.010
And so I, unfortunately, let the balls drop
at times that I was acutely aware that I was

01:38:51.010 --> 01:38:54.119
doing a disservice to certain areas.

01:38:54.119 --> 01:38:58.820
I learned that I was a five-ball juggler,
and here's what that means for me.

01:38:58.820 --> 01:39:02.540
It means that I'm always constantly juggling
five balls.

01:39:02.540 --> 01:39:07.590
But if another opportunity comes along and
I get excited about it and it makes sense

01:39:07.590 --> 01:39:13.050
both from an artistic and from a business
CEO level, I must put one of the five balls

01:39:13.050 --> 01:39:20.719
I am currently juggling down or put it in
the hands of others and give them full trust

01:39:20.719 --> 01:39:26.310
to do what they need to do before I pick up
that new opportunity so that I'm constantly

01:39:26.310 --> 01:39:30.929
at five and don't extend myself.

01:39:30.929 --> 01:39:36.360
And so it was a lesson hard learned over the
years.

01:39:36.360 --> 01:39:43.620
What that also meant was, "Todd, you can't
do all the artwork yourself, so you're going

01:39:43.620 --> 01:39:47.520
to have to become a good scout and you're
going to have to find talent."

01:39:47.520 --> 01:39:56.409
At some point on a good day, I feel that I
am Phil Jackson when he was coaching the Chicago

01:39:56.409 --> 01:39:57.409
Bulls.

01:39:57.409 --> 01:40:04.780
I think, maybe not, his conversation went
something like this, "Michael Jordan, Scottie

01:40:04.780 --> 01:40:10.040
Pippen, and I don't even know what the name
is of the rest of you dudes, could you guys

01:40:10.040 --> 01:40:13.960
just go over there and your job is, could
you put that ball in that bucket?

01:40:13.960 --> 01:40:19.739
Oh, by the way, if you have any questions
to that in-depth analysis I just gave you,

01:40:19.739 --> 01:40:26.290
I'll be over here in my three-piece suit with
my clipboard and you just come over and ask

01:40:26.290 --> 01:40:27.290
me a question."

01:40:27.290 --> 01:40:33.760
Phil Jackson was smart enough to find the
proper talent and put it in the proper place

01:40:33.760 --> 01:40:40.510
so that the end of that run, not only did
Michael Jordan have six championship rings,

01:40:40.510 --> 01:40:41.510
so did Phil.

01:40:41.510 --> 01:40:45.260
Do you know how many buckets Phil made during
that time?

01:40:45.260 --> 01:40:46.260
Zero.

01:40:46.260 --> 01:40:52.480
He was just smart enough to know to put Michael
and the crew out there in the right iterations.

01:40:52.480 --> 01:40:59.409
And so there are times where you have to just
go, "Todd, you can't build this all yourself."

01:40:59.409 --> 01:41:03.679
There are some things that make sense for
me being the lead and/or even isolating myself

01:41:03.679 --> 01:41:04.830
artistically.

01:41:04.830 --> 01:41:08.920
There are plenty of other things we do, making
toys, making comic book, making movies, making

01:41:08.920 --> 01:41:12.550
video games, all of it, that's a collective
team.

01:41:12.550 --> 01:41:18.520
I actually sort of enjoy being with the collective
team because if I can find my Michael Jordans

01:41:18.520 --> 01:41:25.110
or my Scottie Pippens and I'm in a meeting
and they show me their skill, artistically

01:41:25.110 --> 01:41:30.260
or with ideas or how to promote or how to
sell or market, whatever, in any field in

01:41:30.260 --> 01:41:36.840
any sort of skill set, and I feel like I'm
the dumbest guy in the room, those are great

01:41:36.840 --> 01:41:42.020
days for me, Tim, because I sit there and
go, "Oh, my gosh, I wouldn't have even thought

01:41:42.020 --> 01:41:43.020
of that.

01:41:43.020 --> 01:41:44.989
I can't even do it to that skill set.

01:41:44.989 --> 01:41:48.219
Thank God they work for me."

01:41:48.219 --> 01:41:50.900
I just sit there and go, "Hmm."

01:41:50.900 --> 01:41:56.340
If I really want to make it look like that
I'm sort of more important that I am, I'll

01:41:56.340 --> 01:42:01.260
say something like, "Interesting, I'll take
it under advisement and get back to everybody

01:42:01.260 --> 01:42:04.570
tomorrow," where inside I'm going, "Holy shit,
this is awesome.

01:42:04.570 --> 01:42:08.400
I just hope they don't quit and start their
own company and compete against me because

01:42:08.400 --> 01:42:09.440
they're super awesome."

01:42:09.440 --> 01:42:10.510
And so that's it.

01:42:10.510 --> 01:42:12.840
You just have to find talent and coach them
along the way.

01:42:12.840 --> 01:42:13.840
I'm just a coach.

01:42:13.840 --> 01:42:15.320
I feel like I'm a coach now.

01:42:15.320 --> 01:42:21.350
Tim Ferriss: Harkening back to what you said
about last year and toy sales, the example

01:42:21.350 --> 01:42:25.179
you gave was DC, so we're not talking about
Freddy Krueger, we're talking about DC.

01:42:25.179 --> 01:42:27.230
It's not the R-rated advantage.

01:42:27.230 --> 01:42:29.090
Presumably, these are at large retailers.

01:42:29.090 --> 01:42:30.699
Todd McFarlane: Oh, yeah.

01:42:30.699 --> 01:42:37.139
Tim Ferriss: You no longer have the Hot Topic
advantage.

01:42:37.139 --> 01:42:40.119
How are you competing so effectively?

01:42:40.119 --> 01:42:44.739
I would imagine also the people who have emulated
and tried to copy the style by adding more

01:42:44.739 --> 01:42:46.679
detail and trying to compete.

01:42:46.679 --> 01:42:47.679
I assume that.

01:42:47.679 --> 01:42:51.349
Maybe that's an incorrect assumption, but
how are you guys still competing so effectively

01:42:51.349 --> 01:42:57.159
in selling, certainly as multiple of your
head counts, so much more than these other

01:42:57.159 --> 01:42:58.159
guys?

01:42:58.159 --> 01:42:59.159
Todd McFarlane: Okay.

01:42:59.159 --> 01:43:00.159
Well, let me just tell you, brand matters,
right?

01:43:00.159 --> 01:43:01.159
Tim Ferriss: Mm-hmm.

01:43:01.159 --> 01:43:06.469
Todd McFarlane: I'd been making toys for 25
years that I said every day of my life, "I

01:43:06.469 --> 01:43:08.460
can sell Star Wars, just give me the contract.

01:43:08.460 --> 01:43:09.460
What are you talking about?

01:43:09.460 --> 01:43:10.619
It's Star Wars, right?

01:43:10.619 --> 01:43:11.770
What are you talking about?

01:43:11.770 --> 01:43:12.770
Star Wars."

01:43:12.770 --> 01:43:14.239
How come I don't sell a lot of Spider-Man?

01:43:14.239 --> 01:43:16.340
Because I don't have the contract.

01:43:16.340 --> 01:43:21.150
Eventually, I'd walk around Toy Fair and it
seemed like everybody had a piece of Marvel.

01:43:21.150 --> 01:43:25.570
Everybody had a piece of Star Wars and DC
Comics, except for me.

01:43:25.570 --> 01:43:28.650
I don't know why I was the only guy.

01:43:28.650 --> 01:43:34.480
But somehow, we ended up doing a deal, Warner
Bros. walking away from Mattel.

01:43:34.480 --> 01:43:39.330
They had given Mattel both the Master Toy
license and the Collective sliver.

01:43:39.330 --> 01:43:42.239
I guess it didn't —
Tim Ferriss: What is the difference between

01:43:42.239 --> 01:43:44.530
the Master Toy and the Collective?

01:43:44.530 --> 01:43:47.480
I guess I'm not sure what the difference is
between those two.

01:43:47.480 --> 01:43:48.590
Would you mind just explaining that?

01:43:48.590 --> 01:43:52.710
Todd McFarlane: Well, those definitions are
internal definitions for every corporation.

01:43:52.710 --> 01:43:54.680
They define them differently.

01:43:54.680 --> 01:43:59.610
But essentially, a Master Toy license means
that you basically, not only get to do action

01:43:59.610 --> 01:44:04.360
figures, but you get to do the Nerf gun versions
of them and you get to do the Hot Wheels with

01:44:04.360 --> 01:44:05.510
the logos on it.

01:44:05.510 --> 01:44:06.510
Tim Ferriss: Right.

01:44:06.510 --> 01:44:10.211
Todd McFarlane: Basically, you can do puzzles,
you're in the entire store, if you want to

01:44:10.211 --> 01:44:11.211
be.

01:44:11.211 --> 01:44:12.320
You've got all the aisles.

01:44:12.320 --> 01:44:14.810
I'm in aisle five, boys action figure.

01:44:14.810 --> 01:44:16.730
I always said, "No, I don't want to diversify.

01:44:16.730 --> 01:44:20.120
I want to just be the king of aisle five,"
if I could.

01:44:20.120 --> 01:44:21.120
That's it.

01:44:21.120 --> 01:44:22.270
Just focus.

01:44:22.270 --> 01:44:24.050
Don't get lost in all the other aisles.

01:44:24.050 --> 01:44:26.179
Just be good at one aisle.

01:44:26.179 --> 01:44:29.110
I'd rather be a master of one than the jack
of all trades.

01:44:29.110 --> 01:44:35.480
Eventually, we ended up, a couple years back,
getting, after decades at trying, finally,

01:44:35.480 --> 01:44:38.679
we got the license for the DC Multiverse.

01:44:38.679 --> 01:44:42.090
Let me tell you really what the big piece
of that was.

01:44:42.090 --> 01:44:44.409
I had the word "Batman" and "Superman" on
my toys.

01:44:44.409 --> 01:44:46.500
What are you talking about?

01:44:46.500 --> 01:44:49.719
Those are global brands.

01:44:49.719 --> 01:44:55.360
I've always told people, "Look," and you and
I have discussed it here, "If you do a quality

01:44:55.360 --> 01:45:02.290
product at a fair price and do little bit
of marketing and build a better mouse trap,

01:45:02.290 --> 01:45:03.290
you can survive."

01:45:03.290 --> 01:45:04.290
How do I know?

01:45:04.290 --> 01:45:07.489
I've done it for 25, 27 years prior.

01:45:07.489 --> 01:45:09.260
But I always had a but to it.

01:45:09.260 --> 01:45:15.780
But if you ever give me a triple A brand on
top of that, on top of that mythology, shit,

01:45:15.780 --> 01:45:16.780
get out of the way.

01:45:16.780 --> 01:45:17.780
What do you talk about?

01:45:17.780 --> 01:45:23.750
Because now I'm going to give them price and
quality and a name that everybody knows.

01:45:23.750 --> 01:45:25.489
I've been saying that for years, and guess
what?

01:45:25.489 --> 01:45:27.860
They finally give me one of the big brands,
and guess what?

01:45:27.860 --> 01:45:29.810
We outsold all of them.

01:45:29.810 --> 01:45:30.810
Boom.

01:45:30.810 --> 01:45:34.380
Now, here's one of the things, also, that
mattered.

01:45:34.380 --> 01:45:39.520
Big companies, and this is their job, I'm
not saying it's good, bad, I'm just saying

01:45:39.520 --> 01:45:43.020
this is their job, is to maximize value for
the shareholders every 90 days.

01:45:43.020 --> 01:45:44.020
How do you do that?

01:45:44.020 --> 01:45:45.610
You just make as much money as you can.

01:45:45.610 --> 01:45:47.099
How do you make as much money as you can?

01:45:47.099 --> 01:45:49.260
You figure out how to cut corners.

01:45:49.260 --> 01:45:57.940
And so one of the ways you cut corners in
our trade of toys is that you reuse the same

01:45:57.940 --> 01:45:58.940
pieces.

01:45:58.940 --> 01:45:59.940
Lego does the same thing.

01:45:59.940 --> 01:46:04.190
I mean, I don't care how ambitious whatever
it is you're buying, there are still certain

01:46:04.190 --> 01:46:05.190
blocks.

01:46:05.190 --> 01:46:09.250
I mean the colors may be different, but there
are still certain blocks that are universal

01:46:09.250 --> 01:46:10.920
in every one of their builds.

01:46:10.920 --> 01:46:15.099
And then, they put the unique pieces that
will make it into the shape they need.

01:46:15.099 --> 01:46:21.119
But there's still a portion of the reuse of
blocks that have been used for decades because

01:46:21.119 --> 01:46:22.340
that's their formula.

01:46:22.340 --> 01:46:23.340
Cool.

01:46:23.340 --> 01:46:24.530
Works for them.

01:46:24.530 --> 01:46:31.349
Toys are sort of the same, that they go, "Oh,
we'll do a generic body and we won't make

01:46:31.349 --> 01:46:33.200
anything unique about it.

01:46:33.200 --> 01:46:40.780
And then, that way, we can paint it with a
bat symbol on it or a S or a flash and it

01:46:40.780 --> 01:46:43.890
all sort works, and we don't have to cut any
more molds.

01:46:43.890 --> 01:46:45.530
We don't have to cut any more steel."

01:46:45.530 --> 01:46:46.530
Why?

01:46:46.530 --> 01:46:47.530
Because that's money.

01:46:47.530 --> 01:46:50.060
"And we don't have to do all those things."

01:46:50.060 --> 01:46:56.840
Early on, when I was giving Warner Bros. our
lineups and we were talking to them, I go,

01:46:56.840 --> 01:46:59.239
"I want to make this character," and they
were talking about this and then they go,

01:46:59.239 --> 01:47:00.659
"Oh, we can't really make that character."

01:47:00.659 --> 01:47:01.659
I go, "Why?"

01:47:01.659 --> 01:47:04.910
And they go, "Well, it doesn't really share
the same look as any other character."

01:47:04.910 --> 01:47:07.170
I went, "No, no, I know that.

01:47:07.170 --> 01:47:08.170
We're good.

01:47:08.170 --> 01:47:09.429
We'll cut some new molds."

01:47:09.429 --> 01:47:16.080
When they found out that I was willing to
make new molds, specifically for one character,

01:47:16.080 --> 01:47:19.540
they were blown away by that because they'd
been dealing with the big boys.

01:47:19.540 --> 01:47:21.610
They were going, "Hold a sec.

01:47:21.610 --> 01:47:25.040
You're going to do five new characters and
make five new sets of molds?"

01:47:25.040 --> 01:47:26.040
"Uh-huh."

01:47:26.040 --> 01:47:27.739
"And that's okay with you?"

01:47:27.739 --> 01:47:30.460
"Yeah, it's how I've been doing it for years."

01:47:30.460 --> 01:47:31.460
Wow.

01:47:31.460 --> 01:47:36.060
Here's why that mattered, Tim, because it
allowed me now, for the very first time, to

01:47:36.060 --> 01:47:39.270
make characters that nobody had made prior
to that.

01:47:39.270 --> 01:47:42.430
People have been making Superman and Batman
and Wonder Woman toys for decades.

01:47:42.430 --> 01:47:43.800
Of course they had.

01:47:43.800 --> 01:47:50.580
But some of the other iterations of those
characters and/or B characters or C characters

01:47:50.580 --> 01:47:54.949
that look super cool, I don't care if you
even know their name, in plastic, they look

01:47:54.949 --> 01:47:56.800
super cool on a shelf.

01:47:56.800 --> 01:47:57.830
I was able to make them.

01:47:57.830 --> 01:48:04.500
And then I think there was a big enough audience
in geekdom that came by and went, "Oh, my

01:48:04.500 --> 01:48:08.300
goodness, nobody's ever made that character
or that character or that character or that

01:48:08.300 --> 01:48:15.110
character," and they were able to get, literally,
the rookie cards using sort of trading card.

01:48:15.110 --> 01:48:17.920
They were getting the rookie cards of all
these characters that nobody had ever made

01:48:17.920 --> 01:48:20.100
before, and I think it mattered.

01:48:20.100 --> 01:48:25.179
I think it mattered that I was doing something
different than what the big boys did.

01:48:25.179 --> 01:48:27.350
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, that makes sense.

01:48:27.350 --> 01:48:28.350
All right.

01:48:28.350 --> 01:48:29.350
I'm glad I asked.

01:48:29.350 --> 01:48:31.440
Thank you for answering.

01:48:31.440 --> 01:48:32.440
Where to from there?

01:48:32.440 --> 01:48:37.090
Or I should say, not really from there because
these are all parallel lines in a sense.

01:48:37.090 --> 01:48:38.090
Todd McFarlane: Yeah.

01:48:38.090 --> 01:48:39.090
Tim Ferriss: The comic books are going.

01:48:39.090 --> 01:48:40.090
The toys are going.

01:48:40.090 --> 01:48:42.350
Todd McFarlane: They each create an opportunity.

01:48:42.350 --> 01:48:43.739
Again, let's talk about music videos.

01:48:43.739 --> 01:48:44.739
You mentioned that.

01:48:44.739 --> 01:48:46.090
How do you get to music videos?

01:48:46.090 --> 01:48:47.270
I don't even read music.

01:48:47.270 --> 01:48:49.960
I can't read a lick of music and I've got
a Grammy.

01:48:49.960 --> 01:48:50.960
How's that possible.

01:48:50.960 --> 01:48:55.239
Some dumbass kid who doesn't know anything
about music's got a Grammy.

01:48:55.239 --> 01:48:56.250
It bothers my wife.

01:48:56.250 --> 01:49:00.810
She's a very good pianist and it bugs her
that I've got one, so don't say it out loud

01:49:00.810 --> 01:49:02.200
if you come to dinner.

01:49:02.200 --> 01:49:03.510
Here's how you get there.

01:49:03.510 --> 01:49:06.540
You just find the back door.

01:49:06.540 --> 01:49:09.690
They're always going to block the front door.

01:49:09.690 --> 01:49:11.860
Status quo is going to always get put in front
of you.

01:49:11.860 --> 01:49:12.960
You find the back door.

01:49:12.960 --> 01:49:14.440
Here's the back door.

01:49:14.440 --> 01:49:16.720
Out of the blue, we're doing the HBO animation.

01:49:16.720 --> 01:49:19.810
It's doing pretty well and, again, I'm winning
a couple Emmys.

01:49:19.810 --> 01:49:24.300
And then, out of the blue, I don't know why,
go ask him, out of the blue, I get a phone

01:49:24.300 --> 01:49:25.590
call from Eddie Vedder.

01:49:25.590 --> 01:49:28.690
Eddie Vedder's the lead singer of Pearl Jam.

01:49:28.690 --> 01:49:30.880
Eddie then says, "Hey, Todd.

01:49:30.880 --> 01:49:31.880
Eddie."

01:49:31.880 --> 01:49:33.030
"Hey, Eddie, how you doing, bud?"

01:49:33.030 --> 01:49:34.110
"Here's what we want to do, Todd.

01:49:34.110 --> 01:49:38.719
The record label is bugging us to do a music
video.

01:49:38.719 --> 01:49:41.570
We hate music videos and we don't want to
be in it."

01:49:41.570 --> 01:49:44.550
Which is sort of a cool thing that it's like,
"Pardon?

01:49:44.550 --> 01:49:46.650
You don't want to be in your own music video?"

01:49:46.650 --> 01:49:50.410
"No, and we've been putting them off and putting
them off, but they keep bugging us.

01:49:50.410 --> 01:49:55.150
Now we're at the point where I've figured
it out, Todd.

01:49:55.150 --> 01:49:58.760
I've been watching this Spawn animation on
TV.

01:49:58.760 --> 01:50:00.090
We can do it animated.

01:50:00.090 --> 01:50:03.580
Let's just do it animated, and then we don't
have to be in it."

01:50:03.580 --> 01:50:05.780
I was like, "Okay, cool."

01:50:05.780 --> 01:50:07.500
And so he goes, "Do you want to help us with
it?"

01:50:07.500 --> 01:50:08.830
I'm like, 'Yeah, sure, cool.

01:50:08.830 --> 01:50:09.830
What's the gig?"

01:50:09.830 --> 01:50:11.570
And then, he sort of gave the song.

01:50:11.570 --> 01:50:14.219
It was called "Do the Evolution."

01:50:14.219 --> 01:50:15.219
I go, "What's it about?"

01:50:15.219 --> 01:50:18.151
He goes, "Oh, it's about all time, space,
and dimension, but you've got to do it in

01:50:18.151 --> 01:50:19.500
three and a half minutes."

01:50:19.500 --> 01:50:23.510
I go, "Okay, I think we can come up with some
creative things on it."

01:50:23.510 --> 01:50:26.880
And then, Eddie went away and he goes, "Here,
I'll send you something here in a couple days

01:50:26.880 --> 01:50:29.150
just to sort of give you the vibe."

01:50:29.150 --> 01:50:35.969
And so, a couple days later, I receive in
the mail from Eddie this tape and I play it.

01:50:35.969 --> 01:50:44.389
He's taken a couple of episodes of Spawn and
edited them to go with his music of the song

01:50:44.389 --> 01:50:46.530
that we were talking about, "Do the Evolution."

01:50:46.530 --> 01:50:47.530
Tim Ferriss: That must have been surreal.

01:50:47.530 --> 01:50:49.080
Todd McFarlane: I wish I had that tape.

01:50:49.080 --> 01:50:50.679
I don't know what I did with this tape.

01:50:50.679 --> 01:50:52.889
And so I get on the phone, and it was really
good.

01:50:52.889 --> 01:50:54.910
I've got to tell you, Tim, it was really good.

01:50:54.910 --> 01:50:59.530
I get on the phone, I go, "Oh," I said, "Hey,
Eddie, I've got it.

01:50:59.530 --> 01:51:00.820
I understand what you want to do.

01:51:00.820 --> 01:51:01.820
Okay, whatever.

01:51:01.820 --> 01:51:02.940
We'll come up with that.

01:51:02.940 --> 01:51:03.940
Who edited this thing?

01:51:03.940 --> 01:51:04.949
This is super cool."

01:51:04.949 --> 01:51:07.130
He says, "Oh, that was me."

01:51:07.130 --> 01:51:10.080
And I went, "Hold a sec.

01:51:10.080 --> 01:51:12.520
You edited this video?"

01:51:12.520 --> 01:51:13.520
"Uh-huh."

01:51:13.520 --> 01:51:17.739
The big editing machine was called an Avid,
"You have an Avid at home?"

01:51:17.739 --> 01:51:19.739
"Yeah, I have an Avid."

01:51:19.739 --> 01:51:22.560
"Wow, Eddie, you're confusing me a bit.

01:51:22.560 --> 01:51:29.199
Rock and rollers, when they're done off stage,
go home, relax by doing Avid editing?"

01:51:29.199 --> 01:51:32.570
It doesn't make any sense to me.

01:51:32.570 --> 01:51:35.469
But he's like, "Yeah, I just sort of like
it and it's just sort of therapeutic."

01:51:35.469 --> 01:51:36.979
I'm like, "Cool."

01:51:36.979 --> 01:51:42.670
We end up doing the video for him and doing
the animation for it.

01:51:42.670 --> 01:51:48.369
It ends up getting nominated for a Grammy
which, again, it was my first foray into music.

01:51:48.369 --> 01:51:49.699
I went, "What?

01:51:49.699 --> 01:51:51.699
You go into music and you get nominated for
a Grammy.

01:51:51.699 --> 01:51:52.699
That was easy.

01:51:52.699 --> 01:51:53.739
Should have done this earlier."

01:51:53.739 --> 01:51:56.730
We lost to Madonna that year.

01:51:56.730 --> 01:52:02.000
But we're going to edit and Eddie says, "Hey,
is there any way I can come to California

01:52:02.000 --> 01:52:03.270
and edit with you?"

01:52:03.270 --> 01:52:05.590
"Eddie, it's your video.

01:52:05.590 --> 01:52:07.030
You can do whatever you want.

01:52:07.030 --> 01:52:08.219
Sure, heck, come."

01:52:08.219 --> 01:52:13.780
He sat with us in the three days that we were
there editing, and he's really smart.

01:52:13.780 --> 01:52:15.829
I mean, really, he had a lot of good stuff.

01:52:15.829 --> 01:52:16.869
"Hey, what if you did this?

01:52:16.869 --> 01:52:19.400
What if you cut this down and you did this,"
and whatever.

01:52:19.400 --> 01:52:20.900
He's talking the language.

01:52:20.900 --> 01:52:22.949
It was really good.

01:52:22.949 --> 01:52:31.540
The side story of that three days was he came
with a briefcase, the first day, and he's

01:52:31.540 --> 01:52:32.850
Eddie, all dressed in black.

01:52:32.850 --> 01:52:35.570
But he had a briefcase, what I thought was
sort of formal.

01:52:35.570 --> 01:52:41.199
He put the briefcase down next to us and then
we worked all day and we did the editing,

01:52:41.199 --> 01:52:42.800
and then he left.

01:52:42.800 --> 01:52:47.590
And then, the next day he came dressed just
like grunge Eddie, cool.

01:52:47.590 --> 01:52:51.469
He had his briefcase again and he put it right
down next to us.

01:52:51.469 --> 01:52:53.260
Never touched it the whole day again, and
he left.

01:52:53.260 --> 01:52:56.590
And then, on the third day he came, same thing.

01:52:56.590 --> 01:53:01.969
But at some point, I just went, "Hey Eddie,
I don't mean to pry, but why do you keep bringing

01:53:01.969 --> 01:53:04.800
the briefcase with you every day?

01:53:04.800 --> 01:53:06.750
It doesn't seem like you do anything with
it."

01:53:06.750 --> 01:53:10.150
And then, he goes, "Oh, my gosh, Todd, I'm
glad you finally asked."

01:53:10.150 --> 01:53:13.270
Now, I often wonder if I hadn't asked, what
would've happened?

01:53:13.270 --> 01:53:14.570
Would he had just gone home that day?

01:53:14.570 --> 01:53:18.690
He goes, "Oh, my gosh, Todd, I'm glad you
finally asked."

01:53:18.690 --> 01:53:22.500
It was a briefcase, just like you're a spy
or something.

01:53:22.500 --> 01:53:27.119
And then he clicked it open and you're waiting
for the CIA folders, except for there wasn't

01:53:27.119 --> 01:53:32.500
no CIA folders, it was two baseball gloves
and a ball.

01:53:32.500 --> 01:53:35.040
And one of the gloves was lefthanded.

01:53:35.040 --> 01:53:39.030
He turns to me and he goes, "I heard you're
a pretty good baseball player, Todd."

01:53:39.030 --> 01:53:42.400
I went, "Yeah, yeah, I played back then, Eddie."

01:53:42.400 --> 01:53:45.679
He goes, "You want to play catch during lunch
break?"

01:53:45.679 --> 01:53:49.860
I went, "Sure, I can play catch with Eddie
Vedder.

01:53:49.860 --> 01:53:52.610
That's cool," because Eddie's a big baseball
nut.

01:53:52.610 --> 01:53:58.150
And so he had done his homework and he knew
it and he knew I was left-handed.

01:53:58.150 --> 01:54:00.650
Like I said, if I'd never asked him about
it, would he have ever opened it?

01:54:00.650 --> 01:54:01.650
But then it goes even further.

01:54:01.650 --> 01:54:05.550
We finally go outside and, unfortunately,
it's seasonably hot.

01:54:05.550 --> 01:54:09.760
It's about 105, 106 that day in California.

01:54:09.760 --> 01:54:11.270
But I'm from Phoenix, it doesn't matter.

01:54:11.270 --> 01:54:12.369
I can deal.

01:54:12.369 --> 01:54:20.130
But Eddie has got black boots, black pants,
black shirt, black sunglasses, and he's smoking.

01:54:20.130 --> 01:54:25.030
Have you ever seen that scene of John Candy
in the movie Splash where he is trying to

01:54:25.030 --> 01:54:29.139
play handball and he's drinking and whatever,
and he's having a heart attack while he's

01:54:29.139 --> 01:54:30.139
playing?

01:54:30.139 --> 01:54:37.230
We start going and, at about minute eight,
I could see he's starting to slow down and

01:54:37.230 --> 01:54:41.800
the throws are not quite with the authority
and it gets slower.

01:54:41.800 --> 01:54:43.909
And then, he starts breathing heavier.

01:54:43.909 --> 01:54:48.081
And then, pretty soon I go, "Eddie, let's
just take a break."

01:54:48.081 --> 01:54:50.580
He's like, "Yeah, okay, if you want to."

01:54:50.580 --> 01:54:51.580
If you want to.

01:54:51.580 --> 01:54:54.820
And then he sits down and he, "Uh, oh."

01:54:54.820 --> 01:54:58.210
I mean, I go, "I'm going to kill this man."

01:54:58.210 --> 01:55:01.400
We keep playing, he's literally going to pass
out with heat exhaustion, I'm going to have

01:55:01.400 --> 01:55:04.160
to rush him to the hospital.

01:55:04.160 --> 01:55:05.250
But he wasn't going to give in.

01:55:05.250 --> 01:55:08.260
This is a dumb thing about boys and our testosterone.

01:55:08.260 --> 01:55:10.840
Neither one of us was going to give, but I'm
a cockroach.

01:55:10.840 --> 01:55:13.770
I could have gone all day until I killed him.

01:55:13.770 --> 01:55:14.970
I just pulled it back.

01:55:14.970 --> 01:55:18.961
I didn't feel like being the guy that was
the one that sent Eddie to the hospital and

01:55:18.961 --> 01:55:22.230
had to cancel his — because they were on
tour at that point.

01:55:22.230 --> 01:55:23.260
He just came in for a couple days.

01:55:23.260 --> 01:55:24.260
Tim Ferriss: Oh, God.

01:55:24.260 --> 01:55:25.570
Todd McFarlane: I didn't want to be the bad
guy.

01:55:25.570 --> 01:55:31.060
But anyways, Eddie wasn't quite smart enough
to go dress appropriately and don't smoke

01:55:31.060 --> 01:55:33.190
when you're exerting yourself physically but,
anyways.

01:55:33.190 --> 01:55:38.140
Tim Ferriss: And so that video goes on to,
I don't know how many views it has now on

01:55:38.140 --> 01:55:42.380
YouTube, like 50 plus some million views on
YouTube.

01:55:42.380 --> 01:55:44.080
We'll link to that in the show notes.

01:55:44.080 --> 01:55:45.199
Where does all of this lead?

01:55:45.199 --> 01:55:51.290
This is your first foray into music and you're
animating, which means you're directing.

01:55:51.290 --> 01:55:53.579
Todd McFarlane: But that comes from the HBO.

01:55:53.579 --> 01:55:55.610
Again, this is —
Tim Ferriss: Right, it comes from the HBO.

01:55:55.610 --> 01:55:56.610
Todd McFarlane: Right.

01:55:56.610 --> 01:55:58.570
Again, you do Marvel, that begat Image Comics.

01:55:58.570 --> 01:56:04.800
Image begat — I'm going to use biblical
terms, begat Spawn.

01:56:04.800 --> 01:56:06.500
Spawn begat HBO.

01:56:06.500 --> 01:56:11.900
HBO, because Eddie was watching it, begat,
"Hey, you want to do a music video?"

01:56:11.900 --> 01:56:17.290
Music video comes out successful, gets nominated
for a Grammy, that begat people phoning, "Hey,

01:56:17.290 --> 01:56:19.409
you want to do more music videos?"

01:56:19.409 --> 01:56:27.199
It all just becomes this weird accident that
just starts tumbling and these doors of opportunity

01:56:27.199 --> 01:56:31.910
open up where you never knew they were ever
going to open up before.

01:56:31.910 --> 01:56:33.119
None of it's planned.

01:56:33.119 --> 01:56:35.790
It's just happy accidents along the way.

01:56:35.790 --> 01:56:41.361
Tim Ferriss: And doing a good job on each
piece of it so that those doors open or present

01:56:41.361 --> 01:56:42.361
themselves.

01:56:42.361 --> 01:56:44.880
Todd McFarlane: So let's talk about that piece.

01:56:44.880 --> 01:56:47.540
What you just said is important.

01:56:47.540 --> 01:56:53.100
Sometimes I get people go, "Oh, Todd, you're
just sort of getting rich and famous and you

01:56:53.100 --> 01:56:55.219
got a ego," whatever else.

01:56:55.219 --> 01:56:56.219
Probably that's true.

01:56:56.219 --> 01:56:59.550
But let me tell you why I like success.

01:56:59.550 --> 01:57:01.040
Not because I want some more money.

01:57:01.040 --> 01:57:02.090
I've got it.

01:57:02.090 --> 01:57:03.579
Not because I need a bigger ego.

01:57:03.579 --> 01:57:06.810
I've got plenty of people who've said nice
things about me for plenty of years.

01:57:06.810 --> 01:57:08.440
I'm over me.

01:57:08.440 --> 01:57:11.230
Nobody's more bored of Todd than me.

01:57:11.230 --> 01:57:17.810
Here's why success matters, because you can't
keep doing art, which is what I am, an artist.

01:57:17.810 --> 01:57:23.610
If I walk into the room and say, "Oh, get
this, the last thing I did failed," you don't

01:57:23.610 --> 01:57:25.219
get the job.

01:57:25.219 --> 01:57:30.750
You have to have a certain level of success
to get the next job.

01:57:30.750 --> 01:57:34.750
That's why I need to keep the momentum.

01:57:34.750 --> 01:57:36.330
That's why I need to have success.

01:57:36.330 --> 01:57:38.100
Not for the fame or the fortune.

01:57:38.100 --> 01:57:39.790
That's just a byproduct.

01:57:39.790 --> 01:57:46.270
I need it so I can do art again tomorrow and
the next day and the next day.

01:57:46.270 --> 01:57:51.659
Hopefully, if I do it right, I'll die and
I'll have been doing art until the time I

01:57:51.659 --> 01:57:53.760
take my last breath.

01:57:53.760 --> 01:57:55.610
That's the success.

01:57:55.610 --> 01:57:58.530
The art and the success has to be a component.

01:57:58.530 --> 01:58:04.960
You're not dealing with HBO and the NFL and
Microsoft and all these companies we've dealt

01:58:04.960 --> 01:58:11.340
with if you're not able to show that you can
do the job at a level that makes sense for

01:58:11.340 --> 01:58:12.400
them.

01:58:12.400 --> 01:58:15.610
I'm not going to apologize for us doing our
jobs.

01:58:15.610 --> 01:58:17.560
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

01:58:17.560 --> 01:58:19.060
Doesn't seem to be your style.

01:58:19.060 --> 01:58:20.060
All right.

01:58:20.060 --> 01:58:30.800
We're talking about these multiple encounters
with our very nice friend, dumb luck.

01:58:30.800 --> 01:58:34.239
But it's not really, as somebody put it to
me once, I can't remember exactly who it was,

01:58:34.239 --> 01:58:38.660
I wish I could give attribution, but they
talk about the surface area of luck.

01:58:38.660 --> 01:58:43.199
By doing certain things, you can increase
the surface area that is available for luck

01:58:43.199 --> 01:58:44.199
to stick to.

01:58:44.199 --> 01:58:45.199
Todd McFarlane: I agree.

01:58:45.199 --> 01:58:47.570
Tim Ferriss: I think you've done a very good
job with that.

01:58:47.570 --> 01:58:54.090
Where do games or movies enter into this entire
constellation of activities?

01:58:54.090 --> 01:58:55.090
Todd McFarlane: Right.

01:58:55.090 --> 01:58:58.380
Let's go back to the dumb luck just for a
minute to just paint a metaphor.

01:58:58.380 --> 01:59:04.990
Again, I at half court and I got my back to
the basket and I throw it backwards, and then

01:59:04.990 --> 01:59:08.489
I make the bucket and they're like, "Oh, Todd,
you're a genius."

01:59:08.489 --> 01:59:10.389
Some people will look at that and go, "You're
a genius."

01:59:10.389 --> 01:59:12.139
I'm going, "Eh, it's a little bit of dumb
luck."

01:59:12.139 --> 01:59:17.640
But to your point, the reason that I was able
to get that genius shot that some people think

01:59:17.640 --> 01:59:22.521
of, because I was there for four days in a
row and I took 10,000 shots and I missed every

01:59:22.521 --> 01:59:28.139
other one, but I didn't leave until I made
the shot.

01:59:28.139 --> 01:59:33.750
If you take 10,000 shots, sometimes one of
them goes in.

01:59:33.750 --> 01:59:39.020
There's the personality of going when they
say, "No," which is basically a missed shot,

01:59:39.020 --> 01:59:40.580
"Don't do it, Todd."

01:59:40.580 --> 01:59:42.119
"Oh, yeah, I'm going to shoot again."

01:59:42.119 --> 01:59:43.119
"You can't do that."

01:59:43.119 --> 01:59:44.119
"I'm going to shoot again."

01:59:44.119 --> 01:59:45.119
"You can't.

01:59:45.119 --> 01:59:46.119
You can't."

01:59:46.119 --> 01:59:47.119
Shoot, shoot, shoot.

01:59:47.119 --> 01:59:50.400
And then, bam, every now and then you sink
one and then you go, "How do I build off that?"

01:59:50.400 --> 01:59:55.010
Because people saw that, you put it up on
TikTok and you don't show all of your misses

01:59:55.010 --> 01:59:56.030
on all those TikToks.

01:59:56.030 --> 01:59:58.219
You only show the one you made.

01:59:58.219 --> 02:00:01.970
You don't know how many years or hours or
months they spent until they made that one

02:00:01.970 --> 02:00:02.970
shot.

02:00:02.970 --> 02:00:05.130
Business, a little bit is like that.

02:00:05.130 --> 02:00:10.330
Again, it's a perceived skill because when
you see the one shot on TikTok, you think

02:00:10.330 --> 02:00:14.850
they're super awesome because you didn't see
the three days they didn't make it.

02:00:14.850 --> 02:00:19.450
There's a little bit of that going on there,
so back to your question, sorry.

02:00:19.450 --> 02:00:25.250
Tim Ferriss: Well, actually, before we get
to games and movies and how that sort of coalesced

02:00:25.250 --> 02:00:32.790
or entered the scene in some way, let's talk
about creating characters or fictional worlds

02:00:32.790 --> 02:00:35.949
that endure for decades.

02:00:35.949 --> 02:00:44.429
In doing research for this, I found an interview
of yours where you talked about going into

02:00:44.429 --> 02:00:48.860
Hollywood and pitching various ideas.

02:00:48.860 --> 02:00:54.610
I might be misquoting, you can't believe everything
you read on the internet, but the section

02:00:54.610 --> 02:00:58.079
here that I'd love to hear you elaborate on
is, "So these people in the meeting go, 'What's

02:00:58.079 --> 02:00:59.079
the end of it?'

02:00:59.079 --> 02:01:00.639
And my answer is "I don't give a shit.

02:01:00.639 --> 02:01:01.639
What's the end of Superman?

02:01:01.639 --> 02:01:02.639
What's the end of Batman?

02:01:02.639 --> 02:01:03.699
What's the end of Spider-Man?

02:01:03.699 --> 02:01:05.599
Why are you asking me for the end?

02:01:05.599 --> 02:01:11.140
Why aren't you asking, 'Can this last for
30 years?'"

02:01:11.140 --> 02:01:12.140
It goes on.

02:01:12.140 --> 02:01:18.219
But could you just speak to building something
that endures and how that informs, maybe,

02:01:18.219 --> 02:01:20.900
decisions you make or how you think about
creativity?

02:01:20.900 --> 02:01:26.739
Todd McFarlane: Well, that quote that you
spoke is true, and I've had that conversation

02:01:26.739 --> 02:01:28.050
many, many times in Hollywood.

02:01:28.050 --> 02:01:32.570
It's weird that you're walking in, presenting
them the beginning of an idea, and one of

02:01:32.570 --> 02:01:36.489
the first questions is, how does the idea
end?

02:01:36.489 --> 02:01:44.820
For a company, a studio, a conglomerate, who's
built on trying to make money, why would you

02:01:44.820 --> 02:01:46.469
want to kill anything in the crib?

02:01:46.469 --> 02:01:51.540
It's a weird conversation I'm having with
them because, again, what is the end of Batman?

02:01:51.540 --> 02:01:52.540
I don't know.

02:01:52.540 --> 02:01:53.540
Who cares?

02:01:53.540 --> 02:01:55.690
We hope it goes a thousand years.

02:01:55.690 --> 02:01:58.300
Why are we talking about this in the first
meeting?

02:01:58.300 --> 02:02:03.280
I mean, I get it maybe in season three and
the ratings are lagging, we can maybe have

02:02:03.280 --> 02:02:06.380
this conversation, but not in the first one.

02:02:06.380 --> 02:02:17.179
I think one of the ways to create a brand
that does have some value is using another

02:02:17.179 --> 02:02:19.730
word, and it's called attrition.

02:02:19.730 --> 02:02:25.239
Attrition is that, over time, you're going
to have both high points and low points, but

02:02:25.239 --> 02:02:26.829
you've got to just keep going.

02:02:26.829 --> 02:02:27.829
You can't quit.

02:02:27.829 --> 02:02:32.620
You've got to just keep going and going and
going and going and find yourself and put

02:02:32.620 --> 02:02:38.590
yourself in as many corners as possible, during
both the good and the bad time, so that maybe

02:02:38.590 --> 02:02:46.710
people don't know who you are or don't know
what you do but then they go, "You know what?

02:02:46.710 --> 02:02:49.110
I've heard the character Spawn."

02:02:49.110 --> 02:02:51.000
They don't have to have bought anything.

02:02:51.000 --> 02:02:55.340
I'm going to use a big example.

02:02:55.340 --> 02:03:02.690
I know and have read nothing about the Kardashians,
and yet I have information in my head about

02:03:02.690 --> 02:03:03.780
them.

02:03:03.780 --> 02:03:07.429
I've done nothing towards that and, still,
it's in my head.

02:03:07.429 --> 02:03:08.429
Why?

02:03:08.429 --> 02:03:10.580
Because I can't get away from them.

02:03:10.580 --> 02:03:17.590
Somehow, because I'm looking for an article,
I read the headline, and so I've got info.

02:03:17.590 --> 02:03:18.679
That's just attrition.

02:03:18.679 --> 02:03:25.070
And so, were people going, "Did the Spawn
comic book have a lull at times?"

02:03:25.070 --> 02:03:26.239
Of course it did.

02:03:26.239 --> 02:03:28.489
Where the sales were not very good?

02:03:28.489 --> 02:03:30.260
Of course it did.

02:03:30.260 --> 02:03:35.320
But I thought that the value wasn't that,
was I making money in the way that I needed

02:03:35.320 --> 02:03:40.369
to at that point, it was just to keep the
machinery going because the value, at some

02:03:40.369 --> 02:03:49.400
point, isn't now any single issue or any single
year, it's now sum total of all of it.

02:03:49.400 --> 02:03:55.469
So that all the people over 30 years who have
passed through any of the characters I've

02:03:55.469 --> 02:03:56.469
created, let's use Spawn.

02:03:56.469 --> 02:03:58.070
He's my biggest guy.

02:03:58.070 --> 02:04:00.719
Maybe they came in for five years and then
quit, but they're still there.

02:04:00.719 --> 02:04:01.860
They're still there.

02:04:01.860 --> 02:04:05.590
If we ever do a movie, they're going to remember,
"Oh, yeah, yeah.

02:04:05.590 --> 02:04:06.889
20 years ago, I collect that."

02:04:06.889 --> 02:04:10.909
But maybe it was somebody who passed through
it 15 years ago or 10 years ago, or five years

02:04:10.909 --> 02:04:16.170
ago, or just jumped on since we set the world's
record that came in going, "Huh, you know

02:04:16.170 --> 02:04:17.170
what?

02:04:17.170 --> 02:04:20.070
I've been a DC and Marvel acolyte and now
I'm going to sort of try this."

02:04:20.070 --> 02:04:21.219
I don't care.

02:04:21.219 --> 02:04:25.960
All you want to do is collectively keep building
so that when you go to your next move, you

02:04:25.960 --> 02:04:30.230
can maybe peel off a piece of that army off
to the side.

02:04:30.230 --> 02:04:38.060
To me, now, it's just attrition of just going
again, again, again.

02:04:38.060 --> 02:04:40.889
And so it will just get into your brain.

02:04:40.889 --> 02:04:43.400
I'll give you a bad, bad, bad example early
on.

02:04:43.400 --> 02:04:45.780
I mentioned it before when I was at my first
Toy Fair.

02:04:45.780 --> 02:04:47.550
I gave them a Hot Wheel.

02:04:47.550 --> 02:04:48.550
I bought a funny car.

02:04:48.550 --> 02:04:52.500
I don't know if people know what a funny car
is, but a funny car is a cut down dragster

02:04:52.500 --> 02:04:56.130
and they put this really cool fiberglass body
and they go down.

02:04:56.130 --> 02:04:58.090
Just Google, what's a funny car look like?

02:04:58.090 --> 02:04:59.090
They're super cool.

02:04:59.090 --> 02:05:02.800
When I grew up in Orange County, Orange County
International Raceway, we used to go down

02:05:02.800 --> 02:05:06.400
there, funny cars was the coolest thing in
my life.

02:05:06.400 --> 02:05:08.449
Way better than dragons and robots.

02:05:08.449 --> 02:05:16.290
Anyways, I bought a funny car when Spawn came
out and, really, because I wanted a funny

02:05:16.290 --> 02:05:20.370
car, but I had to convince my wife and other
people otherwise.

02:05:20.370 --> 02:05:25.290
It was like, "No, it's not that I need a funny
car, it's just that if I take a funny car

02:05:25.290 --> 02:05:29.280
and I paint the word "Spawn" on it and I take
it to shows, you know who has a funny car

02:05:29.280 --> 02:05:30.699
at comic book conventions?

02:05:30.699 --> 02:05:32.230
No one.

02:05:32.230 --> 02:05:35.179
You know who's ever had a funny car comic
book conventions?

02:05:35.179 --> 02:05:36.750
No one."

02:05:36.750 --> 02:05:41.960
If you bring something to a show that nobody's
ever brought before, you'll be surprised.

02:05:41.960 --> 02:05:49.619
If you bring a elephant to a comic book convention
and paint it purple, people will actually

02:05:49.619 --> 02:05:50.619
look at it.

02:05:50.619 --> 02:05:55.150
They'll come and look at it because they'll
be going, "Why you got a purple elephant here?"

02:05:55.150 --> 02:06:00.060
My purple elephant, my pink elephant, literally
was the funny car.

02:06:00.060 --> 02:06:02.420
Now, here's what I did.

02:06:02.420 --> 02:06:04.770
I did a two tone paint job on it.

02:06:04.770 --> 02:06:10.190
One side was white with the word "Spawn" on
it and it had blood like you hit a deer at

02:06:10.190 --> 02:06:12.900
a thousand miles an hour and it splattered
over the white.

02:06:12.900 --> 02:06:17.090
The other side was black with flames because
it's super cool.

02:06:17.090 --> 02:06:21.210
And then, what I used to do, Tim, is I used
to have it sort of centered in my big giant

02:06:21.210 --> 02:06:22.210
booth.

02:06:22.210 --> 02:06:25.610
And then, I had this piece of paper and it
said, "Vote.

02:06:25.610 --> 02:06:30.790
Which side do you like better so I can finish
this project?"

02:06:30.790 --> 02:06:32.100
Here's what people did.

02:06:32.100 --> 02:06:36.739
I saw it with my own eyes hundreds of times.

02:06:36.739 --> 02:06:40.790
They took their kid or one person and they
went on the white side and they went, "Yeah,

02:06:40.790 --> 02:06:41.860
that's kind of cool with the blood.

02:06:41.860 --> 02:06:43.760
I like it, and I like the fade."

02:06:43.760 --> 02:06:46.880
They went over to the flame black side and
went, "Yeah, I like that.

02:06:46.880 --> 02:06:47.990
But, man, let me see.

02:06:47.990 --> 02:06:49.159
Let me go back over there.

02:06:49.159 --> 02:06:52.170
No, maybe I like that one," and it went back
and forth, back and forth.

02:06:52.170 --> 02:06:54.050
And then, they marked the sheet.

02:06:54.050 --> 02:06:56.090
You know what I did with the sheet at the
end of each show?

02:06:56.090 --> 02:06:57.099
I threw it in the garbage.

02:06:57.099 --> 02:06:58.730
I threw it in the garbage.

02:06:58.730 --> 02:06:59.801
I didn't give a shit.

02:06:59.801 --> 02:07:01.449
I was never going to repaint that car.

02:07:01.449 --> 02:07:07.739
What I needed them to do, Tim, was to spend
five minutes looking at my logo.

02:07:07.739 --> 02:07:13.070
If I had painted that car with one paint job,
the same on both sides, they wouldn't have

02:07:13.070 --> 02:07:14.969
looked at it for five minutes.

02:07:14.969 --> 02:07:20.670
I forced them to look at the word "Spawn"
thinking that they were helping me on some

02:07:20.670 --> 02:07:26.610
polling survey, and it was just I need to
get the word "Spawn" into their wormhole.

02:07:26.610 --> 02:07:29.179
This was my trick.

02:07:29.179 --> 02:07:34.840
If you just figure out iterations of that,
how do you get people to do that?

02:07:34.840 --> 02:07:37.469
Then eventually, over time, time matters.

02:07:37.469 --> 02:07:38.469
Time does matter.

02:07:38.469 --> 02:07:40.900
Time becomes the biggest value you have on
your brand.

02:07:40.900 --> 02:07:48.230
Tim Ferriss: From a creative, let's just say,
writing standpoint, you've now published more

02:07:48.230 --> 02:07:56.119
than 300 editions of Spawn.

02:07:56.119 --> 02:08:02.940
When you started Spawn, how many books out
had you planned from a plot or character perspective?

02:08:02.940 --> 02:08:07.820
How much of it was setting, conditions and
making it cool, and then assuming you're just

02:08:07.820 --> 02:08:11.139
going to figure it out later and see where
it goes?

02:08:11.139 --> 02:08:15.710
How far in advance did you or do you plan
with that kind of thing?

02:08:15.710 --> 02:08:20.739
Todd McFarlane: Tim, I just finished what's
going to be the biggest comic book in the

02:08:20.739 --> 02:08:24.239
country this just last week, the Batman/Spawn
crossover.

02:08:24.239 --> 02:08:26.500
We touched upon it.

02:08:26.500 --> 02:08:30.679
I was winging it until they pried it out of
my hand on Tuesday.

02:08:30.679 --> 02:08:32.460
What are you talking about?

02:08:32.460 --> 02:08:33.909
It horrifies writers.

02:08:33.909 --> 02:08:35.619
The way that I do it is horrifying.

02:08:35.619 --> 02:08:37.980
Do I have it in my head?

02:08:37.980 --> 02:08:38.980
Of course not.

02:08:38.980 --> 02:08:45.159
I just figure it out on the fly and, luckily,
I get it right more times than I get it wrong.

02:08:45.159 --> 02:08:48.060
Here's what the goal was when I started Spawn.

02:08:48.060 --> 02:08:50.300
There was two goals.

02:08:50.300 --> 02:08:55.531
One, we started the company Image Comics,
the third largest company as I mentioned.

02:08:55.531 --> 02:09:01.889
I said to myself, when we started, that Image
Comic Books will exist for my lifetime.

02:09:01.889 --> 02:09:03.210
How do I know that?

02:09:03.210 --> 02:09:08.929
because even if there was only one book called
Spawn, I was never going to take that logo

02:09:08.929 --> 02:09:10.070
off.

02:09:10.070 --> 02:09:13.329
I knew it was going to exist because I'm just
that militant.

02:09:13.329 --> 02:09:17.130
I'm putting it on issue number one and it
will never come off.

02:09:17.130 --> 02:09:21.429
Image Comic Books somehow, maybe in a small
tiny way, will exist.

02:09:21.429 --> 02:09:25.179
Obviously, we're flourishing so I don't have
to worry about there only being one book because

02:09:25.179 --> 02:09:28.230
we do about 60 or 70 books a month.

02:09:28.230 --> 02:09:35.290
Number two, could I create a character that
would outlive me, period.

02:09:35.290 --> 02:09:39.219
The guy who created Superman is dead.

02:09:39.219 --> 02:09:42.440
The guy who created Mickey Mouse, dead.

02:09:42.440 --> 02:09:46.590
The guy who created the Fantastic Four, we
talked about him earlier today, Stan Lee,

02:09:46.590 --> 02:09:47.590
dead.

02:09:47.590 --> 02:09:50.699
Their characters live on.

02:09:50.699 --> 02:09:57.070
I consider creations to be creative children.

02:09:57.070 --> 02:09:59.080
Every parent has the same wish.

02:09:59.080 --> 02:10:02.660
You always want your children to outlive you.

02:10:02.660 --> 02:10:05.070
You always want your children to outlive you.

02:10:05.070 --> 02:10:06.070
Why?

02:10:06.070 --> 02:10:09.989
No parent gets up saying, "I hope my children
go before I do."

02:10:09.989 --> 02:10:11.699
You always want to go.

02:10:11.699 --> 02:10:15.000
We've seen movies, "I'll sacrifice my life.

02:10:15.000 --> 02:10:16.000
Women and children first."

02:10:16.000 --> 02:10:19.370
You'll sacrifice your life for your family,
but you go first.

02:10:19.370 --> 02:10:25.520
If these are creative children, then the goal
is, because I read about these people who

02:10:25.520 --> 02:10:30.260
created these characters that I know about,
but their creators have long since passed

02:10:30.260 --> 02:10:33.150
away, including my good friend Stan.

02:10:33.150 --> 02:10:37.530
Could I create one or two characters?

02:10:37.530 --> 02:10:38.530
I don't need a hundred.

02:10:38.530 --> 02:10:39.639
I'm not Disney.

02:10:39.639 --> 02:10:40.639
I'm not Walt Disney.

02:10:40.639 --> 02:10:41.639
I'm not Stan Lee.

02:10:41.639 --> 02:10:42.639
I'm not that good.

02:10:42.639 --> 02:10:47.219
But could I create one or two characters that
when I die, people say, "I still want to see

02:10:47.219 --> 02:10:48.300
the characters."

02:10:48.300 --> 02:10:52.909
That was the goal in the back of my head.

02:10:52.909 --> 02:10:55.500
Did I have a plan of where it was going?

02:10:55.500 --> 02:10:56.500
No, not really.

02:10:56.500 --> 02:10:58.710
Did I have a plan of how it was going to end?

02:10:58.710 --> 02:10:59.900
I do actually have the end.

02:10:59.900 --> 02:11:03.530
I just never have given it to anybody, and
I hope it never gets written, because the

02:11:03.530 --> 02:11:05.420
only time you write the end is if nobody wants
it.

02:11:05.420 --> 02:11:09.190
So you go, "Ah, I guess we better write the
last story and put it to bed," or whatever

02:11:09.190 --> 02:11:10.190
else.

02:11:10.190 --> 02:11:19.250
Now to the point, Tim, and I hate saying this
out loud, but Spawn's costume was alive.

02:11:19.250 --> 02:11:20.530
It's like a symbiot.

02:11:20.530 --> 02:11:25.429
It moves, and the chains are like the tongue,
and the spikes are like the teeth, and the

02:11:25.429 --> 02:11:27.630
cape is all alive.

02:11:27.630 --> 02:11:30.940
Was his costume alive an issue with number
one?

02:11:30.940 --> 02:11:31.980
No.

02:11:31.980 --> 02:11:33.020
No.

02:11:33.020 --> 02:11:35.040
How did it become alive?

02:11:35.040 --> 02:11:37.750
You just hit these moments real quickly.

02:11:37.750 --> 02:11:39.860
People were right in, because I'm very lazy.

02:11:39.860 --> 02:11:41.949
I never did a lot of referencing.

02:11:41.949 --> 02:11:45.760
There was no internet, but I still didn't
go to the library and get reference.

02:11:45.760 --> 02:11:47.240
I had deadlines, don't have time for it.

02:11:47.240 --> 02:11:48.780
I just fake everything.

02:11:48.780 --> 02:11:53.869
So I was drawing my pages of issue number
one, and I guess this is how bad I am.

02:11:53.869 --> 02:11:56.329
I don't pay attention to my own drawings.

02:11:56.329 --> 02:12:01.449
People were writing in, going, "Todd Todd,
do you know you had the pouch on his left

02:12:01.449 --> 02:12:03.469
leg, and now it's on his right leg?

02:12:03.469 --> 02:12:08.559
Do you know that you had six spikes on his
arm, and now he's got eight spikes?"

02:12:08.559 --> 02:12:11.810
These are easy moments, Tim, that you have
two choices.

02:12:11.810 --> 02:12:14.510
You either admit you're a dumb shit.

02:12:14.510 --> 02:12:19.990
Or you go, "That's because it's alive.

02:12:19.990 --> 02:12:26.280
The costume morphs, and it can do anything
at any time.

02:12:26.280 --> 02:12:28.719
It's a living being."

02:12:28.719 --> 02:12:32.591
And that's now part of the mythology, and
it was just because I screwed up.

02:12:32.591 --> 02:12:33.630
What are you talking about?

02:12:33.630 --> 02:12:38.570
You make lemonade out of your lemons, and
you just keep moving on and hope they don't

02:12:38.570 --> 02:12:39.570
catch up to you.

02:12:39.570 --> 02:12:44.260
I've been in those rooms plenty of times,
where I said something I shouldn't have, and

02:12:44.260 --> 02:12:47.590
they go —
I'll give you an example.

02:12:47.590 --> 02:12:54.810
I was doing a movie pitch for something, and
I think it was for Doom or something.

02:12:54.810 --> 02:12:58.600
I was given this pitch, and they discovered
something underneath the Mojave Desert or

02:12:58.600 --> 02:12:59.600
whatever.

02:12:59.600 --> 02:13:04.000
I was on a rant, and my buddy next to me slides
a piece of paper over to me.

02:13:04.000 --> 02:13:05.099
I'm right in the middle of it.

02:13:05.099 --> 02:13:08.770
I'm with the Warner Bros. people and the prior
writers, because they brought me in to see

02:13:08.770 --> 02:13:10.369
if they could help spark it.

02:13:10.369 --> 02:13:16.369
He slides a piece of paper over to me as I'm
standing up, and on it says, "Todd, it takes

02:13:16.369 --> 02:13:19.590
place on the moon," or something like that.

02:13:19.590 --> 02:13:22.690
I'm going, "And they discover it in the Mojave
Desert."

02:13:22.690 --> 02:13:27.390
I'm actually off a lot of miles, and I'm like
—

02:13:27.390 --> 02:13:34.179
So I just blow the whole premise of this book,
and I'd go, "Well, I'm this deep.

02:13:34.179 --> 02:13:35.989
I've got to finish.

02:13:35.989 --> 02:13:37.880
I've got to keep going."

02:13:37.880 --> 02:13:43.909
And I sit down, and I remember the executive
at Warner Bros. looks at me for a couple seconds,

02:13:43.909 --> 02:13:44.909
dead silence.

02:13:44.909 --> 02:13:51.489
And then he goes, slaps his hand and goes,
"That's what I'm talking about.

02:13:51.489 --> 02:13:54.860
He's not afraid to take chances!

02:13:54.860 --> 02:13:55.860
He's not afraid."

02:13:55.860 --> 02:13:58.380
And the answer was, I just didn't do enough
research, sadly.

02:13:58.380 --> 02:14:01.250
Otherwise, I would've put it on the moon.

02:14:01.250 --> 02:14:06.820
So sometimes you just have to, if you say
it with enough confidence, they'll go along

02:14:06.820 --> 02:14:08.700
for the ride with it.

02:14:08.700 --> 02:14:12.840
"He's not afraid to basically follow everything
in the book.

02:14:12.840 --> 02:14:14.449
This is what I've been talking about.

02:14:14.449 --> 02:14:17.900
We've got to bend some of the rules, blah,
blah blah."

02:14:17.900 --> 02:14:21.409
Anyways, The Spawn, we're up to issue 336.

02:14:21.409 --> 02:14:24.150
Went to the printers a couple days ago.

02:14:24.150 --> 02:14:26.460
Do I know what 337 is?

02:14:26.460 --> 02:14:29.160
I write a book called Gunslinger Spawn.

02:14:29.160 --> 02:14:30.469
Do I know what the next issue is?

02:14:30.469 --> 02:14:31.469
Nope.

02:14:31.469 --> 02:14:32.469
I know there's a guy.

02:14:32.469 --> 02:14:35.599
I just introduced a cliffhanger with a guy
who runs fast.

02:14:35.599 --> 02:14:39.420
That's all I know, that I've got to pick up
with a guy who runs fast.

02:14:39.420 --> 02:14:42.840
So I'll figure it out when I've got to get
the plot in two days.

02:14:42.840 --> 02:14:49.099
10 years from now, you and I may be talking
about my speedster runner, who's the most

02:14:49.099 --> 02:14:50.780
popular guy.

02:14:50.780 --> 02:14:55.280
I had no idea the day before what I was doing
with him.

02:14:55.280 --> 02:14:58.030
Tim Ferriss: Well, Todd, where do you want
to go next?

02:14:58.030 --> 02:14:59.030
I'll leave it up to you.

02:14:59.030 --> 02:15:00.429
You've got so many stories.

02:15:00.429 --> 02:15:04.860
You seemingly have so many lives that run
in tandem.

02:15:04.860 --> 02:15:08.420
You're an excellent five-ball juggler.

02:15:08.420 --> 02:15:11.150
Which ball, if any, would you like to talk
about next?

02:15:11.150 --> 02:15:17.710
Todd McFarlane: What if we get away from the
businesses and talk about personalities, not

02:15:17.710 --> 02:15:18.860
mine, but other people.

02:15:18.860 --> 02:15:24.909
I think that what's of value to people who
are thinking about doing ideas and/or some

02:15:24.909 --> 02:15:33.889
entrepreneurialship is, to some extent, recognizing
your own personalities of whether you're actually

02:15:33.889 --> 02:15:36.380
built for war or not.

02:15:36.380 --> 02:15:38.730
To me, all of this is a war.

02:15:38.730 --> 02:15:44.510
I know my wife has said, "It's sort of sad
that you see your life as a war every day."

02:15:44.510 --> 02:15:50.770
I'm ready, and if I've got one regret, and
I've got a few of them, but one of them is

02:15:50.770 --> 02:15:57.600
I wish I was born 3,000 years ago, because
back then, in a movie like Braveheart, if

02:15:57.600 --> 02:16:02.570
you believed in something so strongly, you
would go to the battlefield.

02:16:02.570 --> 02:16:08.719
You would face your enemy, who basically was
trying to deny you whatever it was that would

02:16:08.719 --> 02:16:11.530
give you personal happiness or what you thought
would be good for your family.

02:16:11.530 --> 02:16:14.760
Only two things were going to happen.

02:16:14.760 --> 02:16:19.480
You were either going to cut his head off,
or he was going to cut yours.

02:16:19.480 --> 02:16:20.560
That simple.

02:16:20.560 --> 02:16:25.870
I've always been the guy willing to go, "I'll
die for what I believe."

02:16:25.870 --> 02:16:30.929
Now, unfortunately, Tim, killing your enemy
and cutting their head off is illegal.

02:16:30.929 --> 02:16:36.229
It's really frustrating to me because man,
there's a lot of heads I'd like to see on

02:16:36.229 --> 02:16:37.229
the ground.

02:16:37.229 --> 02:16:39.319
And I'm sure they'd like to see mine.

02:16:39.319 --> 02:16:40.640
But we can't do that.

02:16:40.640 --> 02:16:42.180
We have to be a law-abiding citizen.

02:16:42.180 --> 02:16:48.090
So now what we have to do in modern times
is sue people and take away market shares

02:16:48.090 --> 02:16:50.590
and tell them "You will never work in this
town."

02:16:50.590 --> 02:16:57.340
And it's just not nearly as satisfying as
if I could just cut their heads off.

02:16:57.340 --> 02:17:07.460
If I get ready for the war, which means that
every day — I'm going to give you a piece

02:17:07.460 --> 02:17:11.420
of advice that don't tell your mom if you're
listening to me, because moms will be horrified

02:17:11.420 --> 02:17:16.769
by this — here it is: lower the bar.

02:17:16.769 --> 02:17:19.910
Lower the bar.

02:17:19.910 --> 02:17:21.080
Don't raise the bar.

02:17:21.080 --> 02:17:22.080
Lower it.

02:17:22.080 --> 02:17:23.860
Here's why.

02:17:23.860 --> 02:17:26.349
Way easier to get over low bars than it is
high bars.

02:17:26.349 --> 02:17:27.349
Try it.

02:17:27.349 --> 02:17:29.139
Put a bar six feet high, try to jump over
it.

02:17:29.139 --> 02:17:30.340
Put a bar six inches off the ground.

02:17:30.340 --> 02:17:33.420
You can get over it 20 times in a minute.

02:17:33.420 --> 02:17:34.650
It's easy.

02:17:34.650 --> 02:17:39.059
Lower the bar, ladies and gentlemen.

02:17:39.059 --> 02:17:43.599
That means that I get up every day, and I
don't have high expectations of anything,

02:17:43.599 --> 02:17:46.080
not even with humanity.

02:17:46.080 --> 02:17:50.920
I'm not a religious person, but I do have
a personal prayer, and it's this simple, every

02:17:50.920 --> 02:17:55.149
day: "Today will not be perfect."

02:17:55.149 --> 02:17:59.110
And many days in a row, Tim, I've nailed that.

02:17:59.110 --> 02:18:05.680
Every single day of my life I have never — I'm
on even a longer streak than Cal Ripken Jr.

02:18:05.680 --> 02:18:06.680
with that.

02:18:06.680 --> 02:18:11.149
What tortures people, and some of you are
listening, are the people who have the bar

02:18:11.149 --> 02:18:12.340
higher than that.

02:18:12.340 --> 02:18:15.170
"Did you see this email this guy sent me?

02:18:15.170 --> 02:18:16.740
That son of a bitch."

02:18:16.740 --> 02:18:19.679
"Oh, you thought today was going to be perfect.

02:18:19.679 --> 02:18:20.679
Did you see that guy?

02:18:20.679 --> 02:18:22.590
That guy cut me off on the freeway."

02:18:22.590 --> 02:18:27.200
"Oh, that guy cut me off here in line.

02:18:27.200 --> 02:18:30.900
I was waiting in line, and they cut me off
getting a ticket for the Black Panther movie,

02:18:30.900 --> 02:18:33.559
and now I can't get in," or whatever.

02:18:33.559 --> 02:18:35.809
"Oh, somebody said something about my haircut."

02:18:35.809 --> 02:18:37.929
"Somebody said something about that."

02:18:37.929 --> 02:18:42.599
So you thought that the world was going to
be perfect and that everybody was going to

02:18:42.599 --> 02:18:45.130
wake up and that they were going to make your
life better.

02:18:45.130 --> 02:18:49.349
You know who woke up on the planet today with
eight billion people and said, "I'm going

02:18:49.349 --> 02:18:50.979
to make Todd's life better today?"

02:18:50.979 --> 02:18:54.189
There was only one human being — one — and
that was me.

02:18:54.189 --> 02:18:58.139
No other human being, not even my wife, that
was their mission today.

02:18:58.139 --> 02:18:59.139
That was me.

02:18:59.139 --> 02:19:05.430
So if you're expecting others to make you
happy and make it accommodating for you, you're

02:19:05.430 --> 02:19:07.059
going to get crushed.

02:19:07.059 --> 02:19:08.420
Here's what I would say.

02:19:08.420 --> 02:19:09.800
Lower the bar.

02:19:09.800 --> 02:19:15.769
Go to your job interview, and before you walk
in, give it your damnedest, but assume you're

02:19:15.769 --> 02:19:18.840
not getting the job.

02:19:18.840 --> 02:19:22.090
Give it your damnedest, but assume you're
not getting it.

02:19:22.090 --> 02:19:27.439
So when they don't phone you back, and when
you don't get the job, you are okay with it,

02:19:27.439 --> 02:19:32.300
because if anything, you actually take it
as a pride that you actually called the shot.

02:19:32.300 --> 02:19:33.800
"I knew it, I knew it.

02:19:33.800 --> 02:19:34.800
I went there.

02:19:34.800 --> 02:19:35.800
They didn't give it to me.

02:19:35.800 --> 02:19:36.800
Dumb shit, but I knew it."

02:19:36.800 --> 02:19:41.939
And pretty soon you don't get hired for 10,
20 jobs in a row, and you're on a hot streak

02:19:41.939 --> 02:19:42.939
now.

02:19:42.939 --> 02:19:46.019
You're actually feeling pretty good that you
called it 20 times in a row, you're not getting

02:19:46.019 --> 02:19:47.019
the job.

02:19:47.019 --> 02:19:48.229
And then guess what happens?

02:19:48.229 --> 02:19:53.200
You go to the 21st interview, and you give
it your best, and you walk away and they go,

02:19:53.200 --> 02:19:54.200
"Todd, where are you going?"

02:19:54.200 --> 02:19:57.080
"I'm out of here because I know you're not
going to hire me."

02:19:57.080 --> 02:20:01.580
And they go, "No, we actually want to offer
you a job."

02:20:01.580 --> 02:20:06.490
And here's why that's a awesome day, because
well, it's sort of quasi-frustrated.

02:20:06.490 --> 02:20:09.730
First off, they break my streak of 20 noes
in a row.

02:20:09.730 --> 02:20:13.310
So I'm a little frustrated that my streak
went away, because I'm like, "God damn it,

02:20:13.310 --> 02:20:14.399
they broke my streak."

02:20:14.399 --> 02:20:17.990
But by breaking my streak, I got the job.

02:20:17.990 --> 02:20:20.340
I got the job.

02:20:20.340 --> 02:20:21.819
It's a good day.

02:20:21.819 --> 02:20:25.971
Here's what most people do, Tim, and my family
members do the same thing.

02:20:25.971 --> 02:20:27.930
And I can't help if it's a personality.

02:20:27.930 --> 02:20:31.220
They actually think they're going to get every
job they go and apply for.

02:20:31.220 --> 02:20:34.570
And when they don't, they get crushed.

02:20:34.570 --> 02:20:42.560
It literally crushes their soul for two or
three days, and they're a shell of a person.

02:20:42.560 --> 02:20:43.560
Why?

02:20:43.560 --> 02:20:45.610
Because they put the bar high.

02:20:45.610 --> 02:20:48.050
The bar was that we were going to get the
job.

02:20:48.050 --> 02:20:54.069
No, put the bar where it says, "I'm not getting
the job," and you will never be disappointed.

02:20:54.069 --> 02:20:59.540
Assume today's not going to be perfect and
assume human beings are flawed, and you will

02:20:59.540 --> 02:21:02.189
have a pretty good life.

02:21:02.189 --> 02:21:03.660
Because guess what happens?

02:21:03.660 --> 02:21:09.200
Some days, nobody cuts you off, nobody cuts
in line, and nobody writes you a bad email.

02:21:09.200 --> 02:21:11.220
And it's a pretty good day.

02:21:11.220 --> 02:21:16.971
It's a pretty good day because discomfort
didn't come.

02:21:16.971 --> 02:21:21.230
Not that you won the lottery, not that your
team won the championship.

02:21:21.230 --> 02:21:23.399
You just didn't have a lot of aggravation.

02:21:23.399 --> 02:21:24.399
You didn't get sued today.

02:21:24.399 --> 02:21:25.399
Cool.

02:21:25.399 --> 02:21:26.399
It's a good day.

02:21:26.399 --> 02:21:27.399
I don't know.

02:21:27.399 --> 02:21:28.399
I psych myself out.

02:21:28.399 --> 02:21:35.240
I psych myself out for little success, so
when any of it comes, it's cool.

02:21:35.240 --> 02:21:37.780
We talked about it with selling the toys at
60,000.

02:21:37.780 --> 02:21:41.790
There's either way to get to 60,000 sales,
and it be a negative, or it can be a positive.

02:21:41.790 --> 02:21:44.229
It depends on how you psych yourself out.

02:21:44.229 --> 02:21:47.630
I am a horrible golfer, horrible golfer.

02:21:47.630 --> 02:21:49.370
I don't do it much.

02:21:49.370 --> 02:21:53.410
Also, I've had people say, "Todd, you're the
worst golfer who doesn't care."

02:21:53.410 --> 02:21:54.410
Here's why I don't care.

02:21:54.410 --> 02:21:56.580
I don't golf, and so I don't golf.

02:21:56.580 --> 02:22:02.120
I don't assume I'm going to get better by
not golfing every three years, any more than

02:22:02.120 --> 02:22:06.760
my Mandarin and my Spanish is going to get
better by me not practicing either one of

02:22:06.760 --> 02:22:08.240
those languages either.

02:22:08.240 --> 02:22:09.939
You don't get better by not doing it.

02:22:09.939 --> 02:22:14.221
So when I go golf, the odd time I'm in Phoenix,
I'm horrible.

02:22:14.221 --> 02:22:19.130
But every now and then, I get lucky, and all
of a sudden, on a par five, I get a six.

02:22:19.130 --> 02:22:23.400
I am dancing naked when I get a six on a par
five.

02:22:23.400 --> 02:22:24.400
But it's interesting.

02:22:24.400 --> 02:22:27.830
I'm with another guy who must have had a different
expectation.

02:22:27.830 --> 02:22:29.480
His bar was different than mine.

02:22:29.480 --> 02:22:31.979
He hits it, he hits it, he gets a six.

02:22:31.979 --> 02:22:37.100
And he is so mad, he actually breaks his club
that costs more than my entire rental.

02:22:37.100 --> 02:22:41.390
And he breaks his club, and I'm sitting there
going, "Wow, he got a six.

02:22:41.390 --> 02:22:42.400
I got a six.

02:22:42.400 --> 02:22:43.729
It's the same number.

02:22:43.729 --> 02:22:48.240
I'm dancing naked, and he's cursing up a storm.

02:22:48.240 --> 02:22:50.811
He must have had a different expectation than
me."

02:22:50.811 --> 02:22:51.811
So that's it.

02:22:51.811 --> 02:22:53.540
I just keep my expectations low.

02:22:53.540 --> 02:22:54.940
It gets you through there.

02:22:54.940 --> 02:22:59.220
Number two, they're always going to say no
to you, always going to say no to you.

02:22:59.220 --> 02:23:05.080
There has never been a story written in which
the person who had the new idea, the person

02:23:05.080 --> 02:23:13.470
in the lead said, "Oh, I will now slow down
or get out of the way so you can pass me."

02:23:13.470 --> 02:23:15.880
Never ever will happen.

02:23:15.880 --> 02:23:22.380
So if you're going to do something that's
going to aggravate the system status quo,

02:23:22.380 --> 02:23:27.061
there's never been anybody in history that's
ever changed anything on any level and was

02:23:27.061 --> 02:23:29.431
liked by everybody.

02:23:29.431 --> 02:23:30.590
Get over it.

02:23:30.590 --> 02:23:33.390
Get over people liking you.

02:23:33.390 --> 02:23:35.609
Just figure out what your goal is.

02:23:35.609 --> 02:23:37.540
I'll give you my personality.

02:23:37.540 --> 02:23:38.670
I take my wife.

02:23:38.670 --> 02:23:41.780
We're going to go to the bar, and we're going
to go dance.

02:23:41.780 --> 02:23:42.780
Okay.

02:23:42.780 --> 02:23:49.279
Nine o'clock comes around, the band starts
playing, nobody wants to be the first one

02:23:49.279 --> 02:23:50.279
on the dance floor.

02:23:50.279 --> 02:23:52.920
Let me also say, if you've never been the
first one on a dance floor, you're probably

02:23:52.920 --> 02:23:55.840
not built to be an entrepreneur.

02:23:55.840 --> 02:24:00.130
Let me just put that there, because that means
you give a shit about the rest of the people

02:24:00.130 --> 02:24:01.470
in that room.

02:24:01.470 --> 02:24:02.950
I don't.

02:24:02.950 --> 02:24:03.950
And here's why.

02:24:03.950 --> 02:24:05.600
First off, I don't even know them.

02:24:05.600 --> 02:24:11.780
Why would I give any weight, why would I give
any power to a stranger in my life?

02:24:11.780 --> 02:24:13.300
Here's what my goal is.

02:24:13.300 --> 02:24:19.200
My goal was to take my lovely wife out for
an evening of dancing.

02:24:19.200 --> 02:24:21.180
There's a dance floor, and there's music.

02:24:21.180 --> 02:24:22.450
I'm going to dance.

02:24:22.450 --> 02:24:27.010
If every other human being in that room doesn't
want to dance, I don't care.

02:24:27.010 --> 02:24:29.670
If they all want to dance, I don't care.

02:24:29.670 --> 02:24:32.200
None of them are of importance to me.

02:24:32.200 --> 02:24:37.520
What's important is that I want to dance with
my wife tonight, and I'm doing it.

02:24:37.520 --> 02:24:38.950
I'm doing it.

02:24:38.950 --> 02:24:41.710
Do I care whether they think I'm a good dancer?

02:24:41.710 --> 02:24:42.710
Nope.

02:24:42.710 --> 02:24:45.360
Do I care if they think I look like a goof?

02:24:45.360 --> 02:24:46.360
Nope.

02:24:46.360 --> 02:24:47.360
Nope.

02:24:47.360 --> 02:24:50.800
Not one second of power do I give to that
person.

02:24:50.800 --> 02:24:55.319
If you are the person that's going to wait
till the dance floor is full so that then

02:24:55.319 --> 02:24:59.520
you can get on and nobody will see you so
you can hide in the middle of the pack, I'm

02:24:59.520 --> 02:25:02.940
just telling you, you're probably not built
to be an entrepreneur.

02:25:02.940 --> 02:25:13.550
You have to just be at times, literally, don't
care.

02:25:13.550 --> 02:25:16.109
It's not a trait that I want to encourage.

02:25:16.109 --> 02:25:18.050
I don't want my kids to be like me.

02:25:18.050 --> 02:25:22.520
But you have to have it, because if you're
going to make change, if you're going to go

02:25:22.520 --> 02:25:27.810
up against competition, they're going to,
every day, try and tell you you can't and

02:25:27.810 --> 02:25:29.170
try and embarrass you somehow.

02:25:29.170 --> 02:25:36.520
And you have to just push it and categorize
it to a side and say, "I don't care.

02:25:36.520 --> 02:25:38.550
This was my goal, and I'm going to do it anyways."

02:25:38.550 --> 02:25:42.720
Tim Ferriss: Now, why would you not want your
kids to have that trait?

02:25:42.720 --> 02:25:43.720
What's the downside?

02:25:43.720 --> 02:25:44.720
Or how do you think about that?

02:25:44.720 --> 02:25:46.899
Todd McFarlane: Because I can be a cold fish
at times.

02:25:46.899 --> 02:25:48.760
When you —
Tim Ferriss: Cold fish.

02:25:48.760 --> 02:25:49.760
Todd McFarlane: Right.

02:25:49.760 --> 02:25:51.870
Tim Ferriss: Meaning you're like too Spock-like,
or you come off as unemotional?

02:25:51.870 --> 02:25:53.189
Todd McFarlane: No, no.

02:25:53.189 --> 02:25:54.430
I told you.

02:25:54.430 --> 02:25:58.570
Let's say you put me out tomorrow, and I could
cut the heads of my enemy though.

02:25:58.570 --> 02:25:59.830
Let's go back to Braveheart.

02:25:59.830 --> 02:26:02.880
And I could cut the heads of my enemy.

02:26:02.880 --> 02:26:06.380
So I got my mortal enemy in front of me, and
I cut his head off.

02:26:06.380 --> 02:26:10.920
Do you know how much time I'm going to give
to feeling guilty that I did it?

02:26:10.920 --> 02:26:12.330
Zero seconds.

02:26:12.330 --> 02:26:13.330
You know why, Tim?

02:26:13.330 --> 02:26:15.240
Because I'm in the middle of a battle.

02:26:15.240 --> 02:26:17.050
Remember, I keep thinking I'm at war.

02:26:17.050 --> 02:26:21.080
And if you watch something like Braveheart,
I'm not the only one, and they're not the

02:26:21.080 --> 02:26:22.340
only one on the battlefield.

02:26:22.340 --> 02:26:28.750
And if I sit there and give time to feel sorry
for what I did to that man and think about

02:26:28.750 --> 02:26:33.730
his family and his children, somebody coming
up behind me is going to cut my head off,

02:26:33.730 --> 02:26:36.060
and I will be dead six seconds later.

02:26:36.060 --> 02:26:43.859
So I must learn to cut and turn and forget
about that dead victim, because somebody's

02:26:43.859 --> 02:26:51.729
out to kill me five seconds later, and I either
survive, or at least attempt to survive, or

02:26:51.729 --> 02:26:54.880
I lament and get my head cut off in a second.

02:26:54.880 --> 02:26:58.880
Here's how this works, Tim, here's how this
works in practical, modern terms for a guy

02:26:58.880 --> 02:26:59.880
like Todd.

02:26:59.880 --> 02:27:02.130
And I don't necessarily want anybody to do
it.

02:27:02.130 --> 02:27:04.580
I'm just telling you who I am.

02:27:04.580 --> 02:27:13.689
I usually phone lawyers that I don't like,
and I'm in a fight with on Friday about 4:30.

02:27:13.689 --> 02:27:17.220
This is actually a very good time, and here's
why it's a good time.

02:27:17.220 --> 02:27:20.660
Because I can get on the phone with them,
and they can argue.

02:27:20.660 --> 02:27:25.180
And then you start raising voices, and you
start sounding like Mussolini on the balcony.

02:27:25.180 --> 02:27:29.340
And you start going, and then you usually
end those conversations like, "Oh, yeah?

02:27:29.340 --> 02:27:30.340
Well, fuck you."

02:27:30.340 --> 02:27:31.340
"Yeah, well, fuck you."

02:27:31.340 --> 02:27:32.340
"Okay, I'll get on the phone with you Monday.

02:27:32.340 --> 02:27:33.760
We're going to settle this bullshit."

02:27:33.760 --> 02:27:34.760
And you go "Click."

02:27:34.760 --> 02:27:37.420
Now, here's what I've told my wife, because
she's heard me do this before.

02:27:37.420 --> 02:27:38.800
She goes, "Todd."

02:27:38.800 --> 02:27:39.800
She's heard me before.

02:27:39.800 --> 02:27:41.800
"Todd, you're going to have a heart attack."

02:27:41.800 --> 02:27:42.800
"No, I'm not."

02:27:42.800 --> 02:27:44.370
"Yes, you're going to have a heart attack."

02:27:44.370 --> 02:27:45.370
"No, I'm not.

02:27:45.370 --> 02:27:46.370
And here's why."

02:27:46.370 --> 02:27:47.370
My wife's name is Wanda.

02:27:47.370 --> 02:27:48.370
"No, Wanda, I'm not going to have a heart
attack."

02:27:48.370 --> 02:27:52.600
And she doesn't believe this, until this one
scary moment, that I go, "No."

02:27:52.600 --> 02:27:53.600
And she goes, "Why?"

02:27:53.600 --> 02:27:55.479
I go, "Because I don't care."

02:27:55.479 --> 02:28:00.420
The moment I hang up that phone, click, I
don't even care about that guy.

02:28:00.420 --> 02:28:02.560
That lawyer I just yelled at?

02:28:02.560 --> 02:28:03.560
Click.

02:28:03.560 --> 02:28:05.409
He's like, "Dude.

02:28:05.409 --> 02:28:06.490
The weekend's coming.

02:28:06.490 --> 02:28:07.770
I'll worry about him on Monday.

02:28:07.770 --> 02:28:10.590
I'll worry about him on Monday."

02:28:10.590 --> 02:28:15.180
But because I can do that, here's what happens
to the lawyer.

02:28:15.180 --> 02:28:16.180
He gets off the phone.

02:28:16.180 --> 02:28:19.020
He goes to all of his people in the office,
he goes, "I just got off the phone with this

02:28:19.020 --> 02:28:20.633
son of a bitch, and here's what he said."

02:28:20.633 --> 02:28:22.750
And he's going to chew on it, and he's going
to chew on it.

02:28:22.750 --> 02:28:24.979
Then he's going to go home, and his wife's
going to say, "How was your day?"

02:28:24.979 --> 02:28:26.370
"Oh, fine, except for at the end, this guy..."

02:28:26.370 --> 02:28:27.891
And he's going to chew on it.

02:28:27.891 --> 02:28:30.400
And then they're going to go out, and they're
going to have a barbecue with their friends.

02:28:30.400 --> 02:28:31.400
They're going to go, "How are things going?"

02:28:31.400 --> 02:28:32.561
"Oh, fine, but I've got this one thing."

02:28:32.561 --> 02:28:35.090
And he's going to chew on it and chew on it.

02:28:35.090 --> 02:28:37.920
And he's going to sit there and think about
me the whole weekend.

02:28:37.920 --> 02:28:40.600
By Monday, he's exhausted.

02:28:40.600 --> 02:28:44.600
And you know how much time I gave him during
that same amount of time?

02:28:44.600 --> 02:28:48.400
Not one second of my emotions.

02:28:48.400 --> 02:28:49.400
Why?

02:28:49.400 --> 02:28:51.939
Because on Monday, click, I turn it back on.

02:28:51.939 --> 02:28:57.620
Guess, between the two of us, who's emotionally
more stronger to continue the conversation?

02:28:57.620 --> 02:29:01.560
The guy who was in the gym the whole time,
bench pressing that weight?

02:29:01.560 --> 02:29:04.890
Or the guy who put it down and let his muscle
sort of relax so I can go back to the gym

02:29:04.890 --> 02:29:06.080
a couple days later?

02:29:06.080 --> 02:29:07.080
I don't care.

02:29:07.080 --> 02:29:08.819
And here's how I know that's true.

02:29:08.819 --> 02:29:10.120
I know it's true.

02:29:10.120 --> 02:29:13.590
You can believe it or not, but I proved it
to my wife one day.

02:29:13.590 --> 02:29:18.990
She took out a life insurance policy or something,
and the doctors have to come to the house,

02:29:18.990 --> 02:29:22.620
and they have to do an exam on you to make
sure that you're not dying or whatever.

02:29:22.620 --> 02:29:25.300
They come, and they got to do an examination
on me and my wife.

02:29:25.300 --> 02:29:27.910
My wife is top fit.

02:29:27.910 --> 02:29:28.910
She's awesome.

02:29:28.910 --> 02:29:29.910
She is a hiker, runner.

02:29:29.910 --> 02:29:33.260
She's like a model.

02:29:33.260 --> 02:29:34.390
She's awesome.

02:29:34.390 --> 02:29:37.880
Anyways, they came, and I was in the middle
of one of these Mussolini moments.

02:29:37.880 --> 02:29:41.340
The doctors came, and they go, "Todd, we've
got to examine you."

02:29:41.340 --> 02:29:43.440
And I go, "Tell them to do Wanda first.

02:29:43.440 --> 02:29:46.130
I'm on the phone, I'm on the phone."

02:29:46.130 --> 02:29:48.050
They go and do Wanda.

02:29:48.050 --> 02:29:50.310
And then, they do my wife, whatever they had
to do.

02:29:50.310 --> 02:29:53.300
And then, they come upstairs.

02:29:53.300 --> 02:29:54.300
She knows.

02:29:54.300 --> 02:29:55.670
She can still hear me yelling.

02:29:55.670 --> 02:29:59.569
She comes up with the doctors, there's a couple
of them, and then they go, "Okay, Todd, it's

02:29:59.569 --> 02:30:00.569
you."

02:30:00.569 --> 02:30:01.569
And I go, "Yeah, oh, yeah, okay.

02:30:01.569 --> 02:30:02.569
And fuck you."

02:30:02.569 --> 02:30:03.569
Click.

02:30:03.569 --> 02:30:04.590
I end my conversation.

02:30:04.590 --> 02:30:05.590
I go, "What are we doing?

02:30:05.590 --> 02:30:07.410
Oh, we've got to do this examination.

02:30:07.410 --> 02:30:08.970
Oh, yeah, okay.

02:30:08.970 --> 02:30:09.970
Cool."

02:30:09.970 --> 02:30:10.970
My wife was waiting for it.

02:30:10.970 --> 02:30:14.609
They do the blood pressure, and they do all
the testing.

02:30:14.609 --> 02:30:18.569
And they go, "Oh, yours is the same as your
wife's."

02:30:18.569 --> 02:30:23.061
I literally, and it horrified my wife, let
me just tell you it horrified her, because

02:30:23.061 --> 02:30:24.760
she goes, "I am married."

02:30:24.760 --> 02:30:27.840
A, she was happy that I'm not going to die
of a heart attack, because I literally can

02:30:27.840 --> 02:30:28.840
turn the switch off.

02:30:28.840 --> 02:30:33.399
Two, she was horrified she's married to a
monster, that he literally doesn't care about

02:30:33.399 --> 02:30:36.050
another human being and can just turn it off.

02:30:36.050 --> 02:30:41.400
I turn it off, Tim, not out of pride, not
because I think that's the way to go, not

02:30:41.400 --> 02:30:42.790
because I think that's it.

02:30:42.790 --> 02:30:48.979
It's because we all, if you're in war, and
I think I'm in war, if you're at war, you

02:30:48.979 --> 02:30:54.140
have to find out ways of surviving that work
for you.

02:30:54.140 --> 02:31:02.340
This is how I've learned to survive, to not
let any — I've been sued, I've been screamed

02:31:02.340 --> 02:31:03.340
at.

02:31:03.340 --> 02:31:04.340
I've had people threatened.

02:31:04.340 --> 02:31:05.340
The other day, what are you talking about?

02:31:05.340 --> 02:31:09.250
One of the big retailers threatened to take
all my toys off their shelf.

02:31:09.250 --> 02:31:13.630
You just accept that this is just part of
the world that you live in.

02:31:13.630 --> 02:31:19.270
If at any time, Tim, I ever get aggravated
— you know who I'm the most aggravated with?

02:31:19.270 --> 02:31:25.370
Me, because at the end, no matter what's happening,
I have to ask the same question.

02:31:25.370 --> 02:31:27.270
"Who put me in this position?"

02:31:27.270 --> 02:31:28.350
"It's uncomfortable.

02:31:28.350 --> 02:31:29.350
I don't want be here.

02:31:29.350 --> 02:31:31.640
Why am I arguing with lawyers today?

02:31:31.640 --> 02:31:33.220
Oh, that was you, Todd.

02:31:33.220 --> 02:31:35.000
You started that company.

02:31:35.000 --> 02:31:36.320
You kept it going.

02:31:36.320 --> 02:31:37.320
You kept doing it."

02:31:37.320 --> 02:31:39.500
I don't get to pass the buck.

02:31:39.500 --> 02:31:44.750
I have to sit there and say, "The guy who
basically caused all this is the guy I shave

02:31:44.750 --> 02:31:45.750
with every day."

02:31:45.750 --> 02:31:46.990
And here's the problem with that.

02:31:46.990 --> 02:31:48.570
I actually like that dude.

02:31:48.570 --> 02:31:51.970
And even if I don't, I've got to live with
him for the rest of my natural life.

02:31:51.970 --> 02:31:53.260
So you know what?

02:31:53.260 --> 02:31:54.260
I'm stuck.

02:31:54.260 --> 02:31:55.260
I'm stuck with it.

02:31:55.260 --> 02:31:57.340
I just figure out ways that work for me.

02:31:57.340 --> 02:32:01.840
I don't want you or any listener to replicate
it.

02:32:01.840 --> 02:32:03.500
I would say, "No."

02:32:03.500 --> 02:32:08.240
What I do do when I go to business classes,
which is super awesome, when I go talk to

02:32:08.240 --> 02:32:11.930
business classes at university, first thing
I say is, "I'm not going to tell you how to

02:32:11.930 --> 02:32:12.930
make money."

02:32:12.930 --> 02:32:17.130
85 percent of them throw their pencil down
in disgust, because they thought I was going

02:32:17.130 --> 02:32:19.280
to give them the 10 lessons in how to make
money.

02:32:19.280 --> 02:32:24.761
I go, "Here's what we're going to talk about
for an hour: how to keep your humanity, how

02:32:24.761 --> 02:32:28.720
to actually be a reasonable human being.

02:32:28.720 --> 02:32:30.649
So here's what I'm going to tell you.

02:32:30.649 --> 02:32:37.680
I would rather you become a garbage collector
and go become a good community person and

02:32:37.680 --> 02:32:46.150
go coach Little League for free than to be
the VP of a bank and be divorced four times

02:32:46.150 --> 02:32:48.720
and estranged from your family and nobody
wants to hang out with you."

02:32:48.720 --> 02:32:51.990
We somehow equate, in North America, money
to success.

02:32:51.990 --> 02:32:56.400
And to me, my definition of success is different.

02:32:56.400 --> 02:33:04.050
I just think that there's a way to just not
care, even if you do have money and prestige

02:33:04.050 --> 02:33:05.050
and all of it.

02:33:05.050 --> 02:33:06.399
To me, it's all silly.

02:33:06.399 --> 02:33:08.170
It's all complicated our lives.

02:33:08.170 --> 02:33:09.899
My wife will tell you the same.

02:33:09.899 --> 02:33:12.490
It was easier when life was simpler.

02:33:12.490 --> 02:33:17.680
But you guys are built, the guys that are
doing their MBAs in these classes, they're

02:33:17.680 --> 02:33:19.170
just little baby sharks.

02:33:19.170 --> 02:33:24.580
When I'm giving that conversation, I know
that they want to emulate the big sharks,

02:33:24.580 --> 02:33:27.740
so I'm only talking to four or five of them
that are out there.

02:33:27.740 --> 02:33:33.890
And at the end of it, after we go with it,
I sometimes do this last little question there.

02:33:33.890 --> 02:33:36.891
I go, "I'm just going to leave you with one
thing, because I know about making money.

02:33:36.891 --> 02:33:37.930
You've got to make money.

02:33:37.930 --> 02:33:39.960
And the reason I don't want to tell you how
to make money, because if I tell you how to

02:33:39.960 --> 02:33:45.359
make a million bucks, you, in your brain and
your intellect and your personal experiences,

02:33:45.359 --> 02:33:47.439
might have figured out how to make a hundred
million.

02:33:47.439 --> 02:33:48.950
Why would I try to tell you how to make a
million?

02:33:48.950 --> 02:33:50.430
You might have made a hundred million.

02:33:50.430 --> 02:33:52.020
You may be a thousand times smarter than me.

02:33:52.020 --> 02:33:54.790
I don't want to limit you.

02:33:54.790 --> 02:34:00.200
But let me just leave — once we talked all
of this to see if we can't leave you with

02:34:00.200 --> 02:34:01.720
one thought here.

02:34:01.720 --> 02:34:07.960
All of you just woke up tomorrow, and you're
not feeling very good, so you go to the doctor.

02:34:07.960 --> 02:34:11.660
Doctor takes you in the back room, and he
does a bunch of blood tests and says, 'Hey,

02:34:11.660 --> 02:34:12.660
I'm going to run the blood test.'

02:34:12.660 --> 02:34:14.221
And you go sit out in the lobby.

02:34:14.221 --> 02:34:15.221
'I'll get back to you.'

02:34:15.221 --> 02:34:17.760
"So you go out in the lobby, and you look
on your cell phone for a while.

02:34:17.760 --> 02:34:20.000
25 minutes later the doctor comes back.

02:34:20.000 --> 02:34:21.000
He doesn't look very good.

02:34:21.000 --> 02:34:23.729
He's got a serious look.

02:34:23.729 --> 02:34:25.100
And he says, 'Hey, I need you to come back
to my office.'

02:34:25.100 --> 02:34:26.649
'Yeah, sure, doc.'

02:34:26.649 --> 02:34:27.950
"You go sit down.

02:34:27.950 --> 02:34:33.439
They go, 'We ran all the tests, and we reran
them, and you have inoperable cancer.

02:34:33.439 --> 02:34:36.250
There is nothing modern medicine can do.

02:34:36.250 --> 02:34:38.100
You will be dead in three weeks.

02:34:38.100 --> 02:34:39.680
You're dead.

02:34:39.680 --> 02:34:40.939
There's no hope.'

02:34:40.939 --> 02:34:47.060
“Now, I'm going to assume that everybody
listening to my voice right now has at least

02:34:47.060 --> 02:34:50.560
one person they care about, at least one person.

02:34:50.560 --> 02:34:54.900
So think about the person that you care about
the most.

02:34:54.900 --> 02:34:59.320
That person, I assume if you care for them
that much, they care about you that much.

02:34:59.320 --> 02:35:04.180
And in three weeks that person is going to
be left without you.

02:35:04.180 --> 02:35:08.050
It will be trauma to their life.

02:35:08.050 --> 02:35:10.689
“So here's the question.

02:35:10.689 --> 02:35:17.609
If you could only pick one human being to
help that person you care about to transition

02:35:17.609 --> 02:35:24.020
after you're gone, to soft-land them as much
as possible, to be by their side, to do anything

02:35:24.020 --> 02:35:29.750
that will be helpful in their grieving period,
who is that individual?

02:35:29.750 --> 02:35:31.689
I want you to pick just one individual.

02:35:31.689 --> 02:35:35.100
I'll give you 15 seconds to think of it.”

02:35:35.100 --> 02:35:39.560
And then I stand there, Tim, in silence.

02:35:39.560 --> 02:35:43.810
And then I go, "My last question before I
leave.

02:35:43.810 --> 02:35:50.811
Did you pick the richest person you know,
or did you pick the best person?

02:35:50.811 --> 02:35:54.510
Why don't you want to be the second one?

02:35:54.510 --> 02:35:59.680
Why don't you want to be the best person instead
of the richest?

02:35:59.680 --> 02:36:06.529
Because when it mattered, and we just proved
it, you didn't pick the rich person.

02:36:06.529 --> 02:36:12.430
Be the second person, and you can still make
money and be that second person.

02:36:12.430 --> 02:36:16.370
Don't worry about it, but don't make the money
the driver.

02:36:16.370 --> 02:36:17.370
Don't do it.

02:36:17.370 --> 02:36:20.010
But I'm only talking to three of you in this
class.

02:36:20.010 --> 02:36:22.930
The rest of you've already sold your soul."

02:36:22.930 --> 02:36:29.050
Tim Ferriss: Well, Todd, I think that is the
best place to maybe bring this conversation

02:36:29.050 --> 02:36:34.660
home and leave people with that to chew on,
without adding too much more.

02:36:34.660 --> 02:36:37.720
I have to say we could easily do a round three
at some point.

02:36:37.720 --> 02:36:45.700
But I do think that is an excellent place
to stop and begin to at least land the plane.

02:36:45.700 --> 02:36:51.890
Is there anything else that you would like
to add, call people's attention to, or anything

02:36:51.890 --> 02:36:52.890
else?

02:36:52.890 --> 02:36:59.779
Any closing comments before we end this conversation,
at least for now?

02:36:59.779 --> 02:37:00.860
Todd McFarlane: Yeah.

02:37:00.860 --> 02:37:08.229
The only thing I'd like to do is encourage
people who have a little bit of doubt.

02:37:08.229 --> 02:37:10.580
If you got a lot of bit of doubt, it might
not work.

02:37:10.580 --> 02:37:14.830
A little bit of doubt, I think we can get
past that hurdle.

02:37:14.830 --> 02:37:21.229
I don't consider myself to be smart or intelligent
or do anything better than anybody else.

02:37:21.229 --> 02:37:27.010
I was able, through just a little bit of perseverance,
to get there.

02:37:27.010 --> 02:37:33.650
I believe that if I can do it, and I was dumb,
I got nothing but Ds in school and stuff like

02:37:33.650 --> 02:37:34.650
that.

02:37:34.650 --> 02:37:39.010
If I can do it, I just think there are so
many people up there.

02:37:39.010 --> 02:37:42.180
What I do at times, it's frustrating to me.

02:37:42.180 --> 02:37:44.760
I'm up on stage sometimes at comic conventions.

02:37:44.760 --> 02:37:48.850
I don't even talk about comic books, but I
go, "I'm going to talk to you as your dad,

02:37:48.850 --> 02:37:49.850
as your uncle.

02:37:49.850 --> 02:37:52.390
And I'm going to tell you why I think you
should try this."

02:37:52.390 --> 02:37:56.229
I think we've spoken about some of those on
your show in these two parts.

02:37:56.229 --> 02:37:59.960
"Just try it, and if it doesn't work, it's
like cutting your hair.

02:37:59.960 --> 02:38:00.960
It'll grow back.

02:38:00.960 --> 02:38:02.600
You can always go back to the system wherever
you're at.

02:38:02.600 --> 02:38:03.600
You can always go back.

02:38:03.600 --> 02:38:09.420
There's no harm, there's no dishonesty in
trying and failing.

02:38:09.420 --> 02:38:10.420
That's okay.

02:38:10.420 --> 02:38:14.359
The person who never tries, I think, has more
regret."

02:38:14.359 --> 02:38:15.359
I talk to them.

02:38:15.359 --> 02:38:20.010
I stand on one side of the stage and I go,
"Come on, man.

02:38:20.010 --> 02:38:21.010
Just try.

02:38:21.010 --> 02:38:22.010
Try.

02:38:22.010 --> 02:38:23.010
Try it once."

02:38:23.010 --> 02:38:26.660
I think everybody, in all honesty, Tim, should
try to be an entrepreneur once in their life.

02:38:26.660 --> 02:38:31.760
And then I walk to the other stage, and I
go, "Now I'm going to talk to you as Todd

02:38:31.760 --> 02:38:32.760
the CEO.

02:38:32.760 --> 02:38:33.760
And here's what I'm going to tell you as Todd
the CEO.

02:38:33.760 --> 02:38:34.760
I hope you don't do it.

02:38:34.760 --> 02:38:38.990
I hope you sow those doubts, and you never
do it.

02:38:38.990 --> 02:38:39.990
And you know why?

02:38:39.990 --> 02:38:43.229
Because that means I will never have to compete
with you.

02:38:43.229 --> 02:38:49.630
And you may be smarter and better and more
well-equipped, and I'll never have to play

02:38:49.630 --> 02:38:51.020
the game against you.

02:38:51.020 --> 02:38:53.950
So make my life easy, and don't compete."

02:38:53.950 --> 02:38:57.090
But I'm telling you, the dad side of me?

02:38:57.090 --> 02:38:58.359
Take me down.

02:38:58.359 --> 02:39:04.680
Man, because the 25-year-old Todd, I'm telling
you, I just know would've listened to me,

02:39:04.680 --> 02:39:10.880
the 60-year-old man and would've said, "Dude,
I will gut you."

02:39:10.880 --> 02:39:14.460
I would never have let an old man on that
stage go, "I hope you don't do it because

02:39:14.460 --> 02:39:16.390
you're not good enough."

02:39:16.390 --> 02:39:19.930
I would've gone and said, "Oh, yeah, I've
made it my personal mission."

02:39:19.930 --> 02:39:27.880
So even if I can just gin up a couple of people
to just go, "Come on, man.

02:39:27.880 --> 02:39:28.880
Come on, man."

02:39:28.880 --> 02:39:30.220
You can do it.

02:39:30.220 --> 02:39:38.460
But if you keep coming up with reasons why
you're not built for entrepreneurialship,

02:39:38.460 --> 02:39:44.510
you have to think a little bit delusional
at times, to the point where you think you're

02:39:44.510 --> 02:39:45.930
better than you are.

02:39:45.930 --> 02:39:47.050
That's okay.

02:39:47.050 --> 02:39:48.300
That's okay.

02:39:48.300 --> 02:39:51.870
Confidence is half the battle, and now we've
got to figure out whether you got the skill

02:39:51.870 --> 02:39:53.550
set, which is the other half.

02:39:53.550 --> 02:39:59.740
But some of the skill can come along the way,
because confidence and determination can take

02:39:59.740 --> 02:40:04.990
you pretty far until you learn those skills.

02:40:04.990 --> 02:40:06.630
Just go, go, go.

02:40:06.630 --> 02:40:08.830
That's all I've got.

02:40:08.830 --> 02:40:11.020
Tim Ferriss: Well done.

02:40:11.020 --> 02:40:18.720
I will say first that I can see why the people
from the NHL or wherever would've said, "Yeah,

02:40:18.720 --> 02:40:26.510
I think we'll just take the 500 grand and
not choose to have this guy jabbing us with

02:40:26.510 --> 02:40:29.380
a spear for the next 20 years.

02:40:29.380 --> 02:40:30.880
So let's just do that deal."

02:40:30.880 --> 02:40:33.520
I can definitely see that.

02:40:33.520 --> 02:40:40.200
I find you to be extremely impressive as a
creator, as an artist, fascinating as a CEO.

02:40:40.200 --> 02:40:41.990
And I got to say, you're a Hell of a good
teacher.

02:40:41.990 --> 02:40:43.140
You're a really good teacher.

02:40:43.140 --> 02:40:49.130
So thank you for taking the time to have these
conversations and to share your stories and

02:40:49.130 --> 02:40:50.980
your lessons and your beliefs.

02:40:50.980 --> 02:40:54.540
It's been a Hell of a lot of fun.

02:40:54.540 --> 02:40:59.680
To everybody listening, we will have links
to everything we discussed, all the music

02:40:59.680 --> 02:41:04.610
videos, anything else that my team and I can
possibly find as a reference to link to related

02:41:04.610 --> 02:41:10.050
to the show, we'll include in the show notes,
including the show notes at tim.blog/podcast.

02:41:10.050 --> 02:41:13.160
As usual, you can find Todd on all the social
networks.

02:41:13.160 --> 02:41:16.450
We'll link to those in the show notes as well
at tim.blog/podcast.

02:41:16.450 --> 02:41:21.479
Until next time, get out there.

02:41:21.479 --> 02:41:22.479
Give it a shot.

02:41:22.479 --> 02:41:23.479
Take your shot.

02:41:23.479 --> 02:41:25.770
You'll miss all of the shots you don't take,
so give it a go.

02:41:25.770 --> 02:41:43.630
You can always go back to the system 

02:41:43.630 --> 02:42:21.880
and 

02:42:21.880 --> 02:42:23.690
the status quo.

02:42:23.690 --> 02:42:52.319
And thanks for listening.

